{"timestamp":"2026-09-04T00:03:00.050Z","ordinal":24002,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"agent_message","id":"amsg_01a069b9-cf51-74c1-944c-1f2530008ef3","author":"/root/pr184_alias_semantic_review","recipient":"/root","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Message Type: FINAL_ANSWER\nTask name: /root\nSender: /root/pr184_alias_semantic_review\nPayload:\nP1 — restoring the failing module-only import scope API must not feed module-object imports back into generic resolution.\n\n`TestPythonBareImportFFIRespectsASTScopes` currently fails because `publish` stores imports only when `context.member == call.name`; `import frobnicate.extra` therefore no longer appears in `importModules(..., \"frobnicate\")`.\n\nDo not simply include `member == \"\"` in the provider-facing `imports` maps: with module temporal rebinding,\n\n```python\nimport one as c\nc = lambda value: value\nc(1)\n```\n\nthe later call is generic-allowed; a C `one.c` function named `c` would then be falsely raw-import-resolved from the stale module-object import.\n\nUse separate views:\n\n- `importModules`: retain `member == local` and `member == \"\"` for the existing scope/dotted-import contract.\n- `genericImportModules` / sets: only `member == local`; use this accessor at provider generic-resolution sites.\n- Keep `genericCallAllowed == false` for a standalone bare module-object call.\n\nAll revised alias, lambda, AuthService constructor, relative/global/nonlocal, incomplete-external, and golden targeted tests passed aside from that existing scope regression."}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a0690a-a093-7352-9a6c-ed6ea1d6d3c0","create_time":1788480180.049923}}}
