{"timestamp":"2026-09-04T00:58:07.035Z","ordinal":24993,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call","id":"ctc_064395e280df5569016a9a179d14f087d087a1b4ac27511c01","status":"completed","call_id":"call_PD6gKqSmBbre5OoR6xW6q02D","name":"exec","input":"const r = await tools.exec_command({\"cmd\":\"gh pr view 184 --repo entireio/entire-graph --json number,title,state,isDraft,headRefName,headRefOid,baseRefOid,mergeStateStatus,mergeable,additions,deletions,changedFiles,commits,statusCheckRollup,url,updatedAt\",\"workdir\":\"/Users/thomi/Projects/entire-graph/.entire/worktrees/pr184-review\",\"yield_time_ms\":30000,\"max_output_tokens\":30000});\ntext(r.output);\n","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a0690a-a093-7352-9a6c-ed6ea1d6d3c0","create_time":1788483475.687466}}}
{"timestamp":"2026-09-04T00:58:08.920Z","ordinal":24994,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a0616d-0c18-7443-82f2-b4c5bc498542","turn_id":"01a0690a-a093-7352-9a6c-ed6ea1d6d3c0","item":{"type":"CommandExecution","id":"exec-6ffffbc6-736c-4712-b42c-97aaa652da70","process_id":"11539","command":["/bin/zsh","-lc","gh pr view 184 --repo entireio/entire-graph --json number,title,state,isDraft,headRefName,headRefOid,baseRefOid,mergeStateStatus,mergeable,additions,deletions,changedFiles,commits,statusCheckRollup,url,updatedAt"],"cwd":"file:///Users/thomi/Projects/entire-graph/.entire/worktrees/pr184-review","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"unknown","cmd":"gh pr view 184 --repo entireio/entire-graph --json number,title,state,isDraft,headRefName,headRefOid,baseRefOid,mergeStateStatus,mergeable,additions,deletions,changedFiles,commits,statusCheckRollup,url,updatedAt"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","status":"completed","stdout":"{\"additions\":4495,\"baseRefOid\":\"9cdc0592352da8987ee727d8e1cfd0bf871ae4a5\",\"changedFiles\":11,\"commits\":[{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-08-29T19:46:52Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"257360244+suhaanthayyil@users.noreply.github.com\",\"id\":\"U_kgDOD1cBdA\",\"login\":\"suhaanthayyil\",\"name\":\"Suhaan Thayyil\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-08-29T19:46:52Z\",\"messageBody\":\"The workspace short-name index used to resolve a bare type name in a\\nsignature is keyed by name alone. resolveTypeReference filtered its\\ncandidates by kind but never by language, so any workspace-unique name\\nbound confidently across a language boundary: an Erlang `-record(point,\\n{x = 0, y = 0}).` became the resolved type of an R function and of a\\nClojure function whose parameter happened to be called `point`\\n(resolution=name_only, scope=module, confidence 0.75). Neither edge is\\npossible - R cannot name an Erlang record.\\n\\nThe invented edge was also unstable. Because the impossible declaration\\ncounted towards ambiguity, adding a second same-named type anywhere in\\nthe corpus silently deleted the edge, and a foreign declaration could\\nsuppress a real, resolvable same-language one.\\n\\n\\\"Same language only\\\" would be the wrong repair: C++ and Objective-C\\nconsume C headers verbatim, Swift imports C and Objective-C declarations\\nthrough the Clang importer, JVM languages name Java classes directly,\\nTypeScript and JavaScript share one type system, and F# names C# types.\\nThose edges exist today and must survive. So the guard is a language\\ncompatibility relation - symmetric, reflexive and deliberately not\\ntransitive, since ClojureScript shares declarations with Clojure and\\nClojure with Java, but ClojureScript cannot name a Java class.\\n\\nFiltering candidates rather than emitted edges also restores the\\nsuppressed same-language edges.\",\"messageHeadline\":\"fix(sem): stop bare type names resolving across language boundaries\",\"oid\":\"21d60e9dd0ad4c36cafffaa2d237197644e5b2a5\"},{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-08-29T20:09:48Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"257360244+suhaanthayyil@users.noreply.github.com\",\"id\":\"U_kgDOD1cBdA\",\"login\":\"suhaanthayyil\",\"name\":\"Suhaan Thayyil\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-08-29T20:09:48Z\",\"messageBody\":\"…ng languages\\n\\nThe first commit filtered resolveTypeReference, because that is where the\\nErlang-record-becomes-an-R-type case surfaced. It left the rest of the same\\ndefect in place: the workspace short-name index is keyed by name alone, and call,\\nconstructor, inheritance and test resolution all read it and pick a target the\\nsame way.\\n\\nMeasured on origin/main over a corpus where Go, Python and Ruby each declare\\ntheir own Point.process, all four CALLS edges resolve by type_inferred and two\\nare wrong:\\n\\n    Go/run     -> Go/Point.process     correct\\n    Kotlin/... -> Java/Shape.area      correct, compatible languages\\n    Python/run -> Go/Point.process     WRONG\\n    Ruby/run   -> Go/Point.process     WRONG\\n\\nPython and Ruby each declare the method the call actually reaches; the resolver\\nchose Go's because the index is keyed by bare name.\\n\\nEvery lookup into that global index now filters candidates through the existing\\nlanguage-compatibility relation. Same-file and same-package candidate lists are\\nleft alone: they cannot cross a language boundary, so filtering them would cost a\\npass for no possible change. Four sites are deliberately NOT filtered because no\\nreferring symbol is in scope to filter against -- resolveQualifiedType,\\ngoMethodSymbolByID, methodReturnTypes, and the extension-member lookup, which\\nalready restricts to an exact language.\\n\\nFiltering candidates rather than emitted edges is what makes this a repair rather\\nthan a deletion. With the impossible declarations out of the candidate set the\\nreal ones resolve, so the edge count is unchanged and the two wrong edges move to\\nthe right target. The compatible Kotlin -> Java edge survives, which a\\nsame-language-only rule would have destroyed.\",\"messageHeadline\":\"fix(sem): stop call, constructor, inheritance and test targets crossi…\",\"oid\":\"b4007ad07515bfdf0054f2fe2cd01ca646912635\"},{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-08-29T21:36:00Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"257360244+suhaanthayyil@users.noreply.github.com\",\"id\":\"U_kgDOD1cBdA\",\"login\":\"suhaanthayyil\",\"name\":\"Suhaan Thayyil\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-08-29T21:36:00Z\",\"messageBody\":\"…he real one\\n\\nThe filter inside resolveCallTargets was reported as unverified when the previous\\ncommit landed: no mutation caught it and no fixture discriminated it. The reason\\nis now established -- it was redundant. All four call sites already wrap their\\nsymbolsByShortName lookup in sharedTypeCandidates, because the referring symbol\\nis known there, so the in-function call was a second application of an\\nidempotent function. Worse, it looked like the guard while the call sites were\\nthe guard.\\n\\nThe path it was meant to protect is real and is now covered. A bare free-function\\ncall with no receiver ends at the globally-unique gate (\\\"direct call expression\\nmatched globally unique symbol name\\\", name_only, 0.68), which fires when exactly\\none symbol in the workspace carries the name -- so a name defined only in a\\nforeign language is globally unique by construction. Unwrapping the bare-name\\ncall site reproduces it exactly:\\n\\n    Python/driver2_py -> Ruby/beta_only   resolution name_only\\n\\nThe earlier attempt to provoke this failed for two reasons worth recording: the\\nfirst fixture defined the name in BOTH languages, so it was never globally\\nunique and the gate never fired; the second put the call and the definition in\\none file, where the same-file branch returns before the global list is consulted.\\nThe name must exist exactly once, in a foreign language, in another file.\\n\\nThe new test discriminates in both directions: a Python-to-Python call across\\nfiles must still resolve (proving the gate is reachable and the filter has not\\ndisabled cross-file resolution), and the Python-to-Ruby call must not.\",\"messageHeadline\":\"fix(sem): drop the redundant in-function candidate filter, and test t…\",\"oid\":\"c43c83c8f8525a9eeceac65dadc709693336933f\"},{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-08-30T15:18:49Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"257360244+suhaanthayyil@users.noreply.github.com\",\"id\":\"U_kgDOD1cBdA\",\"login\":\"suhaanthayyil\",\"name\":\"Suhaan Thayyil\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-08-30T15:18:49Z\",\"messageBody\":\"The language-compatibility groups treat Clojure and ClojureScript as sharing\\ndeclarations, which is true only for portable sources. parser.go maps BOTH .clj\\n(JVM-only) and .cljc (portable) to the language \\\"Clojure\\\", so a pair rule stated\\nin language names cannot tell them apart, and a .cljs consumer bound a\\ndeclaration it can never name. Measured before this change:\\n\\n    ClojureScript(src/app.cljs) -> Clojure(src/jvmonly.clj)    WRONG\\n    ClojureScript(src/app.cljs) -> Clojure(src/portable.cljc)  correct\\n\\nThe refinement reads the declaring file's extension, and only for a\\ncross-language pair: within one language the question does not arise, and a\\n.clj referring to a .clj is exactly what should resolve. It is symmetric in the\\nuseful direction too, since a ClojureScript symbol always comes from .cljs, so a\\nJVM Clojure file no longer binds a ClojureScript-only declaration either.\\n\\nSevering the dialect pair would also have removed the wrong edge, and would have\\ntaken the correct one with it; the test asserts both halves so that shortcut\\nfails.\",\"messageHeadline\":\"fix(sem): let ClojureScript bind only portable Clojure declarations\",\"oid\":\"b941de3be78709dc41706d1a4081c1fc84033561\"},{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-08-30T15:21:21Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"257360244+suhaanthayyil@users.noreply.github.com\",\"id\":\"U_kgDOD1cBdA\",\"login\":\"suhaanthayyil\",\"name\":\"Suhaan Thayyil\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-08-30T15:21:21Z\",\"messageBody\":\"The shared-declaration matrix asserted that ClojureScript binds a type declared\\nin tick.clj. That encodes the defect the previous commit fixes: .clj is JVM-only\\nand a .cljs consumer cannot name it, so the assertion pinned the wrong\\nbehaviour and failed once the refinement landed.\\n\\nThe declaration moves to tick.cljc, the only Clojure source both readers accept.\\nThe assertion is unchanged and still pins what the test is for -- that a\\nlegitimate cross-language declaration edge survives the language filter.\",\"messageHeadline\":\"test(sem): pin the Clojure shared-declaration case to a portable source\",\"oid\":\"ea278b9483581a7c021cb157057051b960fd6ae0\"},{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-09-02T21:13:46Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"257360244+suhaanthayyil@users.noreply.github.com\",\"id\":\"U_kgDOD1cBdA\",\"login\":\"suhaanthayyil\",\"name\":\"Suhaan Thayyil\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-09-02T21:13:46Z\",\"messageBody\":\"Two trail findings on trail 142.\\n\\n01M19M7CBDBX -- the C family was declared as one symmetric group, but its\\ninteroperability runs one way. C++ and Objective-C compile C headers\\nunchanged and Swift imports both through the Clang importer; C has no way to\\nname a C++ class or template, and an Objective-C `.m` cannot name a C++ type\\neither -- only `.mm` can. Listing them symmetrically licensed exactly the\\nimpossible edges this file exists to remove, just in the other direction. The\\nrelation map was already keyed from -> targets, so the fix is a one-way table\\nbeside the symmetric groups rather than a new mechanism.\\n\\nTwo tests encoded the defect and are corrected with it. One asserted\\n`languagesShareTypes(\\\"C\\\", \\\"C++\\\")`; the other's fixture was\\n`void use_shape(Shape s)` in a .c file, which does not compile as C at all.\\nThe legitimate direction stays pinned by C++/renderWidget->C/Widget and\\nSwift/useVec->C/Vec3, both of which still resolve.\\n\\n01M19M6D39B1 -- `.zsh` is labelled Zsh and `.sh` is labelled Bash, and no\\ngroup related them, so `source ./lib.sh; helper` lost its CALLS edge even\\nthough sourcing executes the library's functions in the calling shell. They\\nare now one symmetric pair, which is the correct shape: the naming works in\\nwhichever direction the source is written.\",\"messageHeadline\":\"fix(sem): make C-family type sharing directional, and relate the shells\",\"oid\":\"9f96eb21bbbef668b9f9d1f6c964eabf8cd5abe6\"},{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-09-02T23:14:36Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"257360244+suhaanthayyil@users.noreply.github.com\",\"id\":\"U_kgDOD1cBdA\",\"login\":\"suhaanthayyil\",\"name\":\"Suhaan Thayyil\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-09-02T23:14:36Z\",\"messageBody\":\"…ither dialect\\n\\nTwo follow-up findings on trail 142, both against the previous commit.\\n\\n01M1HZK6P1Z9 -- Swift's direct C++ interoperability imports C++ types and\\nfunctions, so `Swift -> C++` is a real edge the one-way table left out. The\\nreverse stays refused: C++ has no way to name a Swift declaration.\\n\\n01M1HZK6NNTT -- the Clojure refinement decided from the CANDIDATE's extension\\nalone, so every reference out of a portable `.cljc` file to a\\nClojureScript-only declaration was rejected. A reader conditional --\\n`#?(:cljs ...)` -- is exactly how portable code names a dialect-specific\\ndeclaration, and `.cljc` is compiled by both readers. A single-dialect\\nconsumer still needs a portable declaration, which is the half this must not\\nloosen: a `.cljs` reader cannot read `.clj`.\",\"messageHeadline\":\"fix(sem): let Swift reach C++, and let a portable Clojure file name e…\",\"oid\":\"07ff4e21d7659128cd0d8ad3c7c84cd15e40bbfe\"},{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-09-02T23:42:09Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"257360244+suhaanthayyil@users.noreply.github.com\",\"id\":\"U_kgDOD1cBdA\",\"login\":\"suhaanthayyil\",\"name\":\"Suhaan Thayyil\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-09-02T23:42:09Z\",\"messageBody\":\"Trail finding 01M1J6FZ0JZV, against the previous commit. The language label\\nfollows the FILE, not the declaration: a header holding any Objective-C\\nsyntax is labelled Objective-C even where it also declares plain C structs and\\nfunctions, which C++ compiles unchanged. Refusing the pair therefore dropped\\nvalid C++ edges to those declarations wholesale.\\n\\nThe reverse stays refused -- Objective-C `.m` cannot name a C++ type, only\\n`.mm` can, and that pair is already listed.\",\"messageHeadline\":\"fix(sem): let C++ consume Objective-C-labelled declarations\",\"oid\":\"e1ecc4c38be795d1478b796292212819a8239aab\"},{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-09-02T23:48:59Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"257360244+suhaanthayyil@users.noreply.github.com\",\"id\":\"U_kgDOD1cBdA\",\"login\":\"suhaanthayyil\",\"name\":\"Suhaan Thayyil\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-09-02T23:48:59Z\",\"messageBody\":\"Trail finding 01M1J83902SV. An `@objc` Swift class or method is declared in the\\ngenerated `<Module>-Swift.h` header and named directly from Objective-C, so\\nthose type, inheritance, constructor and call edges are real and were being\\ndropped.\\n\\nWith both directions real -- Swift imports Objective-C through the Clang\\nimporter, Objective-C names Swift through the generated header -- the pair is\\nsymmetric rather than one-way, and moves to the symmetric list. The rest of the\\nfamily stays directional: C still cannot name C++, and Objective-C `.m` still\\ncannot name a C++ type.\",\"messageHeadline\":\"fix(sem): let Objective-C name Swift declarations\",\"oid\":\"d3f6fd695f863338dd6e808593f237a7346d3672\"},{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-09-03T02:00:22Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"suhaanthayyil0@gmail.com\",\"id\":\"U_kgDOD1cBdA\",\"login\":\"suhaanthayyil\",\"name\":\"Suhaan Thayyil\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-09-03T02:00:22Z\",\"messageBody\":\"The language-compatibility relation answers one question: may source written in\\nlanguage A name a type DECLARED in language B. Test resolution was swept into\\nthe same filter by analogy with call, constructor and inheritance resolution,\\nbut a TESTS edge names no type. It records a naming convention -- test_frobnicate\\ncovers frobnicate -- and harnesses cross language boundaries as a matter of\\ncourse: pytest over a C extension, a shell script over a compiled binary, JS\\nspecs over a WASM module.\\n\\nMeasured on this branch, a pytest harness beside a C unit produced no TESTS edge\\nat all:\\n\\n    tests/test_frobnicate.py::test_frobnicate -> src/frobnicate.c::frobnicate\\n        dropped, Python cannot name a C type\\n\\nNothing about that binding is ambiguous: frobnicate is workspace-unique among\\nnon-test callables, so resolveTestSubject would have taken it by name_only.\\n\\nThe gate is also asymmetric here in a way that makes no sense for a convention.\\nThe C family relation is directional by design -- C++ and Swift reach C, and C\\nnames nothing -- so a C test could never reach a non-C unit however unambiguous\\nthe subject name, while a C++ test in the same repo could.\\n\\nPrecision for TESTS already comes from resolveTestSubject's evidence order:\\nsame-file, then the test file's import bindings, then a workspace-unique or\\nsame-directory match, and nothing otherwise. That ordering, not type interop, is\\nthe right guard. The other lookups into the workspace short-name index keep the\\nfilter unchanged.\",\"messageHeadline\":\"fix(sem): stop type-sharing from gating TESTS edges\",\"oid\":\"f51de3ec6cbfa2fdc7c793ad392b69db87609959\"},{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-09-03T02:14:45Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"257360244+suhaanthayyil@users.noreply.github.com\",\"id\":\"U_kgDOD1cBdA\",\"login\":\"suhaanthayyil\",\"name\":\"Suhaan Thayyil\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-09-03T02:14:45Z\",\"messageBody\":\"importedReceiverCallRelations filtered its workspace short-name lookup through\\nsharedTypeCandidates before testing the import, so a receiver call that an\\nexplicit import had already bound to a parsed local file was thrown away\\nwhenever the two files were written in different languages.\\n\\nThe language-compatibility relation answers one question: may source in language\\nA name a type DECLARED in language B. This binding is not a type reference. It\\nis an import whose module path resolves to the callee's FILE, which is direct\\nevidence that these two files interoperate, and that outranks a language-pair\\nheuristic. Python/C is deliberately absent from the relation because a Python\\ncaller never names a C struct -- but it does call the extension's exported\\nfunctions.\\n\\nMeasured on this branch, `import frobnicate` beside a parsed `frobnicate.c`:\\n\\n    app.py::run -> external:symbol:frobnicate.compute   (import_external, 0.78)\\n\\ninstead of the internal edge. The identical Python-to-Python fixture resolved\\nto `frobnicate.py::compute` at import_resolved/0.84, so the language pair was\\nthe only difference.\\n\\nImport evidence outranks the heuristic only while it is unambiguous. Module\\npaths are matched by suffix, so one import can match same-named callables in\\nseveral languages at once, and nothing then says which one it bound; emitting\\nthe fanout or picking a winner would both invent an FFI edge. The type-sharing\\ncandidates are therefore still preferred whenever they resolve anything -- every\\npreviously resolved call, same-language fanout included, is unchanged -- and the\\nunfiltered set is consulted only when it names exactly one target. The change is\\nstrictly additive: it can only resolve calls that previously degraded to an\\nunresolved external target.\",\"messageHeadline\":\"fix(sem): let import evidence outrank type-sharing for receiver calls\",\"oid\":\"ccbb244db3b74efee35eb1ec26a643fd243d4ab4\"},{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-09-03T20:04:08Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"thomas@dohmke.de\",\"id\":\"MDQ6VXNlcjcwNzIw\",\"login\":\"ashtom\",\"name\":\"Thomas Dohmke\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-09-03T20:04:08Z\",\"messageBody\":\"\",\"messageHeadline\":\"Merge origin/main into fix/cross-language-name-only-edges\",\"oid\":\"f7ca6ec9c73cce0c54b371f5e91b40cc84733ed4\"},{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-09-03T20:25:06Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"thomas@dohmke.de\",\"id\":\"MDQ6VXNlcjcwNzIw\",\"login\":\"ashtom\",\"name\":\"Thomas Dohmke\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-09-03T20:25:06Z\",\"messageBody\":\"\",\"messageHeadline\":\"fix: filter receiver type candidates by language\",\"oid\":\"b8aace015c59224944bd9d9b8927958154403bbb\"},{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-09-03T20:40:05Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"257360244+suhaanthayyil@users.noreply.github.com\",\"id\":\"U_kgDOD1cBdA\",\"login\":\"suhaanthayyil\",\"name\":\"Suhaan Thayyil\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-09-03T20:40:05Z\",\"messageBody\":\"`import frobnicate; frobnicate.compute(1)` resolves to a locally parsed\\nfrobnicate.c, but `from frobnicate import compute; compute(1)` did not.\\nresolveCallTargets received candidates already narrowed by the\\nlanguage-compatibility relation, so the C declaration was gone before the\\nimport tier could compare its module path, and the call degraded to an\\nunresolved external target.\\n\\nThat relation answers whether source in one language may name a type\\nDECLARED in another. An explicit import whose module path resolves to the\\ncallee's FILE is direct evidence that the two files interoperate, which\\noutranks it -- the reason the qualified spelling of the very same call\\nalready resolves in importedReceiverCallTargets.\\n\\nresolveCallTargets now narrows internally and keeps both sets. The import\\ntier prefers the compatible candidates, so every previously resolved call\\nis unchanged, and consults the wider set only when it names exactly one\\ntarget: module paths are matched by suffix, so one import can match\\nsame-named callables in several languages at once, and nothing then says\\nwhich of them it bound.\",\"messageHeadline\":\"fix(sem): let import evidence outrank the language filter for bare calls\",\"oid\":\"fb89aeb5c4dd26b13d1e08c6d2b4542efb96345c\"},{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-09-03T20:58:04Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"257360244+suhaanthayyil@users.noreply.github.com\",\"id\":\"U_kgDOD1cBdA\",\"login\":\"suhaanthayyil\",\"name\":\"Suhaan Thayyil\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-09-03T20:58:04Z\",\"messageBody\":\"The bare-call import tiers read an explicit import as evidence that\\noutranks the language-compatibility relation. That makes Python's scoping\\nload-bearing where it used to be cosmetic: a name the caller's own body\\nbinds is now resolved into a CONFIDENT cross-language edge instead of a\\nmerely unresolved one.\\n\\n    from frobnicate import compute   # frobnicate.c is parsed\\n\\n    def run(compute):\\n        return compute(1)            # calls the PARAMETER\\n\\nbound run to frobnicate.c's compute, and so did a body that rebinds the\\nname from a callback (`compute = callback`). A function-local name is\\nlocal for the whole body in Python, so at every call site in that body it\\nis the binding, not the same-named file-level or imported callable, and no\\nresolution tier may bind it to one.\\n\\nresolveCallTargets now declines a name the caller supplies as locally\\nbound, which leaves the call to the imported-external fallback exactly as\\nit degraded before the import tiers could reach past the language filter.\\npythonLocalBindingNames reads the binding forms out of one callable body:\\nparameters (including lambda's), assignment, tuple, annotated, augmented\\nand walrus targets, loop and context variables, exception aliases, and\\nnested definitions -- deliberately conservative toward suppression, since\\na name used as a parameter anywhere inside one callable is ambiguous\\nevidence at best.\\n\\nThe set is supplied only for callable bodies. Module scope is not a\\nshadowing scope: a `def` there binds the very symbol a top-level call is\\nmeant to reach, so the file-level scan passes none and keeps resolving\\nthrough the same import evidence.\",\"messageHeadline\":\"fix(sem): a locally bound Python name is not the imported callable\",\"oid\":\"3ef7deae1a7a54f257b8c8dc7f11f6c8cb7ebae1\"},{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-09-03T21:19:07Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"257360244+suhaanthayyil@users.noreply.github.com\",\"id\":\"U_kgDOD1cBdA\",\"login\":\"suhaanthayyil\",\"name\":\"Suhaan Thayyil\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-09-03T21:19:07Z\",\"messageBody\":\"pythonLocalBindingNames scanned one callable's whole block flat, so every\\nbinding form it found became a body-wide shadow. A `lambda`'s parameters,\\na comprehension's loop variables, and a nested `def`'s parameters and\\nlocals are none of those: they bind inside the scope that declares them.\\n\\n    from frobnicate import compute\\n\\n    def run(source):\\n        handler = lambda compute: compute(1)\\n        return compute(source)   # calls the IMPORT, not the parameter\\n\\nThe direction of that error is the expensive one. resolveCallTargets\\ndeclines a bound name before any resolution tier runs, so a name wrongly\\nreported as bound does not merely soften an edge -- it deletes it. The\\nfixture above lost its import-resolved edge to frobnicate.c and degraded\\nto an unresolved external target; a same-file callee lost its edge\\noutright, as did a body holding a nested `def` that names a parameter\\nafter it. `global`/`nonlocal` declare the very opposite of a local\\nbinding and were read as one too, suppressing calls to the module-level\\nsymbol the declaration points at.\\n\\nThe scan is now per-scope: nested `def`/`class` bodies (by indentation),\\nlambdas (to the end of the expression holding them), and comprehensions\\n(Python 3 scopes their variables to the brackets) are cut out of the\\ntext before the binding forms are read, and each is scanned as a scope of\\nits own. A nested definition still binds its own NAME in the enclosing\\nscope, and a name an inner scope binds still joins the set when every\\noccurrence of it in the body is inside that scope -- the one case where a\\nsingle flat set answers correctly for every call site, and the case that\\nkeeps a lambda's parameter shadowing the import for the calls in its own\\nbody. When the uses straddle the boundary the name is left out, because\\nresolving one call too many costs a softer edge while suppressing one\\ndeletes a real one.\\n\\nNames declared `global` or `nonlocal` are removed from the set: those\\nstatements say the name is the module's or the enclosing function's,\\nwhich is the symbol a call by that name is meant to reach.\",\"messageHeadline\":\"fix(sem): a nested scope's Python bindings are not the enclosing body's\",\"oid\":\"d363e75a2a81786482a6d2bb43baf7a55deedb80\"},{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-09-03T21:38:03Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"257360244+suhaanthayyil@users.noreply.github.com\",\"id\":\"U_kgDOD1cBdA\",\"login\":\"suhaanthayyil\",\"name\":\"Suhaan Thayyil\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-09-03T21:38:03Z\",\"messageBody\":\"Two of the one-way edges in typeSharingLanguageEdges are true of the\\nlanguage PAIR but not of every declaration under it, and the candidate\\nfilter read only the pair.\\n\\nC++ -> Objective-C exists because the Objective-C label follows the FILE:\\na header holding any Objective-C syntax is labelled Objective-C even\\nwhere it also declares the plain C structs and functions a `.cpp`\\ncompiles unchanged, and refusing the pair dropped those edges wholesale.\\nIt does not follow that a `.cpp` can name an `@interface`, an `@protocol`\\nor a selector. Measured over a fixture with an `@interface Shape` header\\nand an `@implementation` method:\\n\\n    C++/renderShape  -> Objective-C/Shape        impossible\\n    C++/callIt       -> Objective-C/computeArea  impossible (message send)\\n    C++/renderWidget -> Objective-C/Widget       correct (plain C struct)\\n\\nObjective-C -> Swift exists because of the generated `<Module>-Swift.h`\\nheader, which holds only what Swift exposes to the Objective-C runtime --\\nfar less than the module. Binding by bare name offered it all of it:\\n\\n    Objective-C/useLedger  -> Swift/Ledger    a struct is never bridged\\n    Objective-C/useEngine  -> Swift/Engine    a pure-Swift root class\\n    Objective-C/go         -> Swift/freeFunc  a global func is not bridged\\n    Objective-C/useBridged -> Swift/Bridged   correct (@objc, NSObject)\\n\\nThe relation itself is unchanged -- no edge is removed and nothing is\\nflattened or made symmetric. The refinement is at the CANDIDATE level,\\nwhere candidateSharesDeclarations already refines the Clojure dialects\\nthat one language name cannot separate: for C++ the Objective-C-only\\ndeclaration forms are dropped, and for Objective-C a Swift declaration is\\nkept only when it could appear in the generated header, decided from the\\nattribute and inheritance clause the extractor keeps in its signature.\\n\\nOnly what the language rules make impossible is refused. A declaration\\nwhose exposure cannot be decided from its own signature -- a member,\\nwhose exposure follows its container; a subclass whose chain to NSObject\\nruns through classes a candidate-level check cannot follow; a signature\\nthe parser could not read -- is kept, because dropping a candidate here\\ndeletes the edge rather than merely adding one. Both tests assert the\\nlegitimate edge of the pair still resolves beside the impossible ones.\",\"messageHeadline\":\"fix(sem): the C-family language pairs are not declaration-blind\",\"oid\":\"904e1097608ef454f245aee82698a8c1be959742\"},{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-09-03T21:38:17Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"257360244+suhaanthayyil@users.noreply.github.com\",\"id\":\"U_kgDOD1cBdA\",\"login\":\"suhaanthayyil\",\"name\":\"Suhaan Thayyil\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-09-03T21:38:17Z\",\"messageBody\":\"The import-name map the call resolver reads is built for a whole FILE,\\nbut Python scopes an `import` inside a function to that function. The map\\ntherefore offered a function-local binding to every other function in the\\nfile, and because the bare-call tiers read an explicit import as evidence\\nthat OUTRANKS the language-compatibility relation, the result was not a\\nweak edge but a confident cross-language one:\\n\\n    def uses_import():\\n        from frobnicate import compute   # frobnicate.c is parsed\\n        return compute(1)\\n\\n    def unrelated():\\n        return compute(3)                # NameError at runtime\\n\\n    Python/unrelated -> C/compute   res=import_resolved conf=0.86\\n\\nNames bound only by a nested-scope import are now collected with the\\nscopes they are visible in, and each symbol resolves against the imports\\nit can actually see. A symbol sees such an import two ways: the statement\\nis inside its own block, or the scope holding the statement encloses it --\\na nested `def` really does see the import its enclosing function made, so\\nconfining the import to its own function alone would have deleted that\\ncall instead of the unrelated one.\\n\\nNothing else narrows. A name any module-level import also binds is left\\nout of the map entirely, and so is an indented import that no\\n`def`/`class` encloses (the `if TYPE_CHECKING:` idiom), because both are\\nvisible file-wide already; for a file whose imports are all module-level\\nthe map is empty and the resolver is handed the original map unchanged.\\nThe async-call and data-flow paths resolve through the same visible map,\\nso the mirrored DATA_FLOWS edge goes with the call that justified it.\",\"messageHeadline\":\"fix(sem): a Python import inside one function is not the file's\",\"oid\":\"7a185edb2ef9f78477339f53dd67658cb553bea2\"},{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-09-03T21:58:32Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"thomas@dohmke.de\",\"id\":\"MDQ6VXNlcjcwNzIw\",\"login\":\"ashtom\",\"name\":\"Thomas Dohmke\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-09-03T21:58:32Z\",\"messageBody\":\"\",\"messageHeadline\":\"fix: preserve scoped bare FFI imports\",\"oid\":\"d969441dccd88300126db7f349cb11bee583dddf\"},{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-09-03T22:10:19Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"257360244+suhaanthayyil@users.noreply.github.com\",\"id\":\"U_kgDOD1cBdA\",\"login\":\"suhaanthayyil\",\"name\":\"Suhaan Thayyil\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-09-03T22:10:19Z\",\"messageBody\":\"The scope pass feeds resolveCallTargets, which DECLINES a bound name\\nbefore any resolution tier runs, so a binding it reports that Python does\\nnot make deletes a real call edge outright. Three shapes reported one:\\n\\n  - A line inside brackets is not a statement. `schedule(\\\\n compute=1,\\\\n)`\\n    put a keyword ARGUMENT at the start of a line, where the assignment\\n    scanner read it as a binding, and brackets that never close -- a parse\\n    failure, which the pass took for a clean result -- left a\\n    comprehension unrecognised so its `for` target was read as the body's.\\n    Bracket depth is now counted on the scope's own text and the statement\\n    scanners read only what is at depth zero, so unbalanced source reports\\n    fewer bindings rather than invented ones.\\n\\n  - A name bound in one nested scope was tested for use against the\\n    enclosing body's text alone, which has EVERY nested scope masked out\\n    of it. `key=lambda compute: compute` beside `[compute(v) for v in\\n    values]` therefore looked body-wide and deleted the comprehension's\\n    call. A use in a sibling nested scope now counts as a use.\\n\\n  - PEP 572: an assignment expression inside a comprehension binds in the\\n    scope that CONTAINS the comprehension, unlike the loop variable beside\\n    it. Its targets are hoisted out, through nested comprehensions but not\\n    through a lambda or a def, where a walrus stops.\\n\\nVerified against 247 files of the CPython 3.12 standard library: four\\ncall and data-flow edges restored, none removed.\",\"messageHeadline\":\"fix(sem): a Python binding must come from a statement of that scope\",\"oid\":\"34ccacc726199eb11eba3ee4f565c75c7d29a00c\"},{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-09-03T22:14:12Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"257360244+suhaanthayyil@users.noreply.github.com\",\"id\":\"U_kgDOD1cBdA\",\"login\":\"suhaanthayyil\",\"name\":\"Suhaan Thayyil\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-09-03T22:14:12Z\",\"messageBody\":\"Confining a function-local import cuts both ways: a name hidden from a\\nsymbol that CAN see it loses the import evidence its call edge rests on,\\nso a wrong confinement deletes an edge, while a name left visible where\\nPython would not see it binds a call the caller never reaches.\\n\\n  - `def _load(): global np; import numpy as np` is a real idiom. The\\n    `global` declares that the import binds the MODULE's name, so the rest\\n    of the file does see it; confining it to _load deleted every other\\n    function's edge. A name declared global in the scope that imports it\\n    is now module-level. `nonlocal` is the bounded half of the same rule:\\n    the scope widens to the enclosing function, not to the whole file.\\n\\n  - A class body is not a lexical scope for the functions defined in it --\\n    Python skips class scope when resolving a name inside a method, so a\\n    method naming a class-body import raises NameError. Treating the class\\n    as an enclosing scope bound that call, at import_resolved confidence\\n    and across a language boundary, to a function it cannot reach.\\n\\nA nested `def` still sees the import its enclosing FUNCTION made, and a\\nmodule-level import is still visible everywhere; both are fenced by tests.\",\"messageHeadline\":\"fix(sem): a Python import's scope is the one Python gives it\",\"oid\":\"0975a3f2920316143736341b090d5c62f9a9ee5e\"},{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-09-03T22:14:50Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"thomas@dohmke.de\",\"id\":\"MDQ6VXNlcjcwNzIw\",\"login\":\"ashtom\",\"name\":\"Thomas Dohmke\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-09-03T22:14:50Z\",\"messageBody\":\"\",\"messageHeadline\":\"merge: reconcile PR 184 review fixes\",\"oid\":\"c42703c6200ab201cb427563edcf50d1b0ecc79d\"},{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-09-03T22:18:19Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"thomas@dohmke.de\",\"id\":\"MDQ6VXNlcjcwNzIw\",\"login\":\"ashtom\",\"name\":\"Thomas Dohmke\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-09-03T22:18:19Z\",\"messageBody\":\"\",\"messageHeadline\":\"merge: retain reviewed PR 184 implementation\",\"oid\":\"f1198b6293f59481ea9e191d1e8f2ab76de804be\"},{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-09-03T22:32:39Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"thomas@dohmke.de\",\"id\":\"MDQ6VXNlcjcwNzIw\",\"login\":\"ashtom\",\"name\":\"Thomas Dohmke\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-09-03T22:32:39Z\",\"messageBody\":\"\",\"messageHeadline\":\"fix: retain resolved scoped Python imports\",\"oid\":\"982cfb5c33a736ec1f489d906006dd6be44ec206\"},{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-09-03T22:43:48Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"257360244+suhaanthayyil@users.noreply.github.com\",\"id\":\"U_kgDOD1cBdA\",\"login\":\"suhaanthayyil\",\"name\":\"Suhaan Thayyil\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-09-03T22:43:48Z\",\"messageBody\":\"`from mod import compute as c` binds `c` to mod's `compute`, but every\\nresolution tier looks the call name up in the workspace short-name index\\nand `c` is defined nowhere. The resolved edge was lost and replaced by an\\nexternal target named `mod.c` -- a symbol that exists in no language,\\nwhich is strictly worse than the missing edge it stood in for.\\n\\nThe alias is now carried alongside its original: the import parser records\\nthe member name each local name is bound to, and a bare call through an\\nalias resolves under that member through exactly the two import tiers the\\nun-aliased spelling already uses. Same target, resolution, scope and\\nconfidence; the same ambiguity suppression, so two same-named units behind\\none alias still produce no local edge. Same-file and workspace-wide name\\ntiers stay out of it: the alias defines nothing, so a match there would be\\na coincidence of spelling.\\n\\nTwo guards keep the fix from inventing edges of its own. An alias bound to\\ntwo different members, or one that also names a module, resolves to\\nnothing rather than to a guess; and a name that only ever binds a MODULE\\n(`import mod`, `import mod as m`) emits no bare-call external edge at all,\\nbecause a module object is not callable and `mod.m` names nothing.\",\"messageHeadline\":\"fix(sem): resolve a Python import alias to the member it renames\",\"oid\":\"bcab954997b535d4760fdbc70570a05c46f778b6\"},{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-09-03T22:59:49Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"257360244+suhaanthayyil@users.noreply.github.com\",\"id\":\"U_kgDOD1cBdA\",\"login\":\"suhaanthayyil\",\"name\":\"Suhaan Thayyil\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-09-03T22:59:49Z\",\"messageBody\":\"A call in a Python function signature -- a default argument, a parameter\\nannotation, a return annotation -- is evaluated where the `def` statement\\nruns, in the enclosing scope. The scope walker descended only into the\\nfunction body, so such a call was recorded in no scope at all. The call\\nscan still attributes it to the function whose signature line holds it,\\nand a `complete` scope view reporting no modules for that name makes\\nimportsWithName DELETE the file-level import binding, so the imported\\ncall loses its resolution tier entirely: a Python-to-C `compute()` in a\\ndefault argument emitted no CALLS edge at all. Walk the signature nodes\\nagainst the enclosing bindings, published under the function's owner.\\nDecorators already worked -- tree-sitter hangs them off\\n`decorated_definition`, outside the function node.\\n\\n`from mod import (compute,)` splits on commas into `(compute` and `)`,\\nso the grouping parentheses were parsed as part of the bound name,\\nbinding a name no call can match while the scope stayed complete. Trim\\nthem in parsePythonImportItem, which the three line-oriented import\\nscanners share; the multi-line form, which those scanners cannot see\\npast the first line of, is restored by the AST-derived scope binding.\",\"messageHeadline\":\"fix(sem): resolve Python signature calls and parenthesised imports\",\"oid\":\"6c91abc64935609ca0061bba5c2ff580f46f9dde\"},{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-09-03T23:10:35Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"257360244+suhaanthayyil@users.noreply.github.com\",\"id\":\"U_kgDOD1cBdA\",\"login\":\"suhaanthayyil\",\"name\":\"Suhaan Thayyil\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-09-03T23:10:35Z\",\"messageBody\":\"A `.h` is labelled Objective-C the moment it contains one `#import` or\\none `@interface`, and such headers go on declaring the plain C structs,\\ntypedefs and functions a `.c` includes and compiles verbatim. C named\\nnothing in typeSharingLanguageEdges, so every one of those targets was\\nfiltered out of the candidate list.\\n\\nMeasured over a fixture whose `.c` includes an Objective-C-labelled\\nheader and an otherwise identical plain-C control:\\n\\n\\tC/renderGadget -> C/Gadget               resolved\\n\\tC/renderWidget -> Objective-C/Widget     DROPPED (same declaration)\\n\\tC/callWidth    -> Objective-C/widgetWidth DROPPED\\n\\nAdd the directional `C -> Objective-C` edge and hold it to the same\\nkind refinement the C++ edge already carries: an `@interface`, an\\n`@protocol` and a selector stay unreachable from C, which cannot even\\ndeclare them. The edge is one-way at the family level -- C still cannot\\nname a C++ class or template, nor a Swift declaration.\\n\\nTestLanguagesShareTypesRelation pinned `C -> Objective-C` as\\nimpossible; that assertion was the bug, so it moves to the symmetric\\nblock with the reason the pair is symmetric at the language level and\\nstill narrowed by kind at the candidate level.\",\"messageHeadline\":\"fix(sem): let C name the plain C half of an Objective-C header\",\"oid\":\"071afb519d8c552fac49062d531300f2e36de18a\"},{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-09-03T23:41:30Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"257360244+suhaanthayyil@users.noreply.github.com\",\"id\":\"U_kgDOD1cBdA\",\"login\":\"suhaanthayyil\",\"name\":\"Suhaan Thayyil\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-09-03T23:41:30Z\",\"messageBody\":\"A `.h` is labelled C++ the moment it holds `extern \\\"C\\\"` or a bare `::`\\n(looksLikeCPlusPlusHeader), so the dual-use header -- the one written\\nprecisely SO a `.c` can include it -- is C++, and C names no C++ target in\\ntypeSharingLanguageEdges. A `.c` including such a header reached nothing in\\nit.\\n\\nTwo defects stacked. The first sits upstream of any language rule: an\\n`extern \\\"C\\\" {` line was blanked out of the source before the C++ grammar saw\\nit, and the blanking ran forward until a line beginning `}  // extern \\\"C\\\"`\\n-- or, failing that, to END OF FILE. The canonical dual-use header closes\\nwith a bare `}` under `#ifdef __cplusplus`, so the whole header was erased\\nand contributed a single macro symbol. tree-sitter-cpp parses\\n`linkage_specification` natively, and the fixture the mask was written for\\n(TestTreeSitterParserCPlusPlusMasksDoctestMacros) parses and yields entities\\nwithout it, so the mask is removed rather than bounded.\\n\\nThe second is the reachability rule. Widening the language pair is not the\\nrepair: unlike the C -> Objective-C case, whose offending kinds are the\\nclosed set objectiveCOnlyDeclaration already names, C++ exposes templates,\\noverloads, namespaces and classes a C source genuinely cannot name, and\\n`C -> C++` wholesale would trade a missing-edge bug for a large class of\\nwrong ones. typeSharingLanguageEdges is therefore untouched --\\nlanguagesShareTypes(\\\"C\\\", \\\"C++\\\") stays false -- and the widening is per\\nDECLARATION instead: declaredWithCLinkage marks what sits inside an\\n`extern \\\"C\\\" { ... }` block, which is the construct a C++ header uses to say\\nexactly which declarations have C linkage, and candidateSharesDeclarations\\noffers C those and only those. `extern \\\"C++\\\"` is not matched.\\n\\nMeasured over a fixture whose `.c` includes a dual-use header and an\\notherwise identical plain-C control -- the same two declarations, differing\\nonly in the header that holds them:\\n\\n\\tC/renderGadget -> C/Gadget            resolved (control)\\n\\tC/renderWidget -> C++/Widget          DROPPED  (declared extern \\\"C\\\")\\n\\tC/callWidth    -> C++/widgetWidth     DROPPED  (declared extern \\\"C\\\")\\n\\nresolveTypeReference re-asked languagesShareTypes about a candidate list\\nsharedTypeCandidates had already filtered, and that duplicate silently\\noverrode the finer per-declaration answer: with the rule in place the CALLS\\nedge resolved while USES_TYPE and PARAM_TYPE on the struct beside it still\\ndid not. The redundant conjunct is dropped.\\n\\nThe narrowing is pinned beside the widening: a class, a namespaced function\\nand an overload set in the SAME header stay unreachable from C.\",\"messageHeadline\":\"fix(sem): let C name the extern \\\"C\\\" half of a dual-use C++ header\",\"oid\":\"a09defd6cd761a39199e3b3ca6d47c24e5581e6c\"},{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-09-04T00:04:29Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"257360244+suhaanthayyil@users.noreply.github.com\",\"id\":\"U_kgDOD1cBdA\",\"login\":\"suhaanthayyil\",\"name\":\"Suhaan Thayyil\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-09-04T00:04:29Z\",\"messageBody\":\"tree-sitter gives `except_clause` no fields: its exception expression, its\\n`as` target and its handler block are plain named children. The scope walker\\nasked it for a \\\"body\\\" field, got nothing, and returned -- so no call anywhere\\nin a handler was ever recorded. The function-wide local pass then asked the\\nsame clause for a \\\"name\\\" field, got its last named child (the handler BLOCK)\\ninstead, and recursed into it, declaring every name written anywhere in the\\nhandler a local of the whole function. That deleted the import-resolved edge\\nfor calls in the `try` body too, which the walker had recorded correctly.\\n`with_item` has no alias field either -- the alias hangs off a nested\\n`as_pattern` -- so the same mistake made `with compute() as handle:` report\\nits own import shadowed.\\n\\nTake the alias from the `as_pattern` that actually carries it, let the\\ngeneric child walk reach the exception expression and the handler body like\\nany other statement, and bind an exception group's `as` target as well.\\n\\nA comprehension's OUTERMOST iterable is evaluated where the comprehension is\\nwritten and passed into the comprehension's frame; only later iterables run\\ninside it. Evaluating all of them inside it made\\n`[x for x in compute() for compute in ys]` -- which CPython runs happily --\\nreport its import shadowed by a target bound afterwards. The interleaved\\nfilter `[x for x in xs if compute() for compute in ys]` stays suppressed:\\nCPython raises UnboundLocalError there, so that name really is the\\ncomprehension's own local.\\n\\nFinally, the scanners that record WHAT a local name is bound to are line\\noriented, so a parenthesised from-import spanning lines showed them only\\n`from mod import (`, an item list of one bare paren. No member name was\\nrecorded behind an alias imported that way, and the resolver -- which needs\\nthe member name, because the workspace index is keyed by it -- had nothing to\\nresolve, while the AST walker reported the call unshadowed. Rejoin such a\\nlist into one logical line, bounded, and consume nothing when it never\\ncloses, so a stray paren cannot hide the imports below it.\\n\\n`TestPythonBareImportFFIRespectsASTScopes` listed `except_type` among the\\nshadowed cases, but nothing in it rebinds the name: an `except <expr>:`\\nclause has no `as` target at all. It only looked shadowed because the clause\\nwas skipped, so it moves to the unshadowed list it belongs in.\",\"messageHeadline\":\"fix(sem): a Python try statement is code of its own scope\",\"oid\":\"20d89c019292b6ee7fa0ac8faa6187e81b8d9bd5\"},{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-09-04T00:36:10Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"257360244+suhaanthayyil@users.noreply.github.com\",\"id\":\"U_kgDOD1cBdA\",\"login\":\"suhaanthayyil\",\"name\":\"Suhaan Thayyil\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-09-04T00:36:10Z\",\"messageBody\":\"Python rebinds a `def` or `class` name only once the whole statement has\\nrun, so the parts it evaluates on the way there still read the enclosing\\nbinding. CPython is the oracle:\\n\\n    def compute(): return \\\"OUTER\\\"\\n    def compute(v=compute()): return v   # -> OUTER\\n    def compute(v=compute()): ...        # -> NameError with no outer name\\n\\nThe scope walker bound the name at the `def`/`class` line, so every\\neagerly evaluated part of the statement -- defaults, annotations, bases,\\nclass-body statements, the signatures of the methods a class defines --\\nwas told the name was already rebound. For an imported name that is the\\nfail-closed direction: the view reports no modules, importsWithName drops\\nthe file-level import binding and the resolved edge goes with it.\\n\\nA `def` now binds at its body, which keeps recursion resolving to the\\nfunction itself. A class body interleaves code that runs immediately with\\nmethod bodies that run later, which one offset cannot express, so the\\nclass keeps its binding at the `class` line for deferred code and hands\\nits bases and body a view where that one binding is hidden.\\n\\nNo emitted relation changes today: a definition also defines a same-named\\nsymbol, which wins the same-file tier (or a CONSTRUCTS relation) before\\nimports are consulted, and the generic loop skips a call whose name equals\\nits owner's. The scope view is the observable surface and is where the\\nregression tests assert.\",\"messageHeadline\":\"fix(sem): a Python definition binds its own name after its signature\",\"oid\":\"0ac5865624a1c724568c6840b69f499055152db1\"},{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-09-04T00:45:24Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"257360244+suhaanthayyil@users.noreply.github.com\",\"id\":\"U_kgDOD1cBdA\",\"login\":\"suhaanthayyil\",\"name\":\"Suhaan Thayyil\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-09-04T00:45:24Z\",\"messageBody\":\"…header\\n\\nA dual-use header -- `#ifdef __cplusplus` / `extern \\\"C\\\" {` -- is labelled C++\\nbecause looksLikeCPlusPlusHeader fires on that very marker. The C-linkage\\nexception in candidateSharesDeclarations admitted only C consumers, so an\\nObjective-C `.m` importing such a header reached nothing in it, even though\\n`__cplusplus` is undefined under Objective-C compilation and the preprocessor\\ndeletes the guards outright, leaving plain C the `.m` compiles verbatim.\\n\\nMeasured over a fixture whose `.m` imports such a header, with an otherwise\\nidentical plain-C header as the control:\\n\\n\\tObjective-C/renderGadget -> C/Gadget          resolved (control)\\n\\tObjective-C/renderWidget -> C++/Widget        DROPPED  (same declaration)\\n\\tObjective-C/callWidth    -> C++/widgetWidth   DROPPED  (same declaration)\\n\\nThe widening stays per DECLARATION, not per language: `Objective-C -> C++` is\\nstill absent from typeSharingLanguageEdges, and only what the header itself put\\nunder C linkage is offered. The class, the overload set and the namespaced\\nfunction in the same header keep cLinkage false and stay filtered out.\",\"messageHeadline\":\"fix(sem): let Objective-C name the extern \\\"C\\\" half of a dual-use C++ …\",\"oid\":\"cd7177e415137e9863238735282b07e7619974f5\"}],\"deletions\":102,\"headRefName\":\"fix/cross-language-name-only-edges\",\"headRefOid\":\"cd7177e415137e9863238735282b07e7619974f5\",\"isDraft\":false,\"mergeStateStatus\":\"UNSTABLE\",\"mergeable\":\"MERGEABLE\",\"number\":184,\"state\":\"OPEN\",\"statusCheckRollup\":[{\"__typename\":\"CheckRun\",\"completedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:45:52Z\",\"conclusion\":\"SUCCESS\",\"detailsUrl\":\"https://github.com/entireio/entire-graph/actions/runs/33823078438/job/100869770015\",\"name\":\"lint\",\"startedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:45:34Z\",\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"workflowName\":\"test\"},{\"__typename\":\"CheckRun\",\"completedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:45:53Z\",\"conclusion\":\"SUCCESS\",\"detailsUrl\":\"https://github.com/entireio/entire-graph/actions/runs/33823078438/job/100869769753\",\"name\":\"build (ubuntu-latest)\",\"startedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:45:32Z\",\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"workflowName\":\"test\"},{\"__typename\":\"CheckRun\",\"completedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:45:54Z\",\"conclusion\":\"SUCCESS\",\"detailsUrl\":\"https://github.com/entireio/entire-graph/actions/runs/33823078438/job/100869769874\",\"name\":\"build (macos-latest)\",\"startedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:45:33Z\",\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"workflowName\":\"test\"},{\"__typename\":\"CheckRun\",\"completedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:46:35Z\",\"conclusion\":\"SUCCESS\",\"detailsUrl\":\"https://github.com/entireio/entire-graph/actions/runs/33823078438/job/100869769794\",\"name\":\"build (windows-latest)\",\"startedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:45:33Z\",\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"workflowName\":\"test\"},{\"__typename\":\"CheckRun\",\"completedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:48:13Z\",\"conclusion\":\"SUCCESS\",\"detailsUrl\":\"https://github.com/entireio/entire-graph/actions/runs/33823078438/job/100869769749\",\"name\":\"test (ubuntu-latest)\",\"startedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:45:32Z\",\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"workflowName\":\"test\"},{\"__typename\":\"CheckRun\",\"completedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:52:00Z\",\"conclusion\":\"SUCCESS\",\"detailsUrl\":\"https://github.com/entireio/entire-graph/actions/runs/33823078438/job/100869769885\",\"name\":\"test (macos-latest)\",\"startedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:45:34Z\",\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"workflowName\":\"test\"},{\"__typename\":\"CheckRun\",\"completedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:47:14Z\",\"conclusion\":\"SUCCESS\",\"detailsUrl\":\"https://github.com/entireio/entire-graph/actions/runs/33823078438/job/100869769891\",\"name\":\"test (windows-latest) / compile and plan\",\"startedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:45:52Z\",\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"workflowName\":\"test\"},{\"__typename\":\"CheckRun\",\"completedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:46:42Z\",\"conclusion\":\"SUCCESS\",\"detailsUrl\":\"https://github.com/entireio/entire-graph/actions/runs/33823078438/job/100869770012\",\"name\":\"test (windows-latest) / vet\",\"startedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:45:33Z\",\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"workflowName\":\"test\"},{\"__typename\":\"CheckRun\",\"completedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:45:44Z\",\"conclusion\":\"SUCCESS\",\"detailsUrl\":\"https://github.com/entireio/entire-graph/actions/runs/33823078438/job/100869769847\",\"name\":\"bm25-adapter\",\"startedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:45:33Z\",\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"workflowName\":\"test\"},{\"__typename\":\"CheckRun\",\"completedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:45:55Z\",\"conclusion\":\"SUCCESS\",\"detailsUrl\":\"https://github.com/entireio/entire-graph/actions/runs/33823078438/job/100869769852\",\"name\":\"statusline (ubuntu-latest)\",\"startedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:45:33Z\",\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"workflowName\":\"test\"},{\"__typename\":\"CheckRun\",\"completedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:46:17Z\",\"conclusion\":\"SUCCESS\",\"detailsUrl\":\"https://github.com/entireio/entire-graph/actions/runs/33823078438/job/100869769632\",\"name\":\"statusline (macos-latest)\",\"startedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:45:33Z\",\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"workflowName\":\"test\"},{\"__typename\":\"CheckRun\",\"completedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:49:30Z\",\"conclusion\":\"SUCCESS\",\"detailsUrl\":\"https://github.com/entireio/entire-graph/actions/runs/33823078438/job/100870118749\",\"name\":\"test (windows-latest) / shard 0\",\"startedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:47:16Z\",\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"workflowName\":\"test\"},{\"__typename\":\"CheckRun\",\"completedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:49:54Z\",\"conclusion\":\"SUCCESS\",\"detailsUrl\":\"https://github.com/entireio/entire-graph/actions/runs/33823078438/job/100870118803\",\"name\":\"test (windows-latest) / shard 1\",\"startedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:47:16Z\",\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"workflowName\":\"test\"},{\"__typename\":\"CheckRun\",\"completedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:50:44Z\",\"conclusion\":\"SUCCESS\",\"detailsUrl\":\"https://github.com/entireio/entire-graph/actions/runs/33823078438/job/100870118706\",\"name\":\"test (windows-latest) / shard 2\",\"startedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:47:16Z\",\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"workflowName\":\"test\"},{\"__typename\":\"CheckRun\",\"completedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:49:34Z\",\"conclusion\":\"SUCCESS\",\"detailsUrl\":\"https://github.com/entireio/entire-graph/actions/runs/33823078438/job/100870118818\",\"name\":\"test (windows-latest) / shard 3\",\"startedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:47:16Z\",\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"workflowName\":\"test\"},{\"__typename\":\"CheckRun\",\"completedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:50:12Z\",\"conclusion\":\"SUCCESS\",\"detailsUrl\":\"https://github.com/entireio/entire-graph/actions/runs/33823078438/job/100870118870\",\"name\":\"test (windows-latest) / shard 4\",\"startedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:47:16Z\",\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"workflowName\":\"test\"},{\"__typename\":\"CheckRun\",\"completedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:50:14Z\",\"conclusion\":\"SUCCESS\",\"detailsUrl\":\"https://github.com/entireio/entire-graph/actions/runs/33823078438/job/100870118816\",\"name\":\"test (windows-latest) / shard 5\",\"startedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:47:19Z\",\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"workflowName\":\"test\"},{\"__typename\":\"CheckRun\",\"completedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:49:56Z\",\"conclusion\":\"SUCCESS\",\"detailsUrl\":\"https://github.com/entireio/entire-graph/actions/runs/33823078438/job/100870118808\",\"name\":\"test (windows-latest) / shard 6\",\"startedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:47:16Z\",\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"workflowName\":\"test\"},{\"__typename\":\"CheckRun\",\"completedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:49:19Z\",\"conclusion\":\"SUCCESS\",\"detailsUrl\":\"https://github.com/entireio/entire-graph/actions/runs/33823078438/job/100870118812\",\"name\":\"test (windows-latest) / shard 7\",\"startedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:47:19Z\",\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"workflowName\":\"test\"},{\"__typename\":\"CheckRun\",\"completedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:48:55Z\",\"conclusion\":\"SUCCESS\",\"detailsUrl\":\"https://github.com/entireio/entire-graph/actions/runs/33823078438/job/100870118695\",\"name\":\"test (windows-latest) / non-heavy packages\",\"startedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:47:16Z\",\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"workflowName\":\"test\"},{\"__typename\":\"CheckRun\",\"completedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:50:57Z\",\"conclusion\":\"SUCCESS\",\"detailsUrl\":\"https://github.com/entireio/entire-graph/actions/runs/33823078438/job/100870834386\",\"name\":\"test (windows-latest) / coverage and integrity\",\"startedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:50:46Z\",\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"workflowName\":\"test\"},{\"__typename\":\"CheckRun\",\"completedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:51:08Z\",\"conclusion\":\"SUCCESS\",\"detailsUrl\":\"https://github.com/entireio/entire-graph/actions/runs/33823078438/job/100870875100\",\"name\":\"test (windows-latest)\",\"startedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:51:05Z\",\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"workflowName\":\"test\"},{\"__typename\":\"CheckRun\",\"completedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:52:03Z\",\"conclusion\":\"FAILURE\",\"detailsUrl\":\"https://entire.io/gh/entireio/entire-graph/trails/142\",\"name\":\"Entire Gates\",\"startedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:45:33Z\",\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"workflowName\":\"\"}],\"title\":\"fix(sem): stop bare type names resolving across language boundaries\",\"updatedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:45:26Z\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/entireio/entire-graph/pull/184\"}\n","stderr":"","aggregated_output":"{\"additions\":4495,\"baseRefOid\":\"9cdc0592352da8987ee727d8e1cfd0bf871ae4a5\",\"changedFiles\":11,\"commits\":[{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-08-29T19:46:52Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"257360244+suhaanthayyil@users.noreply.github.com\",\"id\":\"U_kgDOD1cBdA\",\"login\":\"suhaanthayyil\",\"name\":\"Suhaan Thayyil\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-08-29T19:46:52Z\",\"messageBody\":\"The workspace short-name index used to resolve a bare type name in a\\nsignature is keyed by name alone. resolveTypeReference filtered its\\ncandidates by kind but never by language, so any workspace-unique name\\nbound confidently across a language boundary: an Erlang `-record(point,\\n{x = 0, y = 0}).` became the resolved type of an R function and of a\\nClojure function whose parameter happened to be called `point`\\n(resolution=name_only, scope=module, confidence 0.75). Neither edge is\\npossible - R cannot name an Erlang record.\\n\\nThe invented edge was also unstable. Because the impossible declaration\\ncounted towards ambiguity, adding a second same-named type anywhere in\\nthe corpus silently deleted the edge, and a foreign declaration could\\nsuppress a real, resolvable same-language one.\\n\\n\\\"Same language only\\\" would be the wrong repair: C++ and Objective-C\\nconsume C headers verbatim, Swift imports C and Objective-C declarations\\nthrough the Clang importer, JVM languages name Java classes directly,\\nTypeScript and JavaScript share one type system, and F# names C# types.\\nThose edges exist today and must survive. So the guard is a language\\ncompatibility relation - symmetric, reflexive and deliberately not\\ntransitive, since ClojureScript shares declarations with Clojure and\\nClojure with Java, but ClojureScript cannot name a Java class.\\n\\nFiltering candidates rather than emitted edges also restores the\\nsuppressed same-language edges.\",\"messageHeadline\":\"fix(sem): stop bare type names resolving across language boundaries\",\"oid\":\"21d60e9dd0ad4c36cafffaa2d237197644e5b2a5\"},{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-08-29T20:09:48Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"257360244+suhaanthayyil@users.noreply.github.com\",\"id\":\"U_kgDOD1cBdA\",\"login\":\"suhaanthayyil\",\"name\":\"Suhaan Thayyil\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-08-29T20:09:48Z\",\"messageBody\":\"…ng languages\\n\\nThe first commit filtered resolveTypeReference, because that is where the\\nErlang-record-becomes-an-R-type case surfaced. It left the rest of the same\\ndefect in place: the workspace short-name index is keyed by name alone, and call,\\nconstructor, inheritance and test resolution all read it and pick a target the\\nsame way.\\n\\nMeasured on origin/main over a corpus where Go, Python and Ruby each declare\\ntheir own Point.process, all four CALLS edges resolve by type_inferred and two\\nare wrong:\\n\\n    Go/run     -> Go/Point.process     correct\\n    Kotlin/... -> Java/Shape.area      correct, compatible languages\\n    Python/run -> Go/Point.process     WRONG\\n    Ruby/run   -> Go/Point.process     WRONG\\n\\nPython and Ruby each declare the method the call actually reaches; the resolver\\nchose Go's because the index is keyed by bare name.\\n\\nEvery lookup into that global index now filters candidates through the existing\\nlanguage-compatibility relation. Same-file and same-package candidate lists are\\nleft alone: they cannot cross a language boundary, so filtering them would cost a\\npass for no possible change. Four sites are deliberately NOT filtered because no\\nreferring symbol is in scope to filter against -- resolveQualifiedType,\\ngoMethodSymbolByID, methodReturnTypes, and the extension-member lookup, which\\nalready restricts to an exact language.\\n\\nFiltering candidates rather than emitted edges is what makes this a repair rather\\nthan a deletion. With the impossible declarations out of the candidate set the\\nreal ones resolve, so the edge count is unchanged and the two wrong edges move to\\nthe right target. The compatible Kotlin -> Java edge survives, which a\\nsame-language-only rule would have destroyed.\",\"messageHeadline\":\"fix(sem): stop call, constructor, inheritance and test targets crossi…\",\"oid\":\"b4007ad07515bfdf0054f2fe2cd01ca646912635\"},{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-08-29T21:36:00Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"257360244+suhaanthayyil@users.noreply.github.com\",\"id\":\"U_kgDOD1cBdA\",\"login\":\"suhaanthayyil\",\"name\":\"Suhaan Thayyil\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-08-29T21:36:00Z\",\"messageBody\":\"…he real one\\n\\nThe filter inside resolveCallTargets was reported as unverified when the previous\\ncommit landed: no mutation caught it and no fixture discriminated it. The reason\\nis now established -- it was redundant. All four call sites already wrap their\\nsymbolsByShortName lookup in sharedTypeCandidates, because the referring symbol\\nis known there, so the in-function call was a second application of an\\nidempotent function. Worse, it looked like the guard while the call sites were\\nthe guard.\\n\\nThe path it was meant to protect is real and is now covered. A bare free-function\\ncall with no receiver ends at the globally-unique gate (\\\"direct call expression\\nmatched globally unique symbol name\\\", name_only, 0.68), which fires when exactly\\none symbol in the workspace carries the name -- so a name defined only in a\\nforeign language is globally unique by construction. Unwrapping the bare-name\\ncall site reproduces it exactly:\\n\\n    Python/driver2_py -> Ruby/beta_only   resolution name_only\\n\\nThe earlier attempt to provoke this failed for two reasons worth recording: the\\nfirst fixture defined the name in BOTH languages, so it was never globally\\nunique and the gate never fired; the second put the call and the definition in\\none file, where the same-file branch returns before the global list is consulted.\\nThe name must exist exactly once, in a foreign language, in another file.\\n\\nThe new test discriminates in both directions: a Python-to-Python call across\\nfiles must still resolve (proving the gate is reachable and the filter has not\\ndisabled cross-file resolution), and the Python-to-Ruby call must not.\",\"messageHeadline\":\"fix(sem): drop the redundant in-function candidate filter, and test t…\",\"oid\":\"c43c83c8f8525a9eeceac65dadc709693336933f\"},{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-08-30T15:18:49Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"257360244+suhaanthayyil@users.noreply.github.com\",\"id\":\"U_kgDOD1cBdA\",\"login\":\"suhaanthayyil\",\"name\":\"Suhaan Thayyil\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-08-30T15:18:49Z\",\"messageBody\":\"The language-compatibility groups treat Clojure and ClojureScript as sharing\\ndeclarations, which is true only for portable sources. parser.go maps BOTH .clj\\n(JVM-only) and .cljc (portable) to the language \\\"Clojure\\\", so a pair rule stated\\nin language names cannot tell them apart, and a .cljs consumer bound a\\ndeclaration it can never name. Measured before this change:\\n\\n    ClojureScript(src/app.cljs) -> Clojure(src/jvmonly.clj)    WRONG\\n    ClojureScript(src/app.cljs) -> Clojure(src/portable.cljc)  correct\\n\\nThe refinement reads the declaring file's extension, and only for a\\ncross-language pair: within one language the question does not arise, and a\\n.clj referring to a .clj is exactly what should resolve. It is symmetric in the\\nuseful direction too, since a ClojureScript symbol always comes from .cljs, so a\\nJVM Clojure file no longer binds a ClojureScript-only declaration either.\\n\\nSevering the dialect pair would also have removed the wrong edge, and would have\\ntaken the correct one with it; the test asserts both halves so that shortcut\\nfails.\",\"messageHeadline\":\"fix(sem): let ClojureScript bind only portable Clojure declarations\",\"oid\":\"b941de3be78709dc41706d1a4081c1fc84033561\"},{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-08-30T15:21:21Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"257360244+suhaanthayyil@users.noreply.github.com\",\"id\":\"U_kgDOD1cBdA\",\"login\":\"suhaanthayyil\",\"name\":\"Suhaan Thayyil\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-08-30T15:21:21Z\",\"messageBody\":\"The shared-declaration matrix asserted that ClojureScript binds a type declared\\nin tick.clj. That encodes the defect the previous commit fixes: .clj is JVM-only\\nand a .cljs consumer cannot name it, so the assertion pinned the wrong\\nbehaviour and failed once the refinement landed.\\n\\nThe declaration moves to tick.cljc, the only Clojure source both readers accept.\\nThe assertion is unchanged and still pins what the test is for -- that a\\nlegitimate cross-language declaration edge survives the language filter.\",\"messageHeadline\":\"test(sem): pin the Clojure shared-declaration case to a portable source\",\"oid\":\"ea278b9483581a7c021cb157057051b960fd6ae0\"},{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-09-02T21:13:46Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"257360244+suhaanthayyil@users.noreply.github.com\",\"id\":\"U_kgDOD1cBdA\",\"login\":\"suhaanthayyil\",\"name\":\"Suhaan Thayyil\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-09-02T21:13:46Z\",\"messageBody\":\"Two trail findings on trail 142.\\n\\n01M19M7CBDBX -- the C family was declared as one symmetric group, but its\\ninteroperability runs one way. C++ and Objective-C compile C headers\\nunchanged and Swift imports both through the Clang importer; C has no way to\\nname a C++ class or template, and an Objective-C `.m` cannot name a C++ type\\neither -- only `.mm` can. Listing them symmetrically licensed exactly the\\nimpossible edges this file exists to remove, just in the other direction. The\\nrelation map was already keyed from -> targets, so the fix is a one-way table\\nbeside the symmetric groups rather than a new mechanism.\\n\\nTwo tests encoded the defect and are corrected with it. One asserted\\n`languagesShareTypes(\\\"C\\\", \\\"C++\\\")`; the other's fixture was\\n`void use_shape(Shape s)` in a .c file, which does not compile as C at all.\\nThe legitimate direction stays pinned by C++/renderWidget->C/Widget and\\nSwift/useVec->C/Vec3, both of which still resolve.\\n\\n01M19M6D39B1 -- `.zsh` is labelled Zsh and `.sh` is labelled Bash, and no\\ngroup related them, so `source ./lib.sh; helper` lost its CALLS edge even\\nthough sourcing executes the library's functions in the calling shell. They\\nare now one symmetric pair, which is the correct shape: the naming works in\\nwhichever direction the source is written.\",\"messageHeadline\":\"fix(sem): make C-family type sharing directional, and relate the shells\",\"oid\":\"9f96eb21bbbef668b9f9d1f6c964eabf8cd5abe6\"},{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-09-02T23:14:36Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"257360244+suhaanthayyil@users.noreply.github.com\",\"id\":\"U_kgDOD1cBdA\",\"login\":\"suhaanthayyil\",\"name\":\"Suhaan Thayyil\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-09-02T23:14:36Z\",\"messageBody\":\"…ither dialect\\n\\nTwo follow-up findings on trail 142, both against the previous commit.\\n\\n01M1HZK6P1Z9 -- Swift's direct C++ interoperability imports C++ types and\\nfunctions, so `Swift -> C++` is a real edge the one-way table left out. The\\nreverse stays refused: C++ has no way to name a Swift declaration.\\n\\n01M1HZK6NNTT -- the Clojure refinement decided from the CANDIDATE's extension\\nalone, so every reference out of a portable `.cljc` file to a\\nClojureScript-only declaration was rejected. A reader conditional --\\n`#?(:cljs ...)` -- is exactly how portable code names a dialect-specific\\ndeclaration, and `.cljc` is compiled by both readers. A single-dialect\\nconsumer still needs a portable declaration, which is the half this must not\\nloosen: a `.cljs` reader cannot read `.clj`.\",\"messageHeadline\":\"fix(sem): let Swift reach C++, and let a portable Clojure file name e…\",\"oid\":\"07ff4e21d7659128cd0d8ad3c7c84cd15e40bbfe\"},{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-09-02T23:42:09Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"257360244+suhaanthayyil@users.noreply.github.com\",\"id\":\"U_kgDOD1cBdA\",\"login\":\"suhaanthayyil\",\"name\":\"Suhaan Thayyil\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-09-02T23:42:09Z\",\"messageBody\":\"Trail finding 01M1J6FZ0JZV, against the previous commit. The language label\\nfollows the FILE, not the declaration: a header holding any Objective-C\\nsyntax is labelled Objective-C even where it also declares plain C structs and\\nfunctions, which C++ compiles unchanged. Refusing the pair therefore dropped\\nvalid C++ edges to those declarations wholesale.\\n\\nThe reverse stays refused -- Objective-C `.m` cannot name a C++ type, only\\n`.mm` can, and that pair is already listed.\",\"messageHeadline\":\"fix(sem): let C++ consume Objective-C-labelled declarations\",\"oid\":\"e1ecc4c38be795d1478b796292212819a8239aab\"},{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-09-02T23:48:59Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"257360244+suhaanthayyil@users.noreply.github.com\",\"id\":\"U_kgDOD1cBdA\",\"login\":\"suhaanthayyil\",\"name\":\"Suhaan Thayyil\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-09-02T23:48:59Z\",\"messageBody\":\"Trail finding 01M1J83902SV. An `@objc` Swift class or method is declared in the\\ngenerated `<Module>-Swift.h` header and named directly from Objective-C, so\\nthose type, inheritance, constructor and call edges are real and were being\\ndropped.\\n\\nWith both directions real -- Swift imports Objective-C through the Clang\\nimporter, Objective-C names Swift through the generated header -- the pair is\\nsymmetric rather than one-way, and moves to the symmetric list. The rest of the\\nfamily stays directional: C still cannot name C++, and Objective-C `.m` still\\ncannot name a C++ type.\",\"messageHeadline\":\"fix(sem): let Objective-C name Swift declarations\",\"oid\":\"d3f6fd695f863338dd6e808593f237a7346d3672\"},{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-09-03T02:00:22Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"suhaanthayyil0@gmail.com\",\"id\":\"U_kgDOD1cBdA\",\"login\":\"suhaanthayyil\",\"name\":\"Suhaan Thayyil\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-09-03T02:00:22Z\",\"messageBody\":\"The language-compatibility relation answers one question: may source written in\\nlanguage A name a type DECLARED in language B. Test resolution was swept into\\nthe same filter by analogy with call, constructor and inheritance resolution,\\nbut a TESTS edge names no type. It records a naming convention -- test_frobnicate\\ncovers frobnicate -- and harnesses cross language boundaries as a matter of\\ncourse: pytest over a C extension, a shell script over a compiled binary, JS\\nspecs over a WASM module.\\n\\nMeasured on this branch, a pytest harness beside a C unit produced no TESTS edge\\nat all:\\n\\n    tests/test_frobnicate.py::test_frobnicate -> src/frobnicate.c::frobnicate\\n        dropped, Python cannot name a C type\\n\\nNothing about that binding is ambiguous: frobnicate is workspace-unique among\\nnon-test callables, so resolveTestSubject would have taken it by name_only.\\n\\nThe gate is also asymmetric here in a way that makes no sense for a convention.\\nThe C family relation is directional by design -- C++ and Swift reach C, and C\\nnames nothing -- so a C test could never reach a non-C unit however unambiguous\\nthe subject name, while a C++ test in the same repo could.\\n\\nPrecision for TESTS already comes from resolveTestSubject's evidence order:\\nsame-file, then the test file's import bindings, then a workspace-unique or\\nsame-directory match, and nothing otherwise. That ordering, not type interop, is\\nthe right guard. The other lookups into the workspace short-name index keep the\\nfilter unchanged.\",\"messageHeadline\":\"fix(sem): stop type-sharing from gating TESTS edges\",\"oid\":\"f51de3ec6cbfa2fdc7c793ad392b69db87609959\"},{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-09-03T02:14:45Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"257360244+suhaanthayyil@users.noreply.github.com\",\"id\":\"U_kgDOD1cBdA\",\"login\":\"suhaanthayyil\",\"name\":\"Suhaan Thayyil\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-09-03T02:14:45Z\",\"messageBody\":\"importedReceiverCallRelations filtered its workspace short-name lookup through\\nsharedTypeCandidates before testing the import, so a receiver call that an\\nexplicit import had already bound to a parsed local file was thrown away\\nwhenever the two files were written in different languages.\\n\\nThe language-compatibility relation answers one question: may source in language\\nA name a type DECLARED in language B. This binding is not a type reference. It\\nis an import whose module path resolves to the callee's FILE, which is direct\\nevidence that these two files interoperate, and that outranks a language-pair\\nheuristic. Python/C is deliberately absent from the relation because a Python\\ncaller never names a C struct -- but it does call the extension's exported\\nfunctions.\\n\\nMeasured on this branch, `import frobnicate` beside a parsed `frobnicate.c`:\\n\\n    app.py::run -> external:symbol:frobnicate.compute   (import_external, 0.78)\\n\\ninstead of the internal edge. The identical Python-to-Python fixture resolved\\nto `frobnicate.py::compute` at import_resolved/0.84, so the language pair was\\nthe only difference.\\n\\nImport evidence outranks the heuristic only while it is unambiguous. Module\\npaths are matched by suffix, so one import can match same-named callables in\\nseveral languages at once, and nothing then says which one it bound; emitting\\nthe fanout or picking a winner would both invent an FFI edge. The type-sharing\\ncandidates are therefore still preferred whenever they resolve anything -- every\\npreviously resolved call, same-language fanout included, is unchanged -- and the\\nunfiltered set is consulted only when it names exactly one target. The change is\\nstrictly additive: it can only resolve calls that previously degraded to an\\nunresolved external target.\",\"messageHeadline\":\"fix(sem): let import evidence outrank type-sharing for receiver calls\",\"oid\":\"ccbb244db3b74efee35eb1ec26a643fd243d4ab4\"},{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-09-03T20:04:08Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"thomas@dohmke.de\",\"id\":\"MDQ6VXNlcjcwNzIw\",\"login\":\"ashtom\",\"name\":\"Thomas Dohmke\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-09-03T20:04:08Z\",\"messageBody\":\"\",\"messageHeadline\":\"Merge origin/main into fix/cross-language-name-only-edges\",\"oid\":\"f7ca6ec9c73cce0c54b371f5e91b40cc84733ed4\"},{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-09-03T20:25:06Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"thomas@dohmke.de\",\"id\":\"MDQ6VXNlcjcwNzIw\",\"login\":\"ashtom\",\"name\":\"Thomas Dohmke\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-09-03T20:25:06Z\",\"messageBody\":\"\",\"messageHeadline\":\"fix: filter receiver type candidates by language\",\"oid\":\"b8aace015c59224944bd9d9b8927958154403bbb\"},{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-09-03T20:40:05Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"257360244+suhaanthayyil@users.noreply.github.com\",\"id\":\"U_kgDOD1cBdA\",\"login\":\"suhaanthayyil\",\"name\":\"Suhaan Thayyil\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-09-03T20:40:05Z\",\"messageBody\":\"`import frobnicate; frobnicate.compute(1)` resolves to a locally parsed\\nfrobnicate.c, but `from frobnicate import compute; compute(1)` did not.\\nresolveCallTargets received candidates already narrowed by the\\nlanguage-compatibility relation, so the C declaration was gone before the\\nimport tier could compare its module path, and the call degraded to an\\nunresolved external target.\\n\\nThat relation answers whether source in one language may name a type\\nDECLARED in another. An explicit import whose module path resolves to the\\ncallee's FILE is direct evidence that the two files interoperate, which\\noutranks it -- the reason the qualified spelling of the very same call\\nalready resolves in importedReceiverCallTargets.\\n\\nresolveCallTargets now narrows internally and keeps both sets. The import\\ntier prefers the compatible candidates, so every previously resolved call\\nis unchanged, and consults the wider set only when it names exactly one\\ntarget: module paths are matched by suffix, so one import can match\\nsame-named callables in several languages at once, and nothing then says\\nwhich of them it bound.\",\"messageHeadline\":\"fix(sem): let import evidence outrank the language filter for bare calls\",\"oid\":\"fb89aeb5c4dd26b13d1e08c6d2b4542efb96345c\"},{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-09-03T20:58:04Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"257360244+suhaanthayyil@users.noreply.github.com\",\"id\":\"U_kgDOD1cBdA\",\"login\":\"suhaanthayyil\",\"name\":\"Suhaan Thayyil\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-09-03T20:58:04Z\",\"messageBody\":\"The bare-call import tiers read an explicit import as evidence that\\noutranks the language-compatibility relation. That makes Python's scoping\\nload-bearing where it used to be cosmetic: a name the caller's own body\\nbinds is now resolved into a CONFIDENT cross-language edge instead of a\\nmerely unresolved one.\\n\\n    from frobnicate import compute   # frobnicate.c is parsed\\n\\n    def run(compute):\\n        return compute(1)            # calls the PARAMETER\\n\\nbound run to frobnicate.c's compute, and so did a body that rebinds the\\nname from a callback (`compute = callback`). A function-local name is\\nlocal for the whole body in Python, so at every call site in that body it\\nis the binding, not the same-named file-level or imported callable, and no\\nresolution tier may bind it to one.\\n\\nresolveCallTargets now declines a name the caller supplies as locally\\nbound, which leaves the call to the imported-external fallback exactly as\\nit degraded before the import tiers could reach past the language filter.\\npythonLocalBindingNames reads the binding forms out of one callable body:\\nparameters (including lambda's), assignment, tuple, annotated, augmented\\nand walrus targets, loop and context variables, exception aliases, and\\nnested definitions -- deliberately conservative toward suppression, since\\na name used as a parameter anywhere inside one callable is ambiguous\\nevidence at best.\\n\\nThe set is supplied only for callable bodies. Module scope is not a\\nshadowing scope: a `def` there binds the very symbol a top-level call is\\nmeant to reach, so the file-level scan passes none and keeps resolving\\nthrough the same import evidence.\",\"messageHeadline\":\"fix(sem): a locally bound Python name is not the imported callable\",\"oid\":\"3ef7deae1a7a54f257b8c8dc7f11f6c8cb7ebae1\"},{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-09-03T21:19:07Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"257360244+suhaanthayyil@users.noreply.github.com\",\"id\":\"U_kgDOD1cBdA\",\"login\":\"suhaanthayyil\",\"name\":\"Suhaan Thayyil\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-09-03T21:19:07Z\",\"messageBody\":\"pythonLocalBindingNames scanned one callable's whole block flat, so every\\nbinding form it found became a body-wide shadow. A `lambda`'s parameters,\\na comprehension's loop variables, and a nested `def`'s parameters and\\nlocals are none of those: they bind inside the scope that declares them.\\n\\n    from frobnicate import compute\\n\\n    def run(source):\\n        handler = lambda compute: compute(1)\\n        return compute(source)   # calls the IMPORT, not the parameter\\n\\nThe direction of that error is the expensive one. resolveCallTargets\\ndeclines a bound name before any resolution tier runs, so a name wrongly\\nreported as bound does not merely soften an edge -- it deletes it. The\\nfixture above lost its import-resolved edge to frobnicate.c and degraded\\nto an unresolved external target; a same-file callee lost its edge\\noutright, as did a body holding a nested `def` that names a parameter\\nafter it. `global`/`nonlocal` declare the very opposite of a local\\nbinding and were read as one too, suppressing calls to the module-level\\nsymbol the declaration points at.\\n\\nThe scan is now per-scope: nested `def`/`class` bodies (by indentation),\\nlambdas (to the end of the expression holding them), and comprehensions\\n(Python 3 scopes their variables to the brackets) are cut out of the\\ntext before the binding forms are read, and each is scanned as a scope of\\nits own. A nested definition still binds its own NAME in the enclosing\\nscope, and a name an inner scope binds still joins the set when every\\noccurrence of it in the body is inside that scope -- the one case where a\\nsingle flat set answers correctly for every call site, and the case that\\nkeeps a lambda's parameter shadowing the import for the calls in its own\\nbody. When the uses straddle the boundary the name is left out, because\\nresolving one call too many costs a softer edge while suppressing one\\ndeletes a real one.\\n\\nNames declared `global` or `nonlocal` are removed from the set: those\\nstatements say the name is the module's or the enclosing function's,\\nwhich is the symbol a call by that name is meant to reach.\",\"messageHeadline\":\"fix(sem): a nested scope's Python bindings are not the enclosing body's\",\"oid\":\"d363e75a2a81786482a6d2bb43baf7a55deedb80\"},{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-09-03T21:38:03Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"257360244+suhaanthayyil@users.noreply.github.com\",\"id\":\"U_kgDOD1cBdA\",\"login\":\"suhaanthayyil\",\"name\":\"Suhaan Thayyil\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-09-03T21:38:03Z\",\"messageBody\":\"Two of the one-way edges in typeSharingLanguageEdges are true of the\\nlanguage PAIR but not of every declaration under it, and the candidate\\nfilter read only the pair.\\n\\nC++ -> Objective-C exists because the Objective-C label follows the FILE:\\na header holding any Objective-C syntax is labelled Objective-C even\\nwhere it also declares the plain C structs and functions a `.cpp`\\ncompiles unchanged, and refusing the pair dropped those edges wholesale.\\nIt does not follow that a `.cpp` can name an `@interface`, an `@protocol`\\nor a selector. Measured over a fixture with an `@interface Shape` header\\nand an `@implementation` method:\\n\\n    C++/renderShape  -> Objective-C/Shape        impossible\\n    C++/callIt       -> Objective-C/computeArea  impossible (message send)\\n    C++/renderWidget -> Objective-C/Widget       correct (plain C struct)\\n\\nObjective-C -> Swift exists because of the generated `<Module>-Swift.h`\\nheader, which holds only what Swift exposes to the Objective-C runtime --\\nfar less than the module. Binding by bare name offered it all of it:\\n\\n    Objective-C/useLedger  -> Swift/Ledger    a struct is never bridged\\n    Objective-C/useEngine  -> Swift/Engine    a pure-Swift root class\\n    Objective-C/go         -> Swift/freeFunc  a global func is not bridged\\n    Objective-C/useBridged -> Swift/Bridged   correct (@objc, NSObject)\\n\\nThe relation itself is unchanged -- no edge is removed and nothing is\\nflattened or made symmetric. The refinement is at the CANDIDATE level,\\nwhere candidateSharesDeclarations already refines the Clojure dialects\\nthat one language name cannot separate: for C++ the Objective-C-only\\ndeclaration forms are dropped, and for Objective-C a Swift declaration is\\nkept only when it could appear in the generated header, decided from the\\nattribute and inheritance clause the extractor keeps in its signature.\\n\\nOnly what the language rules make impossible is refused. A declaration\\nwhose exposure cannot be decided from its own signature -- a member,\\nwhose exposure follows its container; a subclass whose chain to NSObject\\nruns through classes a candidate-level check cannot follow; a signature\\nthe parser could not read -- is kept, because dropping a candidate here\\ndeletes the edge rather than merely adding one. Both tests assert the\\nlegitimate edge of the pair still resolves beside the impossible ones.\",\"messageHeadline\":\"fix(sem): the C-family language pairs are not declaration-blind\",\"oid\":\"904e1097608ef454f245aee82698a8c1be959742\"},{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-09-03T21:38:17Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"257360244+suhaanthayyil@users.noreply.github.com\",\"id\":\"U_kgDOD1cBdA\",\"login\":\"suhaanthayyil\",\"name\":\"Suhaan Thayyil\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-09-03T21:38:17Z\",\"messageBody\":\"The import-name map the call resolver reads is built for a whole FILE,\\nbut Python scopes an `import` inside a function to that function. The map\\ntherefore offered a function-local binding to every other function in the\\nfile, and because the bare-call tiers read an explicit import as evidence\\nthat OUTRANKS the language-compatibility relation, the result was not a\\nweak edge but a confident cross-language one:\\n\\n    def uses_import():\\n        from frobnicate import compute   # frobnicate.c is parsed\\n        return compute(1)\\n\\n    def unrelated():\\n        return compute(3)                # NameError at runtime\\n\\n    Python/unrelated -> C/compute   res=import_resolved conf=0.86\\n\\nNames bound only by a nested-scope import are now collected with the\\nscopes they are visible in, and each symbol resolves against the imports\\nit can actually see. A symbol sees such an import two ways: the statement\\nis inside its own block, or the scope holding the statement encloses it --\\na nested `def` really does see the import its enclosing function made, so\\nconfining the import to its own function alone would have deleted that\\ncall instead of the unrelated one.\\n\\nNothing else narrows. A name any module-level import also binds is left\\nout of the map entirely, and so is an indented import that no\\n`def`/`class` encloses (the `if TYPE_CHECKING:` idiom), because both are\\nvisible file-wide already; for a file whose imports are all module-level\\nthe map is empty and the resolver is handed the original map unchanged.\\nThe async-call and data-flow paths resolve through the same visible map,\\nso the mirrored DATA_FLOWS edge goes with the call that justified it.\",\"messageHeadline\":\"fix(sem): a Python import inside one function is not the file's\",\"oid\":\"7a185edb2ef9f78477339f53dd67658cb553bea2\"},{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-09-03T21:58:32Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"thomas@dohmke.de\",\"id\":\"MDQ6VXNlcjcwNzIw\",\"login\":\"ashtom\",\"name\":\"Thomas Dohmke\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-09-03T21:58:32Z\",\"messageBody\":\"\",\"messageHeadline\":\"fix: preserve scoped bare FFI imports\",\"oid\":\"d969441dccd88300126db7f349cb11bee583dddf\"},{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-09-03T22:10:19Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"257360244+suhaanthayyil@users.noreply.github.com\",\"id\":\"U_kgDOD1cBdA\",\"login\":\"suhaanthayyil\",\"name\":\"Suhaan Thayyil\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-09-03T22:10:19Z\",\"messageBody\":\"The scope pass feeds resolveCallTargets, which DECLINES a bound name\\nbefore any resolution tier runs, so a binding it reports that Python does\\nnot make deletes a real call edge outright. Three shapes reported one:\\n\\n  - A line inside brackets is not a statement. `schedule(\\\\n compute=1,\\\\n)`\\n    put a keyword ARGUMENT at the start of a line, where the assignment\\n    scanner read it as a binding, and brackets that never close -- a parse\\n    failure, which the pass took for a clean result -- left a\\n    comprehension unrecognised so its `for` target was read as the body's.\\n    Bracket depth is now counted on the scope's own text and the statement\\n    scanners read only what is at depth zero, so unbalanced source reports\\n    fewer bindings rather than invented ones.\\n\\n  - A name bound in one nested scope was tested for use against the\\n    enclosing body's text alone, which has EVERY nested scope masked out\\n    of it. `key=lambda compute: compute` beside `[compute(v) for v in\\n    values]` therefore looked body-wide and deleted the comprehension's\\n    call. A use in a sibling nested scope now counts as a use.\\n\\n  - PEP 572: an assignment expression inside a comprehension binds in the\\n    scope that CONTAINS the comprehension, unlike the loop variable beside\\n    it. Its targets are hoisted out, through nested comprehensions but not\\n    through a lambda or a def, where a walrus stops.\\n\\nVerified against 247 files of the CPython 3.12 standard library: four\\ncall and data-flow edges restored, none removed.\",\"messageHeadline\":\"fix(sem): a Python binding must come from a statement of that scope\",\"oid\":\"34ccacc726199eb11eba3ee4f565c75c7d29a00c\"},{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-09-03T22:14:12Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"257360244+suhaanthayyil@users.noreply.github.com\",\"id\":\"U_kgDOD1cBdA\",\"login\":\"suhaanthayyil\",\"name\":\"Suhaan Thayyil\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-09-03T22:14:12Z\",\"messageBody\":\"Confining a function-local import cuts both ways: a name hidden from a\\nsymbol that CAN see it loses the import evidence its call edge rests on,\\nso a wrong confinement deletes an edge, while a name left visible where\\nPython would not see it binds a call the caller never reaches.\\n\\n  - `def _load(): global np; import numpy as np` is a real idiom. The\\n    `global` declares that the import binds the MODULE's name, so the rest\\n    of the file does see it; confining it to _load deleted every other\\n    function's edge. A name declared global in the scope that imports it\\n    is now module-level. `nonlocal` is the bounded half of the same rule:\\n    the scope widens to the enclosing function, not to the whole file.\\n\\n  - A class body is not a lexical scope for the functions defined in it --\\n    Python skips class scope when resolving a name inside a method, so a\\n    method naming a class-body import raises NameError. Treating the class\\n    as an enclosing scope bound that call, at import_resolved confidence\\n    and across a language boundary, to a function it cannot reach.\\n\\nA nested `def` still sees the import its enclosing FUNCTION made, and a\\nmodule-level import is still visible everywhere; both are fenced by tests.\",\"messageHeadline\":\"fix(sem): a Python import's scope is the one Python gives it\",\"oid\":\"0975a3f2920316143736341b090d5c62f9a9ee5e\"},{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-09-03T22:14:50Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"thomas@dohmke.de\",\"id\":\"MDQ6VXNlcjcwNzIw\",\"login\":\"ashtom\",\"name\":\"Thomas Dohmke\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-09-03T22:14:50Z\",\"messageBody\":\"\",\"messageHeadline\":\"merge: reconcile PR 184 review fixes\",\"oid\":\"c42703c6200ab201cb427563edcf50d1b0ecc79d\"},{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-09-03T22:18:19Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"thomas@dohmke.de\",\"id\":\"MDQ6VXNlcjcwNzIw\",\"login\":\"ashtom\",\"name\":\"Thomas Dohmke\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-09-03T22:18:19Z\",\"messageBody\":\"\",\"messageHeadline\":\"merge: retain reviewed PR 184 implementation\",\"oid\":\"f1198b6293f59481ea9e191d1e8f2ab76de804be\"},{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-09-03T22:32:39Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"thomas@dohmke.de\",\"id\":\"MDQ6VXNlcjcwNzIw\",\"login\":\"ashtom\",\"name\":\"Thomas Dohmke\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-09-03T22:32:39Z\",\"messageBody\":\"\",\"messageHeadline\":\"fix: retain resolved scoped Python imports\",\"oid\":\"982cfb5c33a736ec1f489d906006dd6be44ec206\"},{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-09-03T22:43:48Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"257360244+suhaanthayyil@users.noreply.github.com\",\"id\":\"U_kgDOD1cBdA\",\"login\":\"suhaanthayyil\",\"name\":\"Suhaan Thayyil\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-09-03T22:43:48Z\",\"messageBody\":\"`from mod import compute as c` binds `c` to mod's `compute`, but every\\nresolution tier looks the call name up in the workspace short-name index\\nand `c` is defined nowhere. The resolved edge was lost and replaced by an\\nexternal target named `mod.c` -- a symbol that exists in no language,\\nwhich is strictly worse than the missing edge it stood in for.\\n\\nThe alias is now carried alongside its original: the import parser records\\nthe member name each local name is bound to, and a bare call through an\\nalias resolves under that member through exactly the two import tiers the\\nun-aliased spelling already uses. Same target, resolution, scope and\\nconfidence; the same ambiguity suppression, so two same-named units behind\\none alias still produce no local edge. Same-file and workspace-wide name\\ntiers stay out of it: the alias defines nothing, so a match there would be\\na coincidence of spelling.\\n\\nTwo guards keep the fix from inventing edges of its own. An alias bound to\\ntwo different members, or one that also names a module, resolves to\\nnothing rather than to a guess; and a name that only ever binds a MODULE\\n(`import mod`, `import mod as m`) emits no bare-call external edge at all,\\nbecause a module object is not callable and `mod.m` names nothing.\",\"messageHeadline\":\"fix(sem): resolve a Python import alias to the member it renames\",\"oid\":\"bcab954997b535d4760fdbc70570a05c46f778b6\"},{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-09-03T22:59:49Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"257360244+suhaanthayyil@users.noreply.github.com\",\"id\":\"U_kgDOD1cBdA\",\"login\":\"suhaanthayyil\",\"name\":\"Suhaan Thayyil\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-09-03T22:59:49Z\",\"messageBody\":\"A call in a Python function signature -- a default argument, a parameter\\nannotation, a return annotation -- is evaluated where the `def` statement\\nruns, in the enclosing scope. The scope walker descended only into the\\nfunction body, so such a call was recorded in no scope at all. The call\\nscan still attributes it to the function whose signature line holds it,\\nand a `complete` scope view reporting no modules for that name makes\\nimportsWithName DELETE the file-level import binding, so the imported\\ncall loses its resolution tier entirely: a Python-to-C `compute()` in a\\ndefault argument emitted no CALLS edge at all. Walk the signature nodes\\nagainst the enclosing bindings, published under the function's owner.\\nDecorators already worked -- tree-sitter hangs them off\\n`decorated_definition`, outside the function node.\\n\\n`from mod import (compute,)` splits on commas into `(compute` and `)`,\\nso the grouping parentheses were parsed as part of the bound name,\\nbinding a name no call can match while the scope stayed complete. Trim\\nthem in parsePythonImportItem, which the three line-oriented import\\nscanners share; the multi-line form, which those scanners cannot see\\npast the first line of, is restored by the AST-derived scope binding.\",\"messageHeadline\":\"fix(sem): resolve Python signature calls and parenthesised imports\",\"oid\":\"6c91abc64935609ca0061bba5c2ff580f46f9dde\"},{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-09-03T23:10:35Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"257360244+suhaanthayyil@users.noreply.github.com\",\"id\":\"U_kgDOD1cBdA\",\"login\":\"suhaanthayyil\",\"name\":\"Suhaan Thayyil\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-09-03T23:10:35Z\",\"messageBody\":\"A `.h` is labelled Objective-C the moment it contains one `#import` or\\none `@interface`, and such headers go on declaring the plain C structs,\\ntypedefs and functions a `.c` includes and compiles verbatim. C named\\nnothing in typeSharingLanguageEdges, so every one of those targets was\\nfiltered out of the candidate list.\\n\\nMeasured over a fixture whose `.c` includes an Objective-C-labelled\\nheader and an otherwise identical plain-C control:\\n\\n\\tC/renderGadget -> C/Gadget               resolved\\n\\tC/renderWidget -> Objective-C/Widget     DROPPED (same declaration)\\n\\tC/callWidth    -> Objective-C/widgetWidth DROPPED\\n\\nAdd the directional `C -> Objective-C` edge and hold it to the same\\nkind refinement the C++ edge already carries: an `@interface`, an\\n`@protocol` and a selector stay unreachable from C, which cannot even\\ndeclare them. The edge is one-way at the family level -- C still cannot\\nname a C++ class or template, nor a Swift declaration.\\n\\nTestLanguagesShareTypesRelation pinned `C -> Objective-C` as\\nimpossible; that assertion was the bug, so it moves to the symmetric\\nblock with the reason the pair is symmetric at the language level and\\nstill narrowed by kind at the candidate level.\",\"messageHeadline\":\"fix(sem): let C name the plain C half of an Objective-C header\",\"oid\":\"071afb519d8c552fac49062d531300f2e36de18a\"},{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-09-03T23:41:30Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"257360244+suhaanthayyil@users.noreply.github.com\",\"id\":\"U_kgDOD1cBdA\",\"login\":\"suhaanthayyil\",\"name\":\"Suhaan Thayyil\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-09-03T23:41:30Z\",\"messageBody\":\"A `.h` is labelled C++ the moment it holds `extern \\\"C\\\"` or a bare `::`\\n(looksLikeCPlusPlusHeader), so the dual-use header -- the one written\\nprecisely SO a `.c` can include it -- is C++, and C names no C++ target in\\ntypeSharingLanguageEdges. A `.c` including such a header reached nothing in\\nit.\\n\\nTwo defects stacked. The first sits upstream of any language rule: an\\n`extern \\\"C\\\" {` line was blanked out of the source before the C++ grammar saw\\nit, and the blanking ran forward until a line beginning `}  // extern \\\"C\\\"`\\n-- or, failing that, to END OF FILE. The canonical dual-use header closes\\nwith a bare `}` under `#ifdef __cplusplus`, so the whole header was erased\\nand contributed a single macro symbol. tree-sitter-cpp parses\\n`linkage_specification` natively, and the fixture the mask was written for\\n(TestTreeSitterParserCPlusPlusMasksDoctestMacros) parses and yields entities\\nwithout it, so the mask is removed rather than bounded.\\n\\nThe second is the reachability rule. Widening the language pair is not the\\nrepair: unlike the C -> Objective-C case, whose offending kinds are the\\nclosed set objectiveCOnlyDeclaration already names, C++ exposes templates,\\noverloads, namespaces and classes a C source genuinely cannot name, and\\n`C -> C++` wholesale would trade a missing-edge bug for a large class of\\nwrong ones. typeSharingLanguageEdges is therefore untouched --\\nlanguagesShareTypes(\\\"C\\\", \\\"C++\\\") stays false -- and the widening is per\\nDECLARATION instead: declaredWithCLinkage marks what sits inside an\\n`extern \\\"C\\\" { ... }` block, which is the construct a C++ header uses to say\\nexactly which declarations have C linkage, and candidateSharesDeclarations\\noffers C those and only those. `extern \\\"C++\\\"` is not matched.\\n\\nMeasured over a fixture whose `.c` includes a dual-use header and an\\notherwise identical plain-C control -- the same two declarations, differing\\nonly in the header that holds them:\\n\\n\\tC/renderGadget -> C/Gadget            resolved (control)\\n\\tC/renderWidget -> C++/Widget          DROPPED  (declared extern \\\"C\\\")\\n\\tC/callWidth    -> C++/widgetWidth     DROPPED  (declared extern \\\"C\\\")\\n\\nresolveTypeReference re-asked languagesShareTypes about a candidate list\\nsharedTypeCandidates had already filtered, and that duplicate silently\\noverrode the finer per-declaration answer: with the rule in place the CALLS\\nedge resolved while USES_TYPE and PARAM_TYPE on the struct beside it still\\ndid not. The redundant conjunct is dropped.\\n\\nThe narrowing is pinned beside the widening: a class, a namespaced function\\nand an overload set in the SAME header stay unreachable from C.\",\"messageHeadline\":\"fix(sem): let C name the extern \\\"C\\\" half of a dual-use C++ header\",\"oid\":\"a09defd6cd761a39199e3b3ca6d47c24e5581e6c\"},{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-09-04T00:04:29Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"257360244+suhaanthayyil@users.noreply.github.com\",\"id\":\"U_kgDOD1cBdA\",\"login\":\"suhaanthayyil\",\"name\":\"Suhaan Thayyil\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-09-04T00:04:29Z\",\"messageBody\":\"tree-sitter gives `except_clause` no fields: its exception expression, its\\n`as` target and its handler block are plain named children. The scope walker\\nasked it for a \\\"body\\\" field, got nothing, and returned -- so no call anywhere\\nin a handler was ever recorded. The function-wide local pass then asked the\\nsame clause for a \\\"name\\\" field, got its last named child (the handler BLOCK)\\ninstead, and recursed into it, declaring every name written anywhere in the\\nhandler a local of the whole function. That deleted the import-resolved edge\\nfor calls in the `try` body too, which the walker had recorded correctly.\\n`with_item` has no alias field either -- the alias hangs off a nested\\n`as_pattern` -- so the same mistake made `with compute() as handle:` report\\nits own import shadowed.\\n\\nTake the alias from the `as_pattern` that actually carries it, let the\\ngeneric child walk reach the exception expression and the handler body like\\nany other statement, and bind an exception group's `as` target as well.\\n\\nA comprehension's OUTERMOST iterable is evaluated where the comprehension is\\nwritten and passed into the comprehension's frame; only later iterables run\\ninside it. Evaluating all of them inside it made\\n`[x for x in compute() for compute in ys]` -- which CPython runs happily --\\nreport its import shadowed by a target bound afterwards. The interleaved\\nfilter `[x for x in xs if compute() for compute in ys]` stays suppressed:\\nCPython raises UnboundLocalError there, so that name really is the\\ncomprehension's own local.\\n\\nFinally, the scanners that record WHAT a local name is bound to are line\\noriented, so a parenthesised from-import spanning lines showed them only\\n`from mod import (`, an item list of one bare paren. No member name was\\nrecorded behind an alias imported that way, and the resolver -- which needs\\nthe member name, because the workspace index is keyed by it -- had nothing to\\nresolve, while the AST walker reported the call unshadowed. Rejoin such a\\nlist into one logical line, bounded, and consume nothing when it never\\ncloses, so a stray paren cannot hide the imports below it.\\n\\n`TestPythonBareImportFFIRespectsASTScopes` listed `except_type` among the\\nshadowed cases, but nothing in it rebinds the name: an `except <expr>:`\\nclause has no `as` target at all. It only looked shadowed because the clause\\nwas skipped, so it moves to the unshadowed list it belongs in.\",\"messageHeadline\":\"fix(sem): a Python try statement is code of its own scope\",\"oid\":\"20d89c019292b6ee7fa0ac8faa6187e81b8d9bd5\"},{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-09-04T00:36:10Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"257360244+suhaanthayyil@users.noreply.github.com\",\"id\":\"U_kgDOD1cBdA\",\"login\":\"suhaanthayyil\",\"name\":\"Suhaan Thayyil\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-09-04T00:36:10Z\",\"messageBody\":\"Python rebinds a `def` or `class` name only once the whole statement has\\nrun, so the parts it evaluates on the way there still read the enclosing\\nbinding. CPython is the oracle:\\n\\n    def compute(): return \\\"OUTER\\\"\\n    def compute(v=compute()): return v   # -> OUTER\\n    def compute(v=compute()): ...        # -> NameError with no outer name\\n\\nThe scope walker bound the name at the `def`/`class` line, so every\\neagerly evaluated part of the statement -- defaults, annotations, bases,\\nclass-body statements, the signatures of the methods a class defines --\\nwas told the name was already rebound. For an imported name that is the\\nfail-closed direction: the view reports no modules, importsWithName drops\\nthe file-level import binding and the resolved edge goes with it.\\n\\nA `def` now binds at its body, which keeps recursion resolving to the\\nfunction itself. A class body interleaves code that runs immediately with\\nmethod bodies that run later, which one offset cannot express, so the\\nclass keeps its binding at the `class` line for deferred code and hands\\nits bases and body a view where that one binding is hidden.\\n\\nNo emitted relation changes today: a definition also defines a same-named\\nsymbol, which wins the same-file tier (or a CONSTRUCTS relation) before\\nimports are consulted, and the generic loop skips a call whose name equals\\nits owner's. The scope view is the observable surface and is where the\\nregression tests assert.\",\"messageHeadline\":\"fix(sem): a Python definition binds its own name after its signature\",\"oid\":\"0ac5865624a1c724568c6840b69f499055152db1\"},{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-09-04T00:45:24Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"257360244+suhaanthayyil@users.noreply.github.com\",\"id\":\"U_kgDOD1cBdA\",\"login\":\"suhaanthayyil\",\"name\":\"Suhaan Thayyil\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-09-04T00:45:24Z\",\"messageBody\":\"…header\\n\\nA dual-use header -- `#ifdef __cplusplus` / `extern \\\"C\\\" {` -- is labelled C++\\nbecause looksLikeCPlusPlusHeader fires on that very marker. The C-linkage\\nexception in candidateSharesDeclarations admitted only C consumers, so an\\nObjective-C `.m` importing such a header reached nothing in it, even though\\n`__cplusplus` is undefined under Objective-C compilation and the preprocessor\\ndeletes the guards outright, leaving plain C the `.m` compiles verbatim.\\n\\nMeasured over a fixture whose `.m` imports such a header, with an otherwise\\nidentical plain-C header as the control:\\n\\n\\tObjective-C/renderGadget -> C/Gadget          resolved (control)\\n\\tObjective-C/renderWidget -> C++/Widget        DROPPED  (same declaration)\\n\\tObjective-C/callWidth    -> C++/widgetWidth   DROPPED  (same declaration)\\n\\nThe widening stays per DECLARATION, not per language: `Objective-C -> C++` is\\nstill absent from typeSharingLanguageEdges, and only what the header itself put\\nunder C linkage is offered. The class, the overload set and the namespaced\\nfunction in the same header keep cLinkage false and stay filtered out.\",\"messageHeadline\":\"fix(sem): let Objective-C name the extern \\\"C\\\" half of a dual-use C++ …\",\"oid\":\"cd7177e415137e9863238735282b07e7619974f5\"}],\"deletions\":102,\"headRefName\":\"fix/cross-language-name-only-edges\",\"headRefOid\":\"cd7177e415137e9863238735282b07e7619974f5\",\"isDraft\":false,\"mergeStateStatus\":\"UNSTABLE\",\"mergeable\":\"MERGEABLE\",\"number\":184,\"state\":\"OPEN\",\"statusCheckRollup\":[{\"__typename\":\"CheckRun\",\"completedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:45:52Z\",\"conclusion\":\"SUCCESS\",\"detailsUrl\":\"https://github.com/entireio/entire-graph/actions/runs/33823078438/job/100869770015\",\"name\":\"lint\",\"startedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:45:34Z\",\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"workflowName\":\"test\"},{\"__typename\":\"CheckRun\",\"completedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:45:53Z\",\"conclusion\":\"SUCCESS\",\"detailsUrl\":\"https://github.com/entireio/entire-graph/actions/runs/33823078438/job/100869769753\",\"name\":\"build (ubuntu-latest)\",\"startedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:45:32Z\",\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"workflowName\":\"test\"},{\"__typename\":\"CheckRun\",\"completedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:45:54Z\",\"conclusion\":\"SUCCESS\",\"detailsUrl\":\"https://github.com/entireio/entire-graph/actions/runs/33823078438/job/100869769874\",\"name\":\"build (macos-latest)\",\"startedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:45:33Z\",\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"workflowName\":\"test\"},{\"__typename\":\"CheckRun\",\"completedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:46:35Z\",\"conclusion\":\"SUCCESS\",\"detailsUrl\":\"https://github.com/entireio/entire-graph/actions/runs/33823078438/job/100869769794\",\"name\":\"build (windows-latest)\",\"startedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:45:33Z\",\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"workflowName\":\"test\"},{\"__typename\":\"CheckRun\",\"completedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:48:13Z\",\"conclusion\":\"SUCCESS\",\"detailsUrl\":\"https://github.com/entireio/entire-graph/actions/runs/33823078438/job/100869769749\",\"name\":\"test (ubuntu-latest)\",\"startedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:45:32Z\",\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"workflowName\":\"test\"},{\"__typename\":\"CheckRun\",\"completedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:52:00Z\",\"conclusion\":\"SUCCESS\",\"detailsUrl\":\"https://github.com/entireio/entire-graph/actions/runs/33823078438/job/100869769885\",\"name\":\"test (macos-latest)\",\"startedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:45:34Z\",\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"workflowName\":\"test\"},{\"__typename\":\"CheckRun\",\"completedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:47:14Z\",\"conclusion\":\"SUCCESS\",\"detailsUrl\":\"https://github.com/entireio/entire-graph/actions/runs/33823078438/job/100869769891\",\"name\":\"test (windows-latest) / compile and plan\",\"startedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:45:52Z\",\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"workflowName\":\"test\"},{\"__typename\":\"CheckRun\",\"completedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:46:42Z\",\"conclusion\":\"SUCCESS\",\"detailsUrl\":\"https://github.com/entireio/entire-graph/actions/runs/33823078438/job/100869770012\",\"name\":\"test (windows-latest) / vet\",\"startedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:45:33Z\",\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"workflowName\":\"test\"},{\"__typename\":\"CheckRun\",\"completedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:45:44Z\",\"conclusion\":\"SUCCESS\",\"detailsUrl\":\"https://github.com/entireio/entire-graph/actions/runs/33823078438/job/100869769847\",\"name\":\"bm25-adapter\",\"startedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:45:33Z\",\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"workflowName\":\"test\"},{\"__typename\":\"CheckRun\",\"completedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:45:55Z\",\"conclusion\":\"SUCCESS\",\"detailsUrl\":\"https://github.com/entireio/entire-graph/actions/runs/33823078438/job/100869769852\",\"name\":\"statusline (ubuntu-latest)\",\"startedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:45:33Z\",\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"workflowName\":\"test\"},{\"__typename\":\"CheckRun\",\"completedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:46:17Z\",\"conclusion\":\"SUCCESS\",\"detailsUrl\":\"https://github.com/entireio/entire-graph/actions/runs/33823078438/job/100869769632\",\"name\":\"statusline (macos-latest)\",\"startedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:45:33Z\",\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"workflowName\":\"test\"},{\"__typename\":\"CheckRun\",\"completedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:49:30Z\",\"conclusion\":\"SUCCESS\",\"detailsUrl\":\"https://github.com/entireio/entire-graph/actions/runs/33823078438/job/100870118749\",\"name\":\"test (windows-latest) / shard 0\",\"startedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:47:16Z\",\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"workflowName\":\"test\"},{\"__typename\":\"CheckRun\",\"completedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:49:54Z\",\"conclusion\":\"SUCCESS\",\"detailsUrl\":\"https://github.com/entireio/entire-graph/actions/runs/33823078438/job/100870118803\",\"name\":\"test (windows-latest) / shard 1\",\"startedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:47:16Z\",\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"workflowName\":\"test\"},{\"__typename\":\"CheckRun\",\"completedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:50:44Z\",\"conclusion\":\"SUCCESS\",\"detailsUrl\":\"https://github.com/entireio/entire-graph/actions/runs/33823078438/job/100870118706\",\"name\":\"test (windows-latest) / shard 2\",\"startedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:47:16Z\",\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"workflowName\":\"test\"},{\"__typename\":\"CheckRun\",\"completedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:49:34Z\",\"conclusion\":\"SUCCESS\",\"detailsUrl\":\"https://github.com/entireio/entire-graph/actions/runs/33823078438/job/100870118818\",\"name\":\"test (windows-latest) / shard 3\",\"startedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:47:16Z\",\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"workflowName\":\"test\"},{\"__typename\":\"CheckRun\",\"completedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:50:12Z\",\"conclusion\":\"SUCCESS\",\"detailsUrl\":\"https://github.com/entireio/entire-graph/actions/runs/33823078438/job/100870118870\",\"name\":\"test (windows-latest) / shard 4\",\"startedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:47:16Z\",\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"workflowName\":\"test\"},{\"__typename\":\"CheckRun\",\"completedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:50:14Z\",\"conclusion\":\"SUCCESS\",\"detailsUrl\":\"https://github.com/entireio/entire-graph/actions/runs/33823078438/job/100870118816\",\"name\":\"test (windows-latest) / shard 5\",\"startedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:47:19Z\",\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"workflowName\":\"test\"},{\"__typename\":\"CheckRun\",\"completedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:49:56Z\",\"conclusion\":\"SUCCESS\",\"detailsUrl\":\"https://github.com/entireio/entire-graph/actions/runs/33823078438/job/100870118808\",\"name\":\"test (windows-latest) / shard 6\",\"startedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:47:16Z\",\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"workflowName\":\"test\"},{\"__typename\":\"CheckRun\",\"completedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:49:19Z\",\"conclusion\":\"SUCCESS\",\"detailsUrl\":\"https://github.com/entireio/entire-graph/actions/runs/33823078438/job/100870118812\",\"name\":\"test (windows-latest) / shard 7\",\"startedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:47:19Z\",\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"workflowName\":\"test\"},{\"__typename\":\"CheckRun\",\"completedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:48:55Z\",\"conclusion\":\"SUCCESS\",\"detailsUrl\":\"https://github.com/entireio/entire-graph/actions/runs/33823078438/job/100870118695\",\"name\":\"test (windows-latest) / non-heavy packages\",\"startedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:47:16Z\",\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"workflowName\":\"test\"},{\"__typename\":\"CheckRun\",\"completedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:50:57Z\",\"conclusion\":\"SUCCESS\",\"detailsUrl\":\"https://github.com/entireio/entire-graph/actions/runs/33823078438/job/100870834386\",\"name\":\"test (windows-latest) / coverage and integrity\",\"startedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:50:46Z\",\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"workflowName\":\"test\"},{\"__typename\":\"CheckRun\",\"completedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:51:08Z\",\"conclusion\":\"SUCCESS\",\"detailsUrl\":\"https://github.com/entireio/entire-graph/actions/runs/33823078438/job/100870875100\",\"name\":\"test (windows-latest)\",\"startedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:51:05Z\",\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"workflowName\":\"test\"},{\"__typename\":\"CheckRun\",\"completedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:52:03Z\",\"conclusion\":\"FAILURE\",\"detailsUrl\":\"https://entire.io/gh/entireio/entire-graph/trails/142\",\"name\":\"Entire Gates\",\"startedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:45:33Z\",\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"workflowName\":\"\"}],\"title\":\"fix(sem): stop bare type names resolving across language boundaries\",\"updatedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:45:26Z\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/entireio/entire-graph/pull/184\"}\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":1,"nanos":354826230},"formatted_output":"Warning: truncated output (original token count: 13646)\nTotal output lines: 1\n\n{\"additions\":4495,\"baseRefOid\":\"9cdc0592352da8987ee727d8e1cfd0bf871ae4a5\",\"changedFiles\":11,\"commits\":[{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-08-29T19:46:52Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"257360244+suhaanthayyil@users.noreply.github.com\",\"id\":\"U_kgDOD1cBdA\",\"login\":\"suhaanthayyil\",\"name\":\"Suhaan Thayyil\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-08-29T19:46:52Z\",\"messageBody\":\"The workspace short-name index used to resolve a bare type name in a\\nsignature is keyed by name alone. resolveTypeReference filtered its\\ncandidates by kind but never by language, so any workspace-unique name\\nbound confidently across a language boundary: an Erlang `-record(point,\\n{x = 0, y = 0}).` became the resolved type of an R function and of a\\nClojure function whose parameter happened to be called `point`\\n(resolution=name_only, scope=module, confidence 0.75). Neither edge is\\npossible - R cannot name an Erlang record.\\n\\nThe invented edge was also unstable. Because the impossible declaration\\ncounted towards ambiguity, adding a second same-named type anywhere in\\nthe corpus silently deleted the edge, and a foreign declaration could\\nsuppress a real, resolvable same-language one.\\n\\n\\\"Same language only\\\" would be the wrong repair: C++ and Objective-C\\nconsume C headers verbatim, Swift imports C and Objective-C declarations\\nthrough the Clang importer, JVM languages name Java classes directly,\\nTypeScript and JavaScript share one type system, and F# names C# types.\\nThose edges exist today and must survive. So the guard is a language\\ncompatibility relation - symmetric, reflexive and deliberately not\\ntransitive, since ClojureScript shares declarations with Clojure and\\nClojure with Java, but ClojureScript cannot name a Java class.\\n\\nFiltering candidates rather than emitted edges also restores the\\nsuppressed same-language edges.\",\"messageHeadline\":\"fix(sem): stop bare type names resolving across language boundaries\",\"oid\":\"21d60e9dd0ad4c36cafffaa2d237197644e5b2a5\"},{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-08-29T20:09:48Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"257360244+suhaanthayyil@users.noreply.github.com\",\"id\":\"U_kgDOD1cBdA\",\"login\":\"suhaanthayyil\",\"name\":\"Suhaan Thayyil\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-08-29T20:09:48Z\",\"messageBody\":\"…ng languages\\n\\nThe first commit filtered resolveTypeReference, because that is where the\\nErlang-record-becomes-an-R-type case surfaced. It left the rest of the same\\ndefect in place: the workspace short-name index is keyed by name alone, and call,\\nconstructor, inheritance and test resolution all read it and pick a target the\\nsame way.\\n\\nMeasured on origin/main over a corpus where Go, Python and Ruby each declare\\ntheir own Point.process, all four CALLS edges resolve by type_inferred and two\\nare wrong:\\n\\n    Go/run     -> Go/Point.process     correct\\n    Kotlin/... -> Java/Shape.area      correct, compatible languages\\n    Python/run -> Go/Point.process     WRONG\\n    Ruby/run   -> Go/Point.process     WRONG\\n\\nPython and Ruby each declare the method the call actually reaches; the resolver\\nchose Go's because the index is keyed by bare name.\\n\\nEvery lookup into that global index now filters candidates through the existing\\nlanguage-compatibility relation. Same-file and same-package candidate lists are\\nleft alone: they cannot cross a language boundary, so filtering them would cost a\\npass for no possible change. Four sites are deliberately NOT filtered because no\\nreferring symbol is in scope to filter against -- resolveQualifiedType,\\ngoMethodSymbolByID, methodReturnTypes, and the extension-member lookup, which\\nalready restricts to an exact language.\\n\\nFiltering candidates rather than emitted edges is what makes this a repair rather\\nthan a deletion. With the impossible declarations out of the candidate set the\\nreal ones resolve, so the edge count is unchanged and the two wrong edges move to\\nthe right target. The compatible Kotlin -> Java edge survives, which a\\nsame-language-only rule would have destroyed.\",\"messageHeadline\":\"fix(sem): stop call, constructor, inheritance and test targets crossi…\",\"oid\":\"b4007ad07515bfdf0054f2fe2cd01ca646912635\"},{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-08-29T21:36:00Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"257360244+suhaanthayyil@users.noreply.github.com\",\"id\":\"U_kgDOD1cBdA\",\"login\":\"suhaanthayyil\",\"name\":\"Suhaan Thayyil\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-08-29T21:36:00Z\",\"messageBody\":\"…he real one\\n\\nThe filter inside resolveCallTargets was reported as unverified when the previous\\ncommit landed: no mutation caught it and no fixture discriminated it. The reason\\nis now established -- it was redundant. All four call sites already wrap their\\nsymbolsByShortName lookup in sharedTypeCandidates, because the referring symbol\\nis known there, so the in-function call was a second application of an\\nidempotent function. Worse, it looked like the guard while the call sites were\\nthe guard.\\n\\nThe path it was meant to protect is real and is now covered. A bare free-function\\ncall with no receiver ends at the globally-unique gate (\\\"direct call expression\\nmatched globally unique symbol name\\\", name_only, 0.68), which fires when exactly\\none symbol in the workspace carries the name -- so a name defined only in a\\nforeign language is globally unique by construction. Unwrapping the bare-name\\ncall site reproduces it exactly:\\n\\n    Python/driver2_py -> Ruby/beta_only   resolution name_only\\n\\nThe earlier attempt to provoke this failed for two reasons worth recording: the\\nfirst fixture defined the name in BOTH languages, so it was never globally\\nunique and the gate never fired; the second put the call and the definition in\\none file, where the same-file branch returns before the global list is consulted.\\nThe name must exist exactly once, in a foreign language, in another file.\\n\\nThe new test discriminates in both directions: a Python-to-Python call across\\nfiles must still resolve (proving the gate is reachable and the filter has not\\ndisabled cross-file resolution), and the Python-to-Ruby call must not.\",\"messageHeadline\":\"fix(sem): drop the redundant in-function candidate filter, and test t…\",\"oid\":\"c43c83c8f8525a9eeceac65dadc709693336933f\"},{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-08-30T15:18:49Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"257360244+suhaanthayyil@users.noreply.github.com\",\"id\":\"U_kgDOD1cBdA\",\"login\":\"suhaanthayyil\",\"name\":\"Suhaan Thayyil\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-08-30T15:18:49Z\",\"messageBody\":\"The language-compatibility groups treat Clojure and ClojureScript as sharing\\ndeclarations, which is true only for portable sources. parser.go maps BOTH .clj\\n(JVM-only) and .cljc (portable) to the language \\\"Clojure\\\", so a pair rule stated\\nin language names cannot tell them apart, and a .cljs consumer bound a\\ndeclaration it can never name. Measured before this change:\\n\\n    ClojureScript(src/app.cljs) -> Clojure(src/jvmonly.clj)    WRONG\\n    ClojureScript(src/app.cljs) -> Clojure(src/portable.cljc)  correct\\n\\nThe refinement reads the declaring file's extension, and only for a\\ncross-language pair: within one language the question does not arise, and a\\n.clj referring to a .clj is exactly what should resolve. It is symmetric in the\\nuseful direction too, since a ClojureScript symbol always comes from .cljs, so a\\nJVM Clojure file no longer binds a ClojureScript-only declaration either.\\n\\nSevering the dialect pair would also have removed the wrong edge, and would have\\ntaken the correct one with it; the test asserts both halves so that shortcut\\nfails.\",\"messageHeadline\":\"fix(sem): let ClojureScript bind only portable Clojure declarations\",\"oid\":\"b941de3be78709dc41706d1a4081c1fc84033561\"},{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-08-30T15:21:21Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"257360244+suhaanthayyil@users.noreply.github.com\",\"id\":\"U_kgDOD1cBdA\",\"login\":\"suhaanthayyil\",\"name\":\"Suhaan Thayyil\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-08-30T15:21:21Z\",\"messageBody\":\"The shared-declaration matrix asserted that ClojureScript binds a type declared\\nin tick.clj. That encodes the defect the previous commit fixes: .clj is JVM-only\\nand a .cljs consumer cannot name it, so the assertion pinned the wrong\\nbehaviour and failed once the refinement landed.\\n\\nThe declaration moves to tick.cljc, the only Clojure source both readers accept.\\nThe assertion is unchanged and still pins what the test is for -- that a\\nlegitimate cross-language declaration edge survives the language filter.\",\"messageHeadline\":\"test(sem): pin the Clojure shared-declaration case to a portable source\",\"oid\":\"ea278b9483581a7c021cb157057051b960fd6ae0\"},{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-09-02T21:13:46Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"257360244+suhaanthayyil@users.noreply.github.com\",\"id\":\"U_kgDOD1cBdA\",\"login\":\"suhaanthayyil\",\"name\":\"Suhaan Thayyil\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-09-02T21:13:46Z\",\"messageBody\":\"Two trail findings on trail 142.\\n\\n01M19M7CBDBX -- the C family was declared as one symmetric group, but its\\ninteroperability runs one way. C++ and Objective-C compile C headers\\nunchanged and Swift imports both through the Clang importer; C has no way to\\nname a C++ class or template, and an Objective-C `.m` cannot name a C++ type\\neither -- only `.mm` can. Listing them symmetrically licensed exactly the\\nimpossible edges this file exists to remove, just in the other direction. The\\nrelation map was already keyed from -> targets, so the fix is a one-way table\\nbeside the symmetric groups rather than a new mechanism.\\n\\nTwo tests encoded the defect and are corrected with it. One asserted\\n`languagesShareTypes(\\\"C\\\", \\\"C++\\\")`; the other's fixture was\\n`void use_shape(Shape s)` in a .c file, which does not compile as C at all.\\nThe legitimate direction stays pinned by C++/renderWidget->C/Widget and\\nSwift/useVec->C/Vec3, both of which still resolve.\\n\\n01M19M6D39B1 -- `.zsh` is labelled Zsh and `.sh` is labelled Bash, and no\\ngroup related them, so `source ./lib.sh; helper` lost its CALLS edge even\\nthough sourcing executes the library's functions in the calling shell. They\\nare now one symmetric pair, which is the correct shape: the naming works in\\nwhichever direction the source is written.\",\"messageHeadline\":\"fix(sem): make C-family type sharing directional, and relate the shells\",\"oid\":\"9f96eb21bbbef668b9f9d1f6c964eabf8cd5abe6\"},{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-09-02T23:14:36Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"257360244+suhaanthayyil@users.noreply.github.com\",\"id\":\"U_kgDOD1cBdA\",\"login\":\"suhaanthayyil\",\"name\":\"Suhaan Thayyil\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-09-02T23:14:36Z\",\"messageBody\":\"…ither dialect\\n\\nTwo follow-up findings on trail 142, both against the previous commit.\\n\\n01M1HZK6P1Z9 -- Swift's direct C++ interoperability imports C++ types and\\nfunctions, so `Swift -> C++` is a real edge the one-way table left out. The\\nreverse stays refused: C++ has no way to name a Swift declaration.\\n\\n01M1HZK6NNTT -- the Clojure refinement decided from the CANDIDATE's extension\\nalone, so every reference out of a portable `.cljc` file to a\\nClojureScript-only declaration was rejected. A reader conditional --\\n`#?(:cljs ...)` -- is exactly how portable code names a dialect-specific\\ndeclaration, and `.cljc` is compiled by both readers. A single-dialect\\nconsumer still needs a portable declaration, which is the half this must not\\nloosen: a `.cljs` reader cannot read `.clj`.\",\"messageHeadline\":\"fix(sem): let Swift reach C++, and let a portable Clojure file name e…\",\"oid\":\"07ff4e21d7659128cd0d8ad3c7c84cd15e40bbfe\"},{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-09-02T23:42:09Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"257360244+suhaanthayyil@users.noreply.github.com\",\"id\":\"U_kgDOD1cBdA\",\"login\":\"suhaanthayyil\",\"name\":\"Suhaan Thayyil\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-09-02T23:42:09Z\",\"messageBody\":\"Trail finding 01M1J6FZ0JZV, against the previous commit. The language label\\nfollows the FILE, not the declaration: a header holding any Objective-C\\nsyntax is labelled Objective-C even where it also declares plain C structs and\\nfunctions, which C++ compiles unchanged. Refusing the pair therefore dropped\\nvalid C++ edges to those declarations wholesale.\\n\\nThe reverse stays refused -- Objective-C `.m` cannot name a C++ type, only\\n`.mm` can, and that pair is already listed.\",\"messageHeadline\":\"fix(sem): let C++ consume Objective-C-labelled declarations\",\"oid\":\"e1ecc4c38be795d1478b796292212819a8239aab\"},{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-09-02T23:48:59Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"257360244+suhaanthayyil@users.noreply.github.com\",\"id\":\"U_kgDOD1cBdA\",\"login\":\"suhaanthayyil\",\"name\":\"Suhaan Thayyil\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-09-02T23:48:59Z\",\"messageBody\":\"Trail finding 01M1J83902SV. An `@objc` Swift class or method is declared in the\\ngenerated `<Module>-Swift.h` header and named directly from Objective-C, so\\nthose type, inheritance, constructor and call edges are real and were being\\ndropped.\\n\\nWith both directions real -- Swift imports Objective-C through the Clang\\nimporter, Objective-C names Swift through the generated header -- the pair is\\nsymmetric rather than one-way, and moves to the symmetric list. The rest of the\\nfamily stays directional: C still cannot name C++, and Objective-C `.m` still\\ncannot name a C++ type.\",\"messageHeadline\":\"fix(sem): let Objective-C name Swift declarations\",\"oid\":\"d3f6fd695f863338dd6e808593f237a7346d3672\"},{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-09-03T02:00:22Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"suhaanthayyil0@gmail.com\",\"id\":\"U_kgDOD1cBdA\",\"login\":\"suhaanthayyil\",\"name\":\"Suhaan Thayyil\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-09-03T02:00:22Z\",\"messageBody\":\"The language-compatibility relation answers one question: may source written in\\nlanguage A name a type DECLARED in language B. Test resolution was swept into\\nthe same filter by analogy with call, constructor and inheritance resolution,\\nbut a TESTS edge names no type. It records a naming convention -- test_frobnicate\\ncovers frobnicate -- and harnesses cross language boundaries as a matter of\\ncourse: pytest over a C extension, a shell script over a compiled binary, JS\\nspecs over a WASM module.\\n\\nMeasured on this branch, a pytest harness beside a C unit produced no TESTS edge\\nat all:\\n\\n    tests/test_frobnicate.py::test_frobnicate -> src/frobnicate.c::frobnicate\\n        dropped, Python cannot name a C type\\n\\nNothing about that binding is ambiguous: frobnicate is workspace-unique among\\nnon-test callables, so resolveTestSubject would have taken it by name_only.\\n\\nThe gate is also asymmetric here in a way that makes no sense for a convention.\\nThe C family relation is directional by design -- C++ and Swift reach C, and C\\nnames nothing -- so a C test could never reach a non-C unit however unambiguous\\nthe subject name, while a C++ test in the same repo could.\\n\\nPrecision for TESTS already comes from resolveTestSubject's evidence order:\\nsame-file, then the test file's import bindings, then a workspace-unique or\\nsame-directory match, and nothing otherwise. That ordering, not type interop, is\\nthe right guard. The other lookups into the workspace short-name index keep the\\nfilter unchanged.\",\"messageHeadline\":\"fix(sem): stop type-sharing from gating TESTS edges\",\"oid\":\"f51de3ec6cbfa2fdc7c793ad392b69db87609959\"},{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-09-03T02:14:45Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"257360244+suhaanthayyil@users.noreply.github.com\",\"id\":\"U_kgDOD1cBdA\",\"login\":\"suhaanthayyil\",\"name\":\"Suhaan Thayyil\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-09-03T02:14:45Z\",\"messageBody\":\"importedReceiverCallRelations filtered its workspace short-name lookup through\\nsharedTypeCandidates before testing the import, so a receiver call that an\\nexplicit import had already bound to a parsed local file was thrown away\\nwhenever the two files were written in different languages.\\n\\nThe language-compatibility relation answers one question: may source in language\\nA name a type DECLARED in language B. This binding is not a type reference. It\\nis an import whose module path resolves to the callee's FILE, which is direct\\nevidence that these two files interoperate, and that outranks a language-pair\\nheuristic. Python/C is deliberately absent from the relation because a Python\\ncaller never names a C struct -- but it does call the extension's exported\\nfunctions.\\n\\nMeasured on this branch, `import frobnicate` beside a parsed `frobnicate.c`:\\n\\n    app.py::run -> external:symbol:frobnicate.compute   (import_external, 0.78)\\n\\ninstead of the internal edge. The identical Python-to-Python fixture resolved\\nto `frobnicate.py::compute` at import_resolved/0.84, so the language pair was\\nthe only difference.\\n\\nImport evidence outranks the heuristic only while it is unambiguous. Module\\npaths are matched by suffix, so one import can match same-named callables in\\nseveral languages at once, and nothing then says which one it bound; emitting\\nthe fanout or picking a winner would both invent an FFI edge. The type-sharing\\ncandidates are therefore still preferred whenever they resolve anything -- every\\npreviously resolved call, same-language fanout included, is unchanged -- and the\\nunfiltered set is consulted only when it names exactly one target. The change is\\nstrictly additive: it can only resolve calls that previously degraded to an\\nunresolved external target.\",\"messageHeadline\":\"fix(sem): let import evidence outrank type-sharing for receiver calls\",\"oid\":\"ccbb244db3b74efee35eb1ec26a643fd243d4ab4\"},{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-09-03T20:04:08Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"thomas@dohmke.de\",\"id\":\"MDQ6VXNlcjcwNzIw\",\"login\":\"ashtom\",\"name\":\"Thomas Dohmke\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-09-03T20:04:08Z\",\"messageBody\":\"\",\"messageHeadline\":\"Merge origin/main into fix/cross-language-name-only-edges\",\"oid\":\"f7ca6ec9c73cce0c54b371f5e91b40cc84733ed4\"},{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-09-03T20:25:06Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"thomas@dohmke.de\",\"id\":\"MDQ6VXNlcjcwNzIw\",\"login\":\"ashtom\",\"name\":\"Thomas Dohmke\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-09-03T20:25:06Z\",\"messageBody\":\"\",\"messageHeadline\":\"fix: filter receiver type candidates by language\",\"oid\":\"b8aace015c59224944bd9d9b8927958154403bbb\"},{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-09-03T20:40:05Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"257360244+suhaanthayyil@users.noreply.github.com\",\"id\":\"U_kgDOD1cBdA\",\"login\":\"suhaanthayyil\",\"name\":\"Suhaan Thayyil\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-09-03T20:40:05Z\",\"messageBody\":\"`import frobnicate; frobnicate.compute(1)` resolves to a locally parsed\\nfrobnicate.c, but `from frobnicate import compute; compute(1)` did not.\\nresolveCallTargets received candidates already narrowed by the\\nlanguage-compatibility relation, so the C declaration was gone before the\\nimport tier could compare its module path, and the call degraded to an\\nunresolved external target.\\n\\nThat relation answers whether source in one language may name a type\\nDECLARED in another. An explicit import whose module path resolves to the\\ncallee's FILE is direct evidence that the two files interoperate, which\\noutranks it -- the reason the qualified spelling of the very same call\\nalready resolves in importedReceiverCallTargets.\\n\\nresolveCallTargets now narrows internally and keeps both sets. The import\\ntier prefers the compatible candidates, so every previously resolved call\\nis unchanged, and consults the wider set only when it names exactly one\\ntarget: module paths are matched by suffix, so one import can match\\nsame-named callables in several languages at once, and nothing then says\\nwhich of them it bound.\",\"messageHeadline\":\"fix(sem): let import evidence outrank the language filter for bare calls\",\"oid\":\"fb89aeb5c4dd26b13d1e08c6d2b4542efb96345c\"},{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-09-03T20:58:04Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"257360244+suhaanthayyil@users.noreply.github.com\",\"id\":\"U_kgDOD1cBdA\",\"login\":\"suhaanthayyil\",\"name\":\"Suhaan Thayyil\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-09-03T20:58:04Z\",\"messageBody\":\"The bare-call import tiers read an explicit import as evidence that\\noutranks the language-compatibility relation. That makes Python's scoping\\nload-bearing where it used to be cosmetic: a name the caller's own body\\nbinds is now resolved into a CONFIDENT cross-language edge instead of a\\nmerely unresolved one.\\n\\n    from frobnicate import compute   # frobnicate.c is parsed\\n\\n    def run(compute):\\n        return compute(1)            # calls the PARAMETER\\n\\nbound run to frobnicate.c's compute, and so did a…3646 tokens truncated… that also names a module, resolves to\\nnothing rather than to a guess; and a name that only ever binds a MODULE\\n(`import mod`, `import mod as m`) emits no bare-call external edge at all,\\nbecause a module object is not callable and `mod.m` names nothing.\",\"messageHeadline\":\"fix(sem): resolve a Python import alias to the member it renames\",\"oid\":\"bcab954997b535d4760fdbc70570a05c46f778b6\"},{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-09-03T22:59:49Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"257360244+suhaanthayyil@users.noreply.github.com\",\"id\":\"U_kgDOD1cBdA\",\"login\":\"suhaanthayyil\",\"name\":\"Suhaan Thayyil\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-09-03T22:59:49Z\",\"messageBody\":\"A call in a Python function signature -- a default argument, a parameter\\nannotation, a return annotation -- is evaluated where the `def` statement\\nruns, in the enclosing scope. The scope walker descended only into the\\nfunction body, so such a call was recorded in no scope at all. The call\\nscan still attributes it to the function whose signature line holds it,\\nand a `complete` scope view reporting no modules for that name makes\\nimportsWithName DELETE the file-level import binding, so the imported\\ncall loses its resolution tier entirely: a Python-to-C `compute()` in a\\ndefault argument emitted no CALLS edge at all. Walk the signature nodes\\nagainst the enclosing bindings, published under the function's owner.\\nDecorators already worked -- tree-sitter hangs them off\\n`decorated_definition`, outside the function node.\\n\\n`from mod import (compute,)` splits on commas into `(compute` and `)`,\\nso the grouping parentheses were parsed as part of the bound name,\\nbinding a name no call can match while the scope stayed complete. Trim\\nthem in parsePythonImportItem, which the three line-oriented import\\nscanners share; the multi-line form, which those scanners cannot see\\npast the first line of, is restored by the AST-derived scope binding.\",\"messageHeadline\":\"fix(sem): resolve Python signature calls and parenthesised imports\",\"oid\":\"6c91abc64935609ca0061bba5c2ff580f46f9dde\"},{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-09-03T23:10:35Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"257360244+suhaanthayyil@users.noreply.github.com\",\"id\":\"U_kgDOD1cBdA\",\"login\":\"suhaanthayyil\",\"name\":\"Suhaan Thayyil\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-09-03T23:10:35Z\",\"messageBody\":\"A `.h` is labelled Objective-C the moment it contains one `#import` or\\none `@interface`, and such headers go on declaring the plain C structs,\\ntypedefs and functions a `.c` includes and compiles verbatim. C named\\nnothing in typeSharingLanguageEdges, so every one of those targets was\\nfiltered out of the candidate list.\\n\\nMeasured over a fixture whose `.c` includes an Objective-C-labelled\\nheader and an otherwise identical plain-C control:\\n\\n\\tC/renderGadget -> C/Gadget               resolved\\n\\tC/renderWidget -> Objective-C/Widget     DROPPED (same declaration)\\n\\tC/callWidth    -> Objective-C/widgetWidth DROPPED\\n\\nAdd the directional `C -> Objective-C` edge and hold it to the same\\nkind refinement the C++ edge already carries: an `@interface`, an\\n`@protocol` and a selector stay unreachable from C, which cannot even\\ndeclare them. The edge is one-way at the family level -- C still cannot\\nname a C++ class or template, nor a Swift declaration.\\n\\nTestLanguagesShareTypesRelation pinned `C -> Objective-C` as\\nimpossible; that assertion was the bug, so it moves to the symmetric\\nblock with the reason the pair is symmetric at the language level and\\nstill narrowed by kind at the candidate level.\",\"messageHeadline\":\"fix(sem): let C name the plain C half of an Objective-C header\",\"oid\":\"071afb519d8c552fac49062d531300f2e36de18a\"},{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-09-03T23:41:30Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"257360244+suhaanthayyil@users.noreply.github.com\",\"id\":\"U_kgDOD1cBdA\",\"login\":\"suhaanthayyil\",\"name\":\"Suhaan Thayyil\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-09-03T23:41:30Z\",\"messageBody\":\"A `.h` is labelled C++ the moment it holds `extern \\\"C\\\"` or a bare `::`\\n(looksLikeCPlusPlusHeader), so the dual-use header -- the one written\\nprecisely SO a `.c` can include it -- is C++, and C names no C++ target in\\ntypeSharingLanguageEdges. A `.c` including such a header reached nothing in\\nit.\\n\\nTwo defects stacked. The first sits upstream of any language rule: an\\n`extern \\\"C\\\" {` line was blanked out of the source before the C++ grammar saw\\nit, and the blanking ran forward until a line beginning `}  // extern \\\"C\\\"`\\n-- or, failing that, to END OF FILE. The canonical dual-use header closes\\nwith a bare `}` under `#ifdef __cplusplus`, so the whole header was erased\\nand contributed a single macro symbol. tree-sitter-cpp parses\\n`linkage_specification` natively, and the fixture the mask was written for\\n(TestTreeSitterParserCPlusPlusMasksDoctestMacros) parses and yields entities\\nwithout it, so the mask is removed rather than bounded.\\n\\nThe second is the reachability rule. Widening the language pair is not the\\nrepair: unlike the C -> Objective-C case, whose offending kinds are the\\nclosed set objectiveCOnlyDeclaration already names, C++ exposes templates,\\noverloads, namespaces and classes a C source genuinely cannot name, and\\n`C -> C++` wholesale would trade a missing-edge bug for a large class of\\nwrong ones. typeSharingLanguageEdges is therefore untouched --\\nlanguagesShareTypes(\\\"C\\\", \\\"C++\\\") stays false -- and the widening is per\\nDECLARATION instead: declaredWithCLinkage marks what sits inside an\\n`extern \\\"C\\\" { ... }` block, which is the construct a C++ header uses to say\\nexactly which declarations have C linkage, and candidateSharesDeclarations\\noffers C those and only those. `extern \\\"C++\\\"` is not matched.\\n\\nMeasured over a fixture whose `.c` includes a dual-use header and an\\notherwise identical plain-C control -- the same two declarations, differing\\nonly in the header that holds them:\\n\\n\\tC/renderGadget -> C/Gadget            resolved (control)\\n\\tC/renderWidget -> C++/Widget          DROPPED  (declared extern \\\"C\\\")\\n\\tC/callWidth    -> C++/widgetWidth     DROPPED  (declared extern \\\"C\\\")\\n\\nresolveTypeReference re-asked languagesShareTypes about a candidate list\\nsharedTypeCandidates had already filtered, and that duplicate silently\\noverrode the finer per-declaration answer: with the rule in place the CALLS\\nedge resolved while USES_TYPE and PARAM_TYPE on the struct beside it still\\ndid not. The redundant conjunct is dropped.\\n\\nThe narrowing is pinned beside the widening: a class, a namespaced function\\nand an overload set in the SAME header stay unreachable from C.\",\"messageHeadline\":\"fix(sem): let C name the extern \\\"C\\\" half of a dual-use C++ header\",\"oid\":\"a09defd6cd761a39199e3b3ca6d47c24e5581e6c\"},{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-09-04T00:04:29Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"257360244+suhaanthayyil@users.noreply.github.com\",\"id\":\"U_kgDOD1cBdA\",\"login\":\"suhaanthayyil\",\"name\":\"Suhaan Thayyil\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-09-04T00:04:29Z\",\"messageBody\":\"tree-sitter gives `except_clause` no fields: its exception expression, its\\n`as` target and its handler block are plain named children. The scope walker\\nasked it for a \\\"body\\\" field, got nothing, and returned -- so no call anywhere\\nin a handler was ever recorded. The function-wide local pass then asked the\\nsame clause for a \\\"name\\\" field, got its last named child (the handler BLOCK)\\ninstead, and recursed into it, declaring every name written anywhere in the\\nhandler a local of the whole function. That deleted the import-resolved edge\\nfor calls in the `try` body too, which the walker had recorded correctly.\\n`with_item` has no alias field either -- the alias hangs off a nested\\n`as_pattern` -- so the same mistake made `with compute() as handle:` report\\nits own import shadowed.\\n\\nTake the alias from the `as_pattern` that actually carries it, let the\\ngeneric child walk reach the exception expression and the handler body like\\nany other statement, and bind an exception group's `as` target as well.\\n\\nA comprehension's OUTERMOST iterable is evaluated where the comprehension is\\nwritten and passed into the comprehension's frame; only later iterables run\\ninside it. Evaluating all of them inside it made\\n`[x for x in compute() for compute in ys]` -- which CPython runs happily --\\nreport its import shadowed by a target bound afterwards. The interleaved\\nfilter `[x for x in xs if compute() for compute in ys]` stays suppressed:\\nCPython raises UnboundLocalError there, so that name really is the\\ncomprehension's own local.\\n\\nFinally, the scanners that record WHAT a local name is bound to are line\\noriented, so a parenthesised from-import spanning lines showed them only\\n`from mod import (`, an item list of one bare paren. No member name was\\nrecorded behind an alias imported that way, and the resolver -- which needs\\nthe member name, because the workspace index is keyed by it -- had nothing to\\nresolve, while the AST walker reported the call unshadowed. Rejoin such a\\nlist into one logical line, bounded, and consume nothing when it never\\ncloses, so a stray paren cannot hide the imports below it.\\n\\n`TestPythonBareImportFFIRespectsASTScopes` listed `except_type` among the\\nshadowed cases, but nothing in it rebinds the name: an `except <expr>:`\\nclause has no `as` target at all. It only looked shadowed because the clause\\nwas skipped, so it moves to the unshadowed list it belongs in.\",\"messageHeadline\":\"fix(sem): a Python try statement is code of its own scope\",\"oid\":\"20d89c019292b6ee7fa0ac8faa6187e81b8d9bd5\"},{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-09-04T00:36:10Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"257360244+suhaanthayyil@users.noreply.github.com\",\"id\":\"U_kgDOD1cBdA\",\"login\":\"suhaanthayyil\",\"name\":\"Suhaan Thayyil\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-09-04T00:36:10Z\",\"messageBody\":\"Python rebinds a `def` or `class` name only once the whole statement has\\nrun, so the parts it evaluates on the way there still read the enclosing\\nbinding. CPython is the oracle:\\n\\n    def compute(): return \\\"OUTER\\\"\\n    def compute(v=compute()): return v   # -> OUTER\\n    def compute(v=compute()): ...        # -> NameError with no outer name\\n\\nThe scope walker bound the name at the `def`/`class` line, so every\\neagerly evaluated part of the statement -- defaults, annotations, bases,\\nclass-body statements, the signatures of the methods a class defines --\\nwas told the name was already rebound. For an imported name that is the\\nfail-closed direction: the view reports no modules, importsWithName drops\\nthe file-level import binding and the resolved edge goes with it.\\n\\nA `def` now binds at its body, which keeps recursion resolving to the\\nfunction itself. A class body interleaves code that runs immediately with\\nmethod bodies that run later, which one offset cannot express, so the\\nclass keeps its binding at the `class` line for deferred code and hands\\nits bases and body a view where that one binding is hidden.\\n\\nNo emitted relation changes today: a definition also defines a same-named\\nsymbol, which wins the same-file tier (or a CONSTRUCTS relation) before\\nimports are consulted, and the generic loop skips a call whose name equals\\nits owner's. The scope view is the observable surface and is where the\\nregression tests assert.\",\"messageHeadline\":\"fix(sem): a Python definition binds its own name after its signature\",\"oid\":\"0ac5865624a1c724568c6840b69f499055152db1\"},{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-09-04T00:45:24Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"257360244+suhaanthayyil@users.noreply.github.com\",\"id\":\"U_kgDOD1cBdA\",\"login\":\"suhaanthayyil\",\"name\":\"Suhaan Thayyil\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-09-04T00:45:24Z\",\"messageBody\":\"…header\\n\\nA dual-use header -- `#ifdef __cplusplus` / `extern \\\"C\\\" {` -- is labelled C++\\nbecause looksLikeCPlusPlusHeader fires on that very marker. The C-linkage\\nexception in candidateSharesDeclarations admitted only C consumers, so an\\nObjective-C `.m` importing such a header reached nothing in it, even though\\n`__cplusplus` is undefined under Objective-C compilation and the preprocessor\\ndeletes the guards outright, leaving plain C the `.m` compiles verbatim.\\n\\nMeasured over a fixture whose `.m` imports such a header, with an otherwise\\nidentical plain-C header as the control:\\n\\n\\tObjective-C/renderGadget -> C/Gadget          resolved (control)\\n\\tObjective-C/renderWidget -> C++/Widget        DROPPED  (same declaration)\\n\\tObjective-C/callWidth    -> C++/widgetWidth   DROPPED  (same declaration)\\n\\nThe widening stays per DECLARATION, not per language: `Objective-C -> C++` is\\nstill absent from typeSharingLanguageEdges, and only what the header itself put\\nunder C linkage is offered. The class, the overload set and the namespaced\\nfunction in the same header keep cLinkage false and stay filtered out.\",\"messageHeadline\":\"fix(sem): let Objective-C name the extern \\\"C\\\" half of a dual-use C++ …\",\"oid\":\"cd7177e415137e9863238735282b07e7619974f5\"}],\"deletions\":102,\"headRefName\":\"fix/cross-language-name-only-edges\",\"headRefOid\":\"cd7177e415137e9863238735282b07e7619974f5\",\"isDraft\":false,\"mergeStateStatus\":\"UNSTABLE\",\"mergeable\":\"MERGEABLE\",\"number\":184,\"state\":\"OPEN\",\"statusCheckRollup\":[{\"__typename\":\"CheckRun\",\"completedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:45:52Z\",\"conclusion\":\"SUCCESS\",\"detailsUrl\":\"https://github.com/entireio/entire-graph/actions/runs/33823078438/job/100869770015\",\"name\":\"lint\",\"startedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:45:34Z\",\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"workflowName\":\"test\"},{\"__typename\":\"CheckRun\",\"completedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:45:53Z\",\"conclusion\":\"SUCCESS\",\"detailsUrl\":\"https://github.com/entireio/entire-graph/actions/runs/33823078438/job/100869769753\",\"name\":\"build (ubuntu-latest)\",\"startedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:45:32Z\",\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"workflowName\":\"test\"},{\"__typename\":\"CheckRun\",\"completedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:45:54Z\",\"conclusion\":\"SUCCESS\",\"detailsUrl\":\"https://github.com/entireio/entire-graph/actions/runs/33823078438/job/100869769874\",\"name\":\"build (macos-latest)\",\"startedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:45:33Z\",\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"workflowName\":\"test\"},{\"__typename\":\"CheckRun\",\"completedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:46:35Z\",\"conclusion\":\"SUCCESS\",\"detailsUrl\":\"https://github.com/entireio/entire-graph/actions/runs/33823078438/job/100869769794\",\"name\":\"build (windows-latest)\",\"startedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:45:33Z\",\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"workflowName\":\"test\"},{\"__typename\":\"CheckRun\",\"completedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:48:13Z\",\"conclusion\":\"SUCCESS\",\"detailsUrl\":\"https://github.com/entireio/entire-graph/actions/runs/33823078438/job/100869769749\",\"name\":\"test (ubuntu-latest)\",\"startedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:45:32Z\",\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"workflowName\":\"test\"},{\"__typename\":\"CheckRun\",\"completedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:52:00Z\",\"conclusion\":\"SUCCESS\",\"detailsUrl\":\"https://github.com/entireio/entire-graph/actions/runs/33823078438/job/100869769885\",\"name\":\"test (macos-latest)\",\"startedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:45:34Z\",\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"workflowName\":\"test\"},{\"__typename\":\"CheckRun\",\"completedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:47:14Z\",\"conclusion\":\"SUCCESS\",\"detailsUrl\":\"https://github.com/entireio/entire-graph/actions/runs/33823078438/job/100869769891\",\"name\":\"test (windows-latest) / compile and plan\",\"startedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:45:52Z\",\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"workflowName\":\"test\"},{\"__typename\":\"CheckRun\",\"completedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:46:42Z\",\"conclusion\":\"SUCCESS\",\"detailsUrl\":\"https://github.com/entireio/entire-graph/actions/runs/33823078438/job/100869770012\",\"name\":\"test (windows-latest) / vet\",\"startedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:45:33Z\",\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"workflowName\":\"test\"},{\"__typename\":\"CheckRun\",\"completedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:45:44Z\",\"conclusion\":\"SUCCESS\",\"detailsUrl\":\"https://github.com/entireio/entire-graph/actions/runs/33823078438/job/100869769847\",\"name\":\"bm25-adapter\",\"startedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:45:33Z\",\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"workflowName\":\"test\"},{\"__typename\":\"CheckRun\",\"completedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:45:55Z\",\"conclusion\":\"SUCCESS\",\"detailsUrl\":\"https://github.com/entireio/entire-graph/actions/runs/33823078438/job/100869769852\",\"name\":\"statusline (ubuntu-latest)\",\"startedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:45:33Z\",\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"workflowName\":\"test\"},{\"__typename\":\"CheckRun\",\"completedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:46:17Z\",\"conclusion\":\"SUCCESS\",\"detailsUrl\":\"https://github.com/entireio/entire-graph/actions/runs/33823078438/job/100869769632\",\"name\":\"statusline (macos-latest)\",\"startedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:45:33Z\",\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"workflowName\":\"test\"},{\"__typename\":\"CheckRun\",\"completedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:49:30Z\",\"conclusion\":\"SUCCESS\",\"detailsUrl\":\"https://github.com/entireio/entire-graph/actions/runs/33823078438/job/100870118749\",\"name\":\"test (windows-latest) / shard 0\",\"startedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:47:16Z\",\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"workflowName\":\"test\"},{\"__typename\":\"CheckRun\",\"completedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:49:54Z\",\"conclusion\":\"SUCCESS\",\"detailsUrl\":\"https://github.com/entireio/entire-graph/actions/runs/33823078438/job/100870118803\",\"name\":\"test (windows-latest) / shard 1\",\"startedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:47:16Z\",\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"workflowName\":\"test\"},{\"__typename\":\"CheckRun\",\"completedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:50:44Z\",\"conclusion\":\"SUCCESS\",\"detailsUrl\":\"https://github.com/entireio/entire-graph/actions/runs/33823078438/job/100870118706\",\"name\":\"test (windows-latest) / shard 2\",\"startedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:47:16Z\",\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"workflowName\":\"test\"},{\"__typename\":\"CheckRun\",\"completedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:49:34Z\",\"conclusion\":\"SUCCESS\",\"detailsUrl\":\"https://github.com/entireio/entire-graph/actions/runs/33823078438/job/100870118818\",\"name\":\"test (windows-latest) / shard 3\",\"startedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:47:16Z\",\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"workflowName\":\"test\"},{\"__typename\":\"CheckRun\",\"completedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:50:12Z\",\"conclusion\":\"SUCCESS\",\"detailsUrl\":\"https://github.com/entireio/entire-graph/actions/runs/33823078438/job/100870118870\",\"name\":\"test (windows-latest) / shard 4\",\"startedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:47:16Z\",\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"workflowName\":\"test\"},{\"__typename\":\"CheckRun\",\"completedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:50:14Z\",\"conclusion\":\"SUCCESS\",\"detailsUrl\":\"https://github.com/entireio/entire-graph/actions/runs/33823078438/job/100870118816\",\"name\":\"test (windows-latest) / shard 5\",\"startedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:47:19Z\",\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"workflowName\":\"test\"},{\"__typename\":\"CheckRun\",\"completedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:49:56Z\",\"conclusion\":\"SUCCESS\",\"detailsUrl\":\"https://github.com/entireio/entire-graph/actions/runs/33823078438/job/100870118808\",\"name\":\"test (windows-latest) / shard 6\",\"startedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:47:16Z\",\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"workflowName\":\"test\"},{\"__typename\":\"CheckRun\",\"completedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:49:19Z\",\"conclusion\":\"SUCCESS\",\"detailsUrl\":\"https://github.com/entireio/entire-graph/actions/runs/33823078438/job/100870118812\",\"name\":\"test (windows-latest) / shard 7\",\"startedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:47:19Z\",\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"workflowName\":\"test\"},{\"__typename\":\"CheckRun\",\"completedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:48:55Z\",\"conclusion\":\"SUCCESS\",\"detailsUrl\":\"https://github.com/entireio/entire-graph/actions/runs/33823078438/job/100870118695\",\"name\":\"test (windows-latest) / non-heavy packages\",\"startedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:47:16Z\",\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"workflowName\":\"test\"},{\"__typename\":\"CheckRun\",\"completedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:50:57Z\",\"conclusion\":\"SUCCESS\",\"detailsUrl\":\"https://github.com/entireio/entire-graph/actions/runs/33823078438/job/100870834386\",\"name\":\"test (windows-latest) / coverage and integrity\",\"startedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:50:46Z\",\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"workflowName\":\"test\"},{\"__typename\":\"CheckRun\",\"completedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:51:08Z\",\"conclusion\":\"SUCCESS\",\"detailsUrl\":\"https://github.com/entireio/entire-graph/actions/runs/33823078438/job/100870875100\",\"name\":\"test (windows-latest)\",\"startedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:51:05Z\",\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"workflowName\":\"test\"},{\"__typename\":\"CheckRun\",\"completedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:52:03Z\",\"conclusion\":\"FAILURE\",\"detailsUrl\":\"https://entire.io/gh/entireio/entire-graph/trails/142\",\"name\":\"Entire Gates\",\"startedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:45:33Z\",\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"workflowName\":\"\"}],\"title\":\"fix(sem): stop bare type names resolving across language boundaries\",\"updatedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:45:26Z\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/entireio/entire-graph/pull/184\"}\n"},"started_at_ms":1788483487565,"completed_at_ms":1788483488920}}
{"timestamp":"2026-09-04T00:58:09.063Z","ordinal":24995,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","id":"ctco_01a069ec-4d27-77e3-9af6-c2f49bc07b6a","call_id":"call_PD6gKqSmBbre5OoR6xW6q02D","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 2.0 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"Warning: truncated output (original token count: 13646)\nTotal output lines: 1\n\n{\"additions\":4495,\"baseRefOid\":\"9cdc0592352da8987ee727d8e1cfd0bf871ae4a5\",\"changedFiles\":11,\"commits\":[{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-08-29T19:46:52Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"257360244+suhaanthayyil@users.noreply.github.com\",\"id\":\"U_kgDOD1cBdA\",\"login\":\"suhaanthayyil\",\"name\":\"Suhaan Thayyil\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-08-29T19:46:52Z\",\"messageBody\":\"The workspace short-name index used to resolve a bare type name in a\\nsignature is keyed by name alone. resolveTypeReference filtered its\\ncandidates by kind but never by language, so any workspace-unique name\\nbound confidently across a language boundary: an Erlang `-record(point,\\n{x = 0, y = 0}).` became the resolved type of an R function and of a\\nClojure function whose parameter happened to be called `point`\\n(resolution=name_only, scope=module, confidence 0.75). Neither edge is\\npossible - R cannot name an Erlang record.\\n\\nThe invented edge was also unstable. Because the impossible declaration\\ncounted towards ambiguity, adding a second same-named type anywhere in\\nthe corpus silently deleted the edge, and a foreign declaration could\\nsuppress a real, resolvable same-language one.\\n\\n\\\"Same language only\\\" would be the wrong repair: C++ and Objective-C\\nconsume C headers verbatim, Swift imports C and Objective-C declarations\\nthrough the Clang importer, JVM languages name Java classes directly,\\nTypeScript and JavaScript share one type system, and F# names C# types.\\nThose edges exist today and must survive. So the guard is a language\\ncompatibility relation - symmetric, reflexive and deliberately not\\ntransitive, since ClojureScript shares declarations with Clojure and\\nClojure with Java, but ClojureScript cannot name a Java class.\\n\\nFiltering candidates rather than emitted edges also restores the\\nsuppressed same-language edges.\",\"messageHeadline\":\"fix(sem): stop bare type names resolving across language boundaries\",\"oid\":\"21d60e9dd0ad4c36cafffaa2d237197644e5b2a5\"},{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-08-29T20:09:48Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"257360244+suhaanthayyil@users.noreply.github.com\",\"id\":\"U_kgDOD1cBdA\",\"login\":\"suhaanthayyil\",\"name\":\"Suhaan Thayyil\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-08-29T20:09:48Z\",\"messageBody\":\"…ng languages\\n\\nThe first commit filtered resolveTypeReference, because that is where the\\nErlang-record-becomes-an-R-type case surfaced. It left the rest of the same\\ndefect in place: the workspace short-name index is keyed by name alone, and call,\\nconstructor, inheritance and test resolution all read it and pick a target the\\nsame way.\\n\\nMeasured on origin/main over a corpus where Go, Python and Ruby each declare\\ntheir own Point.process, all four CALLS edges resolve by type_inferred and two\\nare wrong:\\n\\n    Go/run     -> Go/Point.process     correct\\n    Kotlin/... -> Java/Shape.area      correct, compatible languages\\n    Python/run -> Go/Point.process     WRONG\\n    Ruby/run   -> Go/Point.process     WRONG\\n\\nPython and Ruby each declare the method the call actually reaches; the resolver\\nchose Go's because the index is keyed by bare name.\\n\\nEvery lookup into that global index now filters candidates through the existing\\nlanguage-compatibility relation. Same-file and same-package candidate lists are\\nleft alone: they cannot cross a language boundary, so filtering them would cost a\\npass for no possible change. Four sites are deliberately NOT filtered because no\\nreferring symbol is in scope to filter against -- resolveQualifiedType,\\ngoMethodSymbolByID, methodReturnTypes, and the extension-member lookup, which\\nalready restricts to an exact language.\\n\\nFiltering candidates rather than emitted edges is what makes this a repair rather\\nthan a deletion. With the impossible declarations out of the candidate set the\\nreal ones resolve, so the edge count is unchanged and the two wrong edges move to\\nthe right target. The compatible Kotlin -> Java edge survives, which a\\nsame-language-only rule would have destroyed.\",\"messageHeadline\":\"fix(sem): stop call, constructor, inheritance and test targets crossi…\",\"oid\":\"b4007ad07515bfdf0054f2fe2cd01ca646912635\"},{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-08-29T21:36:00Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"257360244+suhaanthayyil@users.noreply.github.com\",\"id\":\"U_kgDOD1cBdA\",\"login\":\"suhaanthayyil\",\"name\":\"Suhaan Thayyil\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-08-29T21:36:00Z\",\"messageBody\":\"…he real one\\n\\nThe filter inside resolveCallTargets was reported as unverified when the previous\\ncommit landed: no mutation caught it and no fixture discriminated it. The reason\\nis now established -- it was redundant. All four call sites already wrap their\\nsymbolsByShortName lookup in sharedTypeCandidates, because the referring symbol\\nis known there, so the in-function call was a second application of an\\nidempotent function. Worse, it looked like the guard while the call sites were\\nthe guard.\\n\\nThe path it was meant to protect is real and is now covered. A bare free-function\\ncall with no receiver ends at the globally-unique gate (\\\"direct call expression\\nmatched globally unique symbol name\\\", name_only, 0.68), which fires when exactly\\none symbol in the workspace carries the name -- so a name defined only in a\\nforeign language is globally unique by construction. Unwrapping the bare-name\\ncall site reproduces it exactly:\\n\\n    Python/driver2_py -> Ruby/beta_only   resolution name_only\\n\\nThe earlier attempt to provoke this failed for two reasons worth recording: the\\nfirst fixture defined the name in BOTH languages, so it was never globally\\nunique and the gate never fired; the second put the call and the definition in\\none file, where the same-file branch returns before the global list is consulted.\\nThe name must exist exactly once, in a foreign language, in another file.\\n\\nThe new test discriminates in both directions: a Python-to-Python call across\\nfiles must still resolve (proving the gate is reachable and the filter has not\\ndisabled cross-file resolution), and the Python-to-Ruby call must not.\",\"messageHeadline\":\"fix(sem): drop the redundant in-function candidate filter, and test t…\",\"oid\":\"c43c83c8f8525a9eeceac65dadc709693336933f\"},{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-08-30T15:18:49Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"257360244+suhaanthayyil@users.noreply.github.com\",\"id\":\"U_kgDOD1cBdA\",\"login\":\"suhaanthayyil\",\"name\":\"Suhaan Thayyil\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-08-30T15:18:49Z\",\"messageBody\":\"The language-compatibility groups treat Clojure and ClojureScript as sharing\\ndeclarations, which is true only for portable sources. parser.go maps BOTH .clj\\n(JVM-only) and .cljc (portable) to the language \\\"Clojure\\\", so a pair rule stated\\nin language names cannot tell them apart, and a .cljs consumer bound a\\ndeclaration it can never name. Measured before this change:\\n\\n    ClojureScript(src/app.cljs) -> Clojure(src/jvmonly.clj)    WRONG\\n    ClojureScript(src/app.cljs) -> Clojure(src/portable.cljc)  correct\\n\\nThe refinement reads the declaring file's extension, and only for a\\ncross-language pair: within one language the question does not arise, and a\\n.clj referring to a .clj is exactly what should resolve. It is symmetric in the\\nuseful direction too, since a ClojureScript symbol always comes from .cljs, so a\\nJVM Clojure file no longer binds a ClojureScript-only declaration either.\\n\\nSevering the dialect pair would also have removed the wrong edge, and would have\\ntaken the correct one with it; the test asserts both halves so that shortcut\\nfails.\",\"messageHeadline\":\"fix(sem): let ClojureScript bind only portable Clojure declarations\",\"oid\":\"b941de3be78709dc41706d1a4081c1fc84033561\"},{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-08-30T15:21:21Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"257360244+suhaanthayyil@users.noreply.github.com\",\"id\":\"U_kgDOD1cBdA\",\"login\":\"suhaanthayyil\",\"name\":\"Suhaan Thayyil\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-08-30T15:21:21Z\",\"messageBody\":\"The shared-declaration matrix asserted that ClojureScript binds a type declared\\nin tick.clj. That encodes the defect the previous commit fixes: .clj is JVM-only\\nand a .cljs consumer cannot name it, so the assertion pinned the wrong\\nbehaviour and failed once the refinement landed.\\n\\nThe declaration moves to tick.cljc, the only Clojure source both readers accept.\\nThe assertion is unchanged and still pins what the test is for -- that a\\nlegitimate cross-language declaration edge survives the language filter.\",\"messageHeadline\":\"test(sem): pin the Clojure shared-declaration case to a portable source\",\"oid\":\"ea278b9483581a7c021cb157057051b960fd6ae0\"},{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-09-02T21:13:46Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"257360244+suhaanthayyil@users.noreply.github.com\",\"id\":\"U_kgDOD1cBdA\",\"login\":\"suhaanthayyil\",\"name\":\"Suhaan Thayyil\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-09-02T21:13:46Z\",\"messageBody\":\"Two trail findings on trail 142.\\n\\n01M19M7CBDBX -- the C family was declared as one symmetric group, but its\\ninteroperability runs one way. C++ and Objective-C compile C headers\\nunchanged and Swift imports both through the Clang importer; C has no way to\\nname a C++ class or template, and an Objective-C `.m` cannot name a C++ type\\neither -- only `.mm` can. Listing them symmetrically licensed exactly the\\nimpossible edges this file exists to remove, just in the other direction. The\\nrelation map was already keyed from -> targets, so the fix is a one-way table\\nbeside the symmetric groups rather than a new mechanism.\\n\\nTwo tests encoded the defect and are corrected with it. One asserted\\n`languagesShareTypes(\\\"C\\\", \\\"C++\\\")`; the other's fixture was\\n`void use_shape(Shape s)` in a .c file, which does not compile as C at all.\\nThe legitimate direction stays pinned by C++/renderWidget->C/Widget and\\nSwift/useVec->C/Vec3, both of which still resolve.\\n\\n01M19M6D39B1 -- `.zsh` is labelled Zsh and `.sh` is labelled Bash, and no\\ngroup related them, so `source ./lib.sh; helper` lost its CALLS edge even\\nthough sourcing executes the library's functions in the calling shell. They\\nare now one symmetric pair, which is the correct shape: the naming works in\\nwhichever direction the source is written.\",\"messageHeadline\":\"fix(sem): make C-family type sharing directional, and relate the shells\",\"oid\":\"9f96eb21bbbef668b9f9d1f6c964eabf8cd5abe6\"},{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-09-02T23:14:36Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"257360244+suhaanthayyil@users.noreply.github.com\",\"id\":\"U_kgDOD1cBdA\",\"login\":\"suhaanthayyil\",\"name\":\"Suhaan Thayyil\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-09-02T23:14:36Z\",\"messageBody\":\"…ither dialect\\n\\nTwo follow-up findings on trail 142, both against the previous commit.\\n\\n01M1HZK6P1Z9 -- Swift's direct C++ interoperability imports C++ types and\\nfunctions, so `Swift -> C++` is a real edge the one-way table left out. The\\nreverse stays refused: C++ has no way to name a Swift declaration.\\n\\n01M1HZK6NNTT -- the Clojure refinement decided from the CANDIDATE's extension\\nalone, so every reference out of a portable `.cljc` file to a\\nClojureScript-only declaration was rejected. A reader conditional --\\n`#?(:cljs ...)` -- is exactly how portable code names a dialect-specific\\ndeclaration, and `.cljc` is compiled by both readers. A single-dialect\\nconsumer still needs a portable declaration, which is the half this must not\\nloosen: a `.cljs` reader cannot read `.clj`.\",\"messageHeadline\":\"fix(sem): let Swift reach C++, and let a portable Clojure file name e…\",\"oid\":\"07ff4e21d7659128cd0d8ad3c7c84cd15e40bbfe\"},{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-09-02T23:42:09Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"257360244+suhaanthayyil@users.noreply.github.com\",\"id\":\"U_kgDOD1cBdA\",\"login\":\"suhaanthayyil\",\"name\":\"Suhaan Thayyil\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-09-02T23:42:09Z\",\"messageBody\":\"Trail finding 01M1J6FZ0JZV, against the previous commit. The language label\\nfollows the FILE, not the declaration: a header holding any Objective-C\\nsyntax is labelled Objective-C even where it also declares plain C structs and\\nfunctions, which C++ compiles unchanged. Refusing the pair therefore dropped\\nvalid C++ edges to those declarations wholesale.\\n\\nThe reverse stays refused -- Objective-C `.m` cannot name a C++ type, only\\n`.mm` can, and that pair is already listed.\",\"messageHeadline\":\"fix(sem): let C++ consume Objective-C-labelled declarations\",\"oid\":\"e1ecc4c38be795d1478b796292212819a8239aab\"},{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-09-02T23:48:59Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"257360244+suhaanthayyil@users.noreply.github.com\",\"id\":\"U_kgDOD1cBdA\",\"login\":\"suhaanthayyil\",\"name\":\"Suhaan Thayyil\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-09-02T23:48:59Z\",\"messageBody\":\"Trail finding 01M1J83902SV. An `@objc` Swift class or method is declared in the\\ngenerated `<Module>-Swift.h` header and named directly from Objective-C, so\\nthose type, inheritance, constructor and call edges are real and were being\\ndropped.\\n\\nWith both directions real -- Swift imports Objective-C through the Clang\\nimporter, Objective-C names Swift through the generated header -- the pair is\\nsymmetric rather than one-way, and moves to the symmetric list. The rest of the\\nfamily stays directional: C still cannot name C++, and Objective-C `.m` still\\ncannot name a C++ type.\",\"messageHeadline\":\"fix(sem): let Objective-C name Swift declarations\",\"oid\":\"d3f6fd695f863338dd6e808593f237a7346d3672\"},{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-09-03T02:00:22Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"suhaanthayyil0@gmail.com\",\"id\":\"U_kgDOD1cBdA\",\"login\":\"suhaanthayyil\",\"name\":\"Suhaan Thayyil\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-09-03T02:00:22Z\",\"messageBody\":\"The language-compatibility relation answers one question: may source written in\\nlanguage A name a type DECLARED in language B. Test resolution was swept into\\nthe same filter by analogy with call, constructor and inheritance resolution,\\nbut a TESTS edge names no type. It records a naming convention -- test_frobnicate\\ncovers frobnicate -- and harnesses cross language boundaries as a matter of\\ncourse: pytest over a C extension, a shell script over a compiled binary, JS\\nspecs over a WASM module.\\n\\nMeasured on this branch, a pytest harness beside a C unit produced no TESTS edge\\nat all:\\n\\n    tests/test_frobnicate.py::test_frobnicate -> src/frobnicate.c::frobnicate\\n        dropped, Python cannot name a C type\\n\\nNothing about that binding is ambiguous: frobnicate is workspace-unique among\\nnon-test callables, so resolveTestSubject would have taken it by name_only.\\n\\nThe gate is also asymmetric here in a way that makes no sense for a convention.\\nThe C family relation is directional by design -- C++ and Swift reach C, and C\\nnames nothing -- so a C test could never reach a non-C unit however unambiguous\\nthe subject name, while a C++ test in the same repo could.\\n\\nPrecision for TESTS already comes from resolveTestSubject's evidence order:\\nsame-file, then the test file's import bindings, then a workspace-unique or\\nsame-directory match, and nothing otherwise. That ordering, not type interop, is\\nthe right guard. The other lookups into the workspace short-name index keep the\\nfilter unchanged.\",\"messageHeadline\":\"fix(sem): stop type-sharing from gating TESTS edges\",\"oid\":\"f51de3ec6cbfa2fdc7c793ad392b69db87609959\"},{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-09-03T02:14:45Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"257360244+suhaanthayyil@users.noreply.github.com\",\"id\":\"U_kgDOD1cBdA\",\"login\":\"suhaanthayyil\",\"name\":\"Suhaan Thayyil\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-09-03T02:14:45Z\",\"messageBody\":\"importedReceiverCallRelations filtered its workspace short-name lookup through\\nsharedTypeCandidates before testing the import, so a receiver call that an\\nexplicit import had already bound to a parsed local file was thrown away\\nwhenever the two files were written in different languages.\\n\\nThe language-compatibility relation answers one question: may source in language\\nA name a type DECLARED in language B. This binding is not a type reference. It\\nis an import whose module path resolves to the callee's FILE, which is direct\\nevidence that these two files interoperate, and that outranks a language-pair\\nheuristic. Python/C is deliberately absent from the relation because a Python\\ncaller never names a C struct -- but it does call the extension's exported\\nfunctions.\\n\\nMeasured on this branch, `import frobnicate` beside a parsed `frobnicate.c`:\\n\\n    app.py::run -> external:symbol:frobnicate.compute   (import_external, 0.78)\\n\\ninstead of the internal edge. The identical Python-to-Python fixture resolved\\nto `frobnicate.py::compute` at import_resolved/0.84, so the language pair was\\nthe only difference.\\n\\nImport evidence outranks the heuristic only while it is unambiguous. Module\\npaths are matched by suffix, so one import can match same-named callables in\\nseveral languages at once, and nothing then says which one it bound; emitting\\nthe fanout or picking a winner would both invent an FFI edge. The type-sharing\\ncandidates are therefore still preferred whenever they resolve anything -- every\\npreviously resolved call, same-language fanout included, is unchanged -- and the\\nunfiltered set is consulted only when it names exactly one target. The change is\\nstrictly additive: it can only resolve calls that previously degraded to an\\nunresolved external target.\",\"messageHeadline\":\"fix(sem): let import evidence outrank type-sharing for receiver calls\",\"oid\":\"ccbb244db3b74efee35eb1ec26a643fd243d4ab4\"},{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-09-03T20:04:08Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"thomas@dohmke.de\",\"id\":\"MDQ6VXNlcjcwNzIw\",\"login\":\"ashtom\",\"name\":\"Thomas Dohmke\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-09-03T20:04:08Z\",\"messageBody\":\"\",\"messageHeadline\":\"Merge origin/main into fix/cross-language-name-only-edges\",\"oid\":\"f7ca6ec9c73cce0c54b371f5e91b40cc84733ed4\"},{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-09-03T20:25:06Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"thomas@dohmke.de\",\"id\":\"MDQ6VXNlcjcwNzIw\",\"login\":\"ashtom\",\"name\":\"Thomas Dohmke\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-09-03T20:25:06Z\",\"messageBody\":\"\",\"messageHeadline\":\"fix: filter receiver type candidates by language\",\"oid\":\"b8aace015c59224944bd9d9b8927958154403bbb\"},{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-09-03T20:40:05Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"257360244+suhaanthayyil@users.noreply.github.com\",\"id\":\"U_kgDOD1cBdA\",\"login\":\"suhaanthayyil\",\"name\":\"Suhaan Thayyil\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-09-03T20:40:05Z\",\"messageBody\":\"`import frobnicate; frobnicate.compute(1)` resolves to a locally parsed\\nfrobnicate.c, but `from frobnicate import compute; compute(1)` did not.\\nresolveCallTargets received candidates already narrowed by the\\nlanguage-compatibility relation, so the C declaration was gone before the\\nimport tier could compare its module path, and the call degraded to an\\nunresolved external target.\\n\\nThat relation answers whether source in one language may name a type\\nDECLARED in another. An explicit import whose module path resolves to the\\ncallee's FILE is direct evidence that the two files interoperate, which\\noutranks it -- the reason the qualified spelling of the very same call\\nalready resolves in importedReceiverCallTargets.\\n\\nresolveCallTargets now narrows internally and keeps both sets. The import\\ntier prefers the compatible candidates, so every previously resolved call\\nis unchanged, and consults the wider set only when it names exactly one\\ntarget: module paths are matched by suffix, so one import can match\\nsame-named callables in several languages at once, and nothing then says\\nwhich of them it bound.\",\"messageHeadline\":\"fix(sem): let import evidence outrank the language filter for bare calls\",\"oid\":\"fb89aeb5c4dd26b13d1e08c6d2b4542efb96345c\"},{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-09-03T20:58:04Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"257360244+suhaanthayyil@users.noreply.github.com\",\"id\":\"U_kgDOD1cBdA\",\"login\":\"suhaanthayyil\",\"name\":\"Suhaan Thayyil\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-09-03T20:58:04Z\",\"messageBody\":\"The bare-call import tiers read an explicit import as evidence that\\noutranks the language-compatibility relation. That makes Python's scoping\\nload-bearing where it used to be cosmetic: a name the caller's own body\\nbinds is now resolved into a CONFIDENT cross-language edge instead of a\\nmerely unresolved one.\\n\\n    from frobnicate import compute   # frobnicate.c is parsed\\n\\n    def run(compute):\\n        return compute(1)            # calls the PARAMETER\\n\\nbound run to frobnicate.c's compute, and so did a…3646 tokens truncated… that also names a module, resolves to\\nnothing rather than to a guess; and a name that only ever binds a MODULE\\n(`import mod`, `import mod as m`) emits no bare-call external edge at all,\\nbecause a module object is not callable and `mod.m` names nothing.\",\"messageHeadline\":\"fix(sem): resolve a Python import alias to the member it renames\",\"oid\":\"bcab954997b535d4760fdbc70570a05c46f778b6\"},{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-09-03T22:59:49Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"257360244+suhaanthayyil@users.noreply.github.com\",\"id\":\"U_kgDOD1cBdA\",\"login\":\"suhaanthayyil\",\"name\":\"Suhaan Thayyil\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-09-03T22:59:49Z\",\"messageBody\":\"A call in a Python function signature -- a default argument, a parameter\\nannotation, a return annotation -- is evaluated where the `def` statement\\nruns, in the enclosing scope. The scope walker descended only into the\\nfunction body, so such a call was recorded in no scope at all. The call\\nscan still attributes it to the function whose signature line holds it,\\nand a `complete` scope view reporting no modules for that name makes\\nimportsWithName DELETE the file-level import binding, so the imported\\ncall loses its resolution tier entirely: a Python-to-C `compute()` in a\\ndefault argument emitted no CALLS edge at all. Walk the signature nodes\\nagainst the enclosing bindings, published under the function's owner.\\nDecorators already worked -- tree-sitter hangs them off\\n`decorated_definition`, outside the function node.\\n\\n`from mod import (compute,)` splits on commas into `(compute` and `)`,\\nso the grouping parentheses were parsed as part of the bound name,\\nbinding a name no call can match while the scope stayed complete. Trim\\nthem in parsePythonImportItem, which the three line-oriented import\\nscanners share; the multi-line form, which those scanners cannot see\\npast the first line of, is restored by the AST-derived scope binding.\",\"messageHeadline\":\"fix(sem): resolve Python signature calls and parenthesised imports\",\"oid\":\"6c91abc64935609ca0061bba5c2ff580f46f9dde\"},{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-09-03T23:10:35Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"257360244+suhaanthayyil@users.noreply.github.com\",\"id\":\"U_kgDOD1cBdA\",\"login\":\"suhaanthayyil\",\"name\":\"Suhaan Thayyil\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-09-03T23:10:35Z\",\"messageBody\":\"A `.h` is labelled Objective-C the moment it contains one `#import` or\\none `@interface`, and such headers go on declaring the plain C structs,\\ntypedefs and functions a `.c` includes and compiles verbatim. C named\\nnothing in typeSharingLanguageEdges, so every one of those targets was\\nfiltered out of the candidate list.\\n\\nMeasured over a fixture whose `.c` includes an Objective-C-labelled\\nheader and an otherwise identical plain-C control:\\n\\n\\tC/renderGadget -> C/Gadget               resolved\\n\\tC/renderWidget -> Objective-C/Widget     DROPPED (same declaration)\\n\\tC/callWidth    -> Objective-C/widgetWidth DROPPED\\n\\nAdd the directional `C -> Objective-C` edge and hold it to the same\\nkind refinement the C++ edge already carries: an `@interface`, an\\n`@protocol` and a selector stay unreachable from C, which cannot even\\ndeclare them. The edge is one-way at the family level -- C still cannot\\nname a C++ class or template, nor a Swift declaration.\\n\\nTestLanguagesShareTypesRelation pinned `C -> Objective-C` as\\nimpossible; that assertion was the bug, so it moves to the symmetric\\nblock with the reason the pair is symmetric at the language level and\\nstill narrowed by kind at the candidate level.\",\"messageHeadline\":\"fix(sem): let C name the plain C half of an Objective-C header\",\"oid\":\"071afb519d8c552fac49062d531300f2e36de18a\"},{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-09-03T23:41:30Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"257360244+suhaanthayyil@users.noreply.github.com\",\"id\":\"U_kgDOD1cBdA\",\"login\":\"suhaanthayyil\",\"name\":\"Suhaan Thayyil\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-09-03T23:41:30Z\",\"messageBody\":\"A `.h` is labelled C++ the moment it holds `extern \\\"C\\\"` or a bare `::`\\n(looksLikeCPlusPlusHeader), so the dual-use header -- the one written\\nprecisely SO a `.c` can include it -- is C++, and C names no C++ target in\\ntypeSharingLanguageEdges. A `.c` including such a header reached nothing in\\nit.\\n\\nTwo defects stacked. The first sits upstream of any language rule: an\\n`extern \\\"C\\\" {` line was blanked out of the source before the C++ grammar saw\\nit, and the blanking ran forward until a line beginning `}  // extern \\\"C\\\"`\\n-- or, failing that, to END OF FILE. The canonical dual-use header closes\\nwith a bare `}` under `#ifdef __cplusplus`, so the whole header was erased\\nand contributed a single macro symbol. tree-sitter-cpp parses\\n`linkage_specification` natively, and the fixture the mask was written for\\n(TestTreeSitterParserCPlusPlusMasksDoctestMacros) parses and yields entities\\nwithout it, so the mask is removed rather than bounded.\\n\\nThe second is the reachability rule. Widening the language pair is not the\\nrepair: unlike the C -> Objective-C case, whose offending kinds are the\\nclosed set objectiveCOnlyDeclaration already names, C++ exposes templates,\\noverloads, namespaces and classes a C source genuinely cannot name, and\\n`C -> C++` wholesale would trade a missing-edge bug for a large class of\\nwrong ones. typeSharingLanguageEdges is therefore untouched --\\nlanguagesShareTypes(\\\"C\\\", \\\"C++\\\") stays false -- and the widening is per\\nDECLARATION instead: declaredWithCLinkage marks what sits inside an\\n`extern \\\"C\\\" { ... }` block, which is the construct a C++ header uses to say\\nexactly which declarations have C linkage, and candidateSharesDeclarations\\noffers C those and only those. `extern \\\"C++\\\"` is not matched.\\n\\nMeasured over a fixture whose `.c` includes a dual-use header and an\\notherwise identical plain-C control -- the same two declarations, differing\\nonly in the header that holds them:\\n\\n\\tC/renderGadget -> C/Gadget            resolved (control)\\n\\tC/renderWidget -> C++/Widget          DROPPED  (declared extern \\\"C\\\")\\n\\tC/callWidth    -> C++/widgetWidth     DROPPED  (declared extern \\\"C\\\")\\n\\nresolveTypeReference re-asked languagesShareTypes about a candidate list\\nsharedTypeCandidates had already filtered, and that duplicate silently\\noverrode the finer per-declaration answer: with the rule in place the CALLS\\nedge resolved while USES_TYPE and PARAM_TYPE on the struct beside it still\\ndid not. The redundant conjunct is dropped.\\n\\nThe narrowing is pinned beside the widening: a class, a namespaced function\\nand an overload set in the SAME header stay unreachable from C.\",\"messageHeadline\":\"fix(sem): let C name the extern \\\"C\\\" half of a dual-use C++ header\",\"oid\":\"a09defd6cd761a39199e3b3ca6d47c24e5581e6c\"},{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-09-04T00:04:29Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"257360244+suhaanthayyil@users.noreply.github.com\",\"id\":\"U_kgDOD1cBdA\",\"login\":\"suhaanthayyil\",\"name\":\"Suhaan Thayyil\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-09-04T00:04:29Z\",\"messageBody\":\"tree-sitter gives `except_clause` no fields: its exception expression, its\\n`as` target and its handler block are plain named children. The scope walker\\nasked it for a \\\"body\\\" field, got nothing, and returned -- so no call anywhere\\nin a handler was ever recorded. The function-wide local pass then asked the\\nsame clause for a \\\"name\\\" field, got its last named child (the handler BLOCK)\\ninstead, and recursed into it, declaring every name written anywhere in the\\nhandler a local of the whole function. That deleted the import-resolved edge\\nfor calls in the `try` body too, which the walker had recorded correctly.\\n`with_item` has no alias field either -- the alias hangs off a nested\\n`as_pattern` -- so the same mistake made `with compute() as handle:` report\\nits own import shadowed.\\n\\nTake the alias from the `as_pattern` that actually carries it, let the\\ngeneric child walk reach the exception expression and the handler body like\\nany other statement, and bind an exception group's `as` target as well.\\n\\nA comprehension's OUTERMOST iterable is evaluated where the comprehension is\\nwritten and passed into the comprehension's frame; only later iterables run\\ninside it. Evaluating all of them inside it made\\n`[x for x in compute() for compute in ys]` -- which CPython runs happily --\\nreport its import shadowed by a target bound afterwards. The interleaved\\nfilter `[x for x in xs if compute() for compute in ys]` stays suppressed:\\nCPython raises UnboundLocalError there, so that name really is the\\ncomprehension's own local.\\n\\nFinally, the scanners that record WHAT a local name is bound to are line\\noriented, so a parenthesised from-import spanning lines showed them only\\n`from mod import (`, an item list of one bare paren. No member name was\\nrecorded behind an alias imported that way, and the resolver -- which needs\\nthe member name, because the workspace index is keyed by it -- had nothing to\\nresolve, while the AST walker reported the call unshadowed. Rejoin such a\\nlist into one logical line, bounded, and consume nothing when it never\\ncloses, so a stray paren cannot hide the imports below it.\\n\\n`TestPythonBareImportFFIRespectsASTScopes` listed `except_type` among the\\nshadowed cases, but nothing in it rebinds the name: an `except <expr>:`\\nclause has no `as` target at all. It only looked shadowed because the clause\\nwas skipped, so it moves to the unshadowed list it belongs in.\",\"messageHeadline\":\"fix(sem): a Python try statement is code of its own scope\",\"oid\":\"20d89c019292b6ee7fa0ac8faa6187e81b8d9bd5\"},{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-09-04T00:36:10Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"257360244+suhaanthayyil@users.noreply.github.com\",\"id\":\"U_kgDOD1cBdA\",\"login\":\"suhaanthayyil\",\"name\":\"Suhaan Thayyil\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-09-04T00:36:10Z\",\"messageBody\":\"Python rebinds a `def` or `class` name only once the whole statement has\\nrun, so the parts it evaluates on the way there still read the enclosing\\nbinding. CPython is the oracle:\\n\\n    def compute(): return \\\"OUTER\\\"\\n    def compute(v=compute()): return v   # -> OUTER\\n    def compute(v=compute()): ...        # -> NameError with no outer name\\n\\nThe scope walker bound the name at the `def`/`class` line, so every\\neagerly evaluated part of the statement -- defaults, annotations, bases,\\nclass-body statements, the signatures of the methods a class defines --\\nwas told the name was already rebound. For an imported name that is the\\nfail-closed direction: the view reports no modules, importsWithName drops\\nthe file-level import binding and the resolved edge goes with it.\\n\\nA `def` now binds at its body, which keeps recursion resolving to the\\nfunction itself. A class body interleaves code that runs immediately with\\nmethod bodies that run later, which one offset cannot express, so the\\nclass keeps its binding at the `class` line for deferred code and hands\\nits bases and body a view where that one binding is hidden.\\n\\nNo emitted relation changes today: a definition also defines a same-named\\nsymbol, which wins the same-file tier (or a CONSTRUCTS relation) before\\nimports are consulted, and the generic loop skips a call whose name equals\\nits owner's. The scope view is the observable surface and is where the\\nregression tests assert.\",\"messageHeadline\":\"fix(sem): a Python definition binds its own name after its signature\",\"oid\":\"0ac5865624a1c724568c6840b69f499055152db1\"},{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-09-04T00:45:24Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"257360244+suhaanthayyil@users.noreply.github.com\",\"id\":\"U_kgDOD1cBdA\",\"login\":\"suhaanthayyil\",\"name\":\"Suhaan Thayyil\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-09-04T00:45:24Z\",\"messageBody\":\"…header\\n\\nA dual-use header -- `#ifdef __cplusplus` / `extern \\\"C\\\" {` -- is labelled C++\\nbecause looksLikeCPlusPlusHeader fires on that very marker. The C-linkage\\nexception in candidateSharesDeclarations admitted only C consumers, so an\\nObjective-C `.m` importing such a header reached nothing in it, even though\\n`__cplusplus` is undefined under Objective-C compilation and the preprocessor\\ndeletes the guards outright, leaving plain C the `.m` compiles verbatim.\\n\\nMeasured over a fixture whose `.m` imports such a header, with an otherwise\\nidentical plain-C header as the control:\\n\\n\\tObjective-C/renderGadget -> C/Gadget          resolved (control)\\n\\tObjective-C/renderWidget -> C++/Widget        DROPPED  (same declaration)\\n\\tObjective-C/callWidth    -> C++/widgetWidth   DROPPED  (same declaration)\\n\\nThe widening stays per DECLARATION, not per language: `Objective-C -> C++` is\\nstill absent from typeSharingLanguageEdges, and only what the header itself put\\nunder C linkage is offered. The class, the overload set and the namespaced\\nfunction in the same header keep cLinkage false and stay filtered out.\",\"messageHeadline\":\"fix(sem): let Objective-C name the extern \\\"C\\\" half of a dual-use C++ …\",\"oid\":\"cd7177e415137e9863238735282b07e7619974f5\"}],\"deletions\":102,\"headRefName\":\"fix/cross-language-name-only-edges\",\"headRefOid\":\"cd7177e415137e9863238735282b07e7619974f5\",\"isDraft\":false,\"mergeStateStatus\":\"UNSTABLE\",\"mergeable\":\"MERGEABLE\",\"number\":184,\"state\":\"OPEN\",\"statusCheckRollup\":[{\"__typename\":\"CheckRun\",\"completedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:45:52Z\",\"conclusion\":\"SUCCESS\",\"detailsUrl\":\"https://github.com/entireio/entire-graph/actions/runs/33823078438/job/100869770015\",\"name\":\"lint\",\"startedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:45:34Z\",\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"workflowName\":\"test\"},{\"__typename\":\"CheckRun\",\"completedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:45:53Z\",\"conclusion\":\"SUCCESS\",\"detailsUrl\":\"https://github.com/entireio/entire-graph/actions/runs/33823078438/job/100869769753\",\"name\":\"build (ubuntu-latest)\",\"startedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:45:32Z\",\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"workflowName\":\"test\"},{\"__typename\":\"CheckRun\",\"completedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:45:54Z\",\"conclusion\":\"SUCCESS\",\"detailsUrl\":\"https://github.com/entireio/entire-graph/actions/runs/33823078438/job/100869769874\",\"name\":\"build (macos-latest)\",\"startedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:45:33Z\",\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"workflowName\":\"test\"},{\"__typename\":\"CheckRun\",\"completedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:46:35Z\",\"conclusion\":\"SUCCESS\",\"detailsUrl\":\"https://github.com/entireio/entire-graph/actions/runs/33823078438/job/100869769794\",\"name\":\"build (windows-latest)\",\"startedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:45:33Z\",\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"workflowName\":\"test\"},{\"__typename\":\"CheckRun\",\"completedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:48:13Z\",\"conclusion\":\"SUCCESS\",\"detailsUrl\":\"https://github.com/entireio/entire-graph/actions/runs/33823078438/job/100869769749\",\"name\":\"test (ubuntu-latest)\",\"startedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:45:32Z\",\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"workflowName\":\"test\"},{\"__typename\":\"CheckRun\",\"completedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:52:00Z\",\"conclusion\":\"SUCCESS\",\"detailsUrl\":\"https://github.com/entireio/entire-graph/actions/runs/33823078438/job/100869769885\",\"name\":\"test (macos-latest)\",\"startedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:45:34Z\",\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"workflowName\":\"test\"},{\"__typename\":\"CheckRun\",\"completedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:47:14Z\",\"conclusion\":\"SUCCESS\",\"detailsUrl\":\"https://github.com/entireio/entire-graph/actions/runs/33823078438/job/100869769891\",\"name\":\"test (windows-latest) / compile and plan\",\"startedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:45:52Z\",\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"workflowName\":\"test\"},{\"__typename\":\"CheckRun\",\"completedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:46:42Z\",\"conclusion\":\"SUCCESS\",\"detailsUrl\":\"https://github.com/entireio/entire-graph/actions/runs/33823078438/job/100869770012\",\"name\":\"test (windows-latest) / vet\",\"startedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:45:33Z\",\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"workflowName\":\"test\"},{\"__typename\":\"CheckRun\",\"completedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:45:44Z\",\"conclusion\":\"SUCCESS\",\"detailsUrl\":\"https://github.com/entireio/entire-graph/actions/runs/33823078438/job/100869769847\",\"name\":\"bm25-adapter\",\"startedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:45:33Z\",\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"workflowName\":\"test\"},{\"__typename\":\"CheckRun\",\"completedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:45:55Z\",\"conclusion\":\"SUCCESS\",\"detailsUrl\":\"https://github.com/entireio/entire-graph/actions/runs/33823078438/job/100869769852\",\"name\":\"statusline (ubuntu-latest)\",\"startedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:45:33Z\",\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"workflowName\":\"test\"},{\"__typename\":\"CheckRun\",\"completedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:46:17Z\",\"conclusion\":\"SUCCESS\",\"detailsUrl\":\"https://github.com/entireio/entire-graph/actions/runs/33823078438/job/100869769632\",\"name\":\"statusline (macos-latest)\",\"startedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:45:33Z\",\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"workflowName\":\"test\"},{\"__typename\":\"CheckRun\",\"completedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:49:30Z\",\"conclusion\":\"SUCCESS\",\"detailsUrl\":\"https://github.com/entireio/entire-graph/actions/runs/33823078438/job/100870118749\",\"name\":\"test (windows-latest) / shard 0\",\"startedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:47:16Z\",\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"workflowName\":\"test\"},{\"__typename\":\"CheckRun\",\"completedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:49:54Z\",\"conclusion\":\"SUCCESS\",\"detailsUrl\":\"https://github.com/entireio/entire-graph/actions/runs/33823078438/job/100870118803\",\"name\":\"test (windows-latest) / shard 1\",\"startedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:47:16Z\",\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"workflowName\":\"test\"},{\"__typename\":\"CheckRun\",\"completedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:50:44Z\",\"conclusion\":\"SUCCESS\",\"detailsUrl\":\"https://github.com/entireio/entire-graph/actions/runs/33823078438/job/100870118706\",\"name\":\"test (windows-latest) / shard 2\",\"startedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:47:16Z\",\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"workflowName\":\"test\"},{\"__typename\":\"CheckRun\",\"completedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:49:34Z\",\"conclusion\":\"SUCCESS\",\"detailsUrl\":\"https://github.com/entireio/entire-graph/actions/runs/33823078438/job/100870118818\",\"name\":\"test (windows-latest) / shard 3\",\"startedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:47:16Z\",\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"workflowName\":\"test\"},{\"__typename\":\"CheckRun\",\"completedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:50:12Z\",\"conclusion\":\"SUCCESS\",\"detailsUrl\":\"https://github.com/entireio/entire-graph/actions/runs/33823078438/job/100870118870\",\"name\":\"test (windows-latest) / shard 4\",\"startedAt\":\"2026-09-04T00:47:16Z\",\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"workflowName\