{"timestamp":"2026-09-04T01:03:02.227Z","ordinal":25083,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"agent_message","id":"amsg_01a069f0-c653-7cb2-aa29-9ca76e0dabe1","author":"/root/pr188_final_terra","recipient":"/root","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Message Type: FINAL_ANSWER\nTask name: /root\nSender: /root/pr188_final_terra\nPayload:\nFindings:\n\n1. **P1 — pre-definition view masks later same-name writes, including walruses.**  \n   [python_scopes.go:624](/Users/thomi/Projects/entire-graph/.entire/worktrees/pr184-review/internal/sem/python_scopes.go:624) hides every event for the class name from the class’s start onward (`>=`), not just the synthetic class-definition binding. Thus a later base-expression walrus is hidden from the remainder of the base list and class body.\n\n   Reproduction confirmed in CPython:\n\n   ```python\n   compute = dict\n   class compute((compute := list)):\n       value = compute()\n   assert compute.__bases__ == (list,)\n   assert compute.value == []\n   ```\n\n   The final `compute()` is the walrus-bound `list`, not the prior/imported `compute`; the current analyzer walks it through `pre` and incorrectly reaches the earlier import. Smallest fix: hide only the class-definition event (its exact offset/identity), not later events. With the current representation, equality on the definition offset is sufficient; add a regression that imports `compute`, rebinds it with a same-named walrus in a base, then calls it in the class body.\n\n2. **P1 — deferred lambdas and generator bodies in a class still use the pre-definition scope.**  \n   [python_scopes.go:313](/Users/thomi/Projects/entire-graph/.entire/worktrees/pr184-review/internal/sem/python_scopes.go:313) and [python_scopes.go:395](/Users/thomi/Projects/entire-graph/.entire/worktrees/pr184-review/internal/sem/python_scopes.go:395) parent those child scopes at the class scope; [lexicalParent](/Users/thomi/Projects/entire-graph/.entire/worktrees/pr184-review/internal/sem/python_scopes.go:213) then skips to `parent`, ignoring `deferred`. Unlike a method, a lambda/generator invoked after class creation must see the completed enclosing class binding.\n\n   CPython reproductions:\n\n   ```python\n   def compute(): return \"old\"\n   class compute:\n       thunk = lambda: compute()\n   assert isinstance(compute.thunk(), compute)\n   ```\n\n   ```python\n   def compute(): return \"old\"\n   class compute:\n       generated = (compute() for _ in range(1))\n   assert isinstance(next(compute.generated), compute)\n   ```\n\n   Current scope resolution instead falls through the hidden pre-class view and can emit an imported `compute` edge. Add lambda and generator-expression regression cases; preserve the existing eager first-iterable behavior for comprehensions.\n\nThe current added focused test passes, but it does not cover either case:\n\n`go test ./internal/sem -run '^TestPythonDefinitionNameBindsOnlyAfterItsOwnSignature$' -count=1`"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a0690a-a093-7352-9a6c-ed6ea1d6d3c0","create_time":1788483782.227193}}}
