{"timestamp":"2026-09-04T01:12:26.120Z","ordinal":25304,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"function_call_output","id":"fco_01a069f9-6108-7d61-980e-e3cbb5609a15","call_id":"call_QfXvLMoJmLjZWc3yL41f1DoR","output":"{\"agents\":[{\"agent_name\":\"/root\",\"agent_status\":\"running\"},{\"agent_name\":\"/root/julia_binding_ast_design\",\"agent_status\":{\"completed\":\"Verdict: #190 does not sacrifice working include/import/overload support—#161 at `b45fd112` does not correctly provide those features.\\n\\n| Feature | #161 `b45fd112` | #190 `3bc54590` |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| Module-body callers | Yes, via heuristic child masking | No correct module-body isolation |\\n| Unique same-file module calls | Yes | Yes |\\n| Real include/cross-file scopes | No; positive test passes via global-uniqueness fallback | No; same inherited fallback |\\n| `import` / `using` / qualified calls | No Julia support | No Julia support |\\n| Nested-module direct methods | Yes | Yes |\\n| Nested/local function callers | Yes, but also double-attributes inner calls to outer functions | Refuses them |\\n| Inner-constructor callers | Yes, using nearest-module ancestry | Refuses container mismatch |\\n| Overloads | Incorrectly chooses the lexically nearest overload | Emits no edge |\\n| Macro body calls | Supported | Supported by the exact-source/same-container path |\\n| Short/long callable forms | Yes | Yes |\\n| One-line ownership | Broad but still buggy and line-granular | Exact symbol spans, safer |\\n| Local/generator shadowing | Missing; produces false module edges | AST-based conservative suppression |\\n\\nThe defensible working features lost with #190 are:\\n\\n- Direct module-body CALLS.\\n- Inner-constructor-to-module CALLS.\\n- Nested/local-function-to-module CALLS.\\n\\nMajor #161 gaps verified from code and later regression tests:\\n\\n- Its include test is accidental: it never parses `include`, and included-file callers cannot see back into the including module.\\n- Parameters, assignments, `let`/`for`/`catch`/`do`/generator binders can falsely resolve to module methods.\\n- Multiple dispatch selects one nearest-line method as an exact target.\\n- One-line parameterized generators and short definitions with trailing module code remain misattributed; later commit `d53e1853` added exact-span regressions for both.\\n- `!`-suffixed definition masks fail because `\\\\b` follows a non-word suffix.\\n- Nested callable bodies are scanned as part of their outer callable.\\n- Imports, exports, aliases, qualified calls, include ancestry, and dispatch identity only appear in the later multi-thousand-line rewrite—not at `b45fd112`.\\n\\n#190’s remaining costs are conservative false negatives: whole-callable shadow suppression, nested-callable refusal, and the exact-head `global_statement` binding bug.\"}},{\"agent_name\":\"/root/next_pr143_readiness\",\"agent_status\":{\"completed\":\"PR #143 / Trail #103 should be next.\\n\\nIt is mergeable but 124 commits behind current `main`, so it needs a refresh and fresh gate run first. Its normal CI is green; blockers are the failed Entire Gate, trail still in `building`, and no trail approval. There are no current open trail findings.\\n\\nPR #184 is smaller but conflicting, 151 commits behind, and needs conflict resolution plus a new semantic review.\"}},{\"agent_name\":\"/root/next_security_141_142\",\"agent_status\":{\"completed\":\"Ranked recommendation: take PR #184 / Trail #142 next.\\n\\n- PR #184 is the smallest and most focused: 4 files, +1,066/−59, 11 commits. Its head is 22 commits behind current `main`, with `MERGEABLE=CONFLICTING`/`DIRTY`; refresh should be moderate because it touches `internal/sem/langcompat.go` and `provider.go`, which may overlap recent main/provider work. CI (lint, all builds/tests, adapters/statusline) is green; only Entire Gates fails.\\n- Trail status for #184: the latest visible review reported one open/current Medium finding (`01M17JEVQRXM`, Clojure `.cljc` vs `.clj` distinction), which appears addressed by later commits (`b941de3b`, corrected by `ea278b94`). The current head has since accumulated additional language-compatibility fixes, so it needs a fresh trail query/review rather than assuming closure. Estimated refresh + focused review: low-to-moderate.\\n\\nPR #141 / Trail #101 is second:\\n\\n- 38 commits, 50 files, +6,393/−192; head is currently 0 commits behind the PR’s recorded base but that base is old (`7b3252aa`), and GitHub reports conflicting/dirty against current `main`.\\n- Its trail has multiple still-open/current findings, including the deterministic filesystem-order sample tradeoff (`01M19H825J37`) explicitly left for maintainer/product direction, plus a separable warning-rendering gap. Refresh/re-review cost is high.\\n\\nPR #142 / Trail #102 is last:\\n\\n- Largest: 45 commits, 60 files, +6,968/−400; also conflicts/dirty against the old base.\\n- Trail still showed an open/current Medium finding at `internal/sem/search_cache.go:849` (`01M19NFGBY1W`), with a proposed remedy that was deliberately not applied; another test seam was failing during review. Refresh/review cost is highest, and it overlaps broad provider/parser/budget changes.\\n\\nIn short: #184 is the practical next candidate, but refresh it onto current `main`, then re-query Trail #142 before approval. Avoid #141/#142 until their unresolved product-level findings are explicitly adjudicated.\"}},{\"agent_name\":\"/root/pr153_remote_monitor/trail148_snapshot\",\"agent_status\":{\"completed\":\"Read-only snapshot at GitHub API time `2026-09-03T09:54:03Z`:\\n\\n- Trail #148: `open / building`; branch `codex/julia-module-bare-calls-minimal`; base `main`; updated `2026-09-03T09:50:05.924Z`; body timestamp `2026-09-03T09:49:45.955473Z`.\\n- Approvals: none.\\n- Findings query (`status=any`, `freshness=any`, dismissed included): Open 1, Resolved 0, Dismissed 0, Stale 0. The sole finding is medium/current/open at `internal/sem/provider.go:2080`, ID `01M1KASX35X7H32TMGXQKRH44M`, review `01M1KASX30QZ2AJSVAWQBA0JRD`, code version `01M1KASX2WPW6QR3SSW42H8HEN`. It reports that non-parameter local bindings can still create incorrect same-named module CALLS edges.\\n- PR #190 head: `refs/heads/codex/julia-module-bare-calls-minimal` at `dc616063cea1df3b5e449cb0ed4b38570146d214`; base `main` at `d292e1a3b5d32a43da1a021e8acb5d7264c330a9`.\\n- Checks: 22 total; 16 completed successfully, 6 in progress with null conclusions. Pending: macOS tests, Windows shards 2/3/7, Cursor Bugbot, Entire Gates.\\n- Entire Gates run `100602470694`: in progress; “Gates are evaluating.” Approvals failed, checks pending, findings passed, and up-to-date passed.\\n- Entire reads exposed the exact code-version ID above, but no checkpoint ID/SHA. GitHub Gates binds Trail ULID `01M1KARA1RH7D7FA3A6GJ82MM6` to the exact PR head SHA.\\n\\nNo files, git state, Trail state, or GitHub state were changed.\"}},{\"agent_name\":\"/root/pr153_remote_monitor/trail148_snapshot/github_pr190\",\"agent_status\":\"interrupted\"},{\"agent_name\":\"/root/pr161_readonly_preaudit\",\"agent_status\":\"interrupted\"},{\"agent_name\":\"/root/pr161_readonly_preaudit/dataflow_cache_audit\",\"agent_status\":\"interrupted\"},{\"agent_name\":\"/root/pr161_readonly_preaudit/scope_import_audit\",\"agent_status\":\"interrupted\"},{\"agent_name\":\"/root/pr184_ffi_shadow_tradeoff\",\"agent_status\":{\"completed\":\"Read-only checkpoint for PR184’s Python bare-import FFI shadowing.\\n\\nCurrent mechanism:\\n\\n- `resolveCallTargetsWithRawImport` receives language-filtered candidates plus the raw workspace index.\\n- `resolveUniqueRawImportedCallTarget` then allows a unique import-path match across languages.\\n- Python imports are collected file-wide by regex; only formal parameters are currently recognized as shadowing.\\n- The call scanner keeps only names, not call-site byte spans or lexical scope.\\n\\nRecommendation: implement a small Python AST-derived function-scope binding guard, without replacing the call resolver.\\n\\nFor each Python callable, parse the existing tree-sitter tree once and collect names bound in that callable’s lexical scope. Before the raw FFI fallback, suppress the fallback when the imported name is locally bound. Collect:\\n\\n- parameters\\n- assignment / annotated / augmented-assignment targets\\n- `for` targets\\n- `with ... as` targets\\n- `except ... as` targets\\n- walrus targets\\n- local `import` / `from ... import`\\n- direct nested `def` / `class` names\\n- `global` / `nonlocal` names conservatively as blockers when combined with rebinding\\n\\nSkip nested function/lambda/class bodies while collecting the enclosing function’s direct bindings; collect their scopes separately. Reuse existing `parameterBindingNames` / identifier-descendant helpers for destructuring. Match callable AST ranges to existing `SymbolRecord` byte ranges.\\n\\nRepresentative behavior:\\n\\n| Case | Current raw FFI | Function-scope AST guard |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| Module import, unshadowed function call | Preserves C edge | Preserves C edge |\\n| Local assignment (`compute = ...`) | False C edge | Suppresses |\\n| Parameter | Already suppressed | Suppresses |\\n| `global compute` with later assignment | False/ambiguous | Suppress conservatively |\\n| `nonlocal compute` | False import binding possible | Suppresses |\\n| Nested `def` with local `compute` | Scope leakage can create false edge | Separate nested scope; suppress only nested call |\\n| `for`, `with`, `except` target | False C edge | Suppresses |\\n| Walrus (`compute := ...`) | False C edge | Suppresses |\\n| Local import | File-wide import map can misbind | Suppresses local scope |\\n| Nested function with no local binding using module-level import | Should preserve explicit import | Preserve if import-scope tracking is retained |\\n\\nApproximate cost: 150–300 LOC plus focused tests, assuming only a binding guard and existing regex call scanner remain. This is a modest new parser/scope pass, but not a full call-resolution rewrite.\\n\\nCheaper regex/block-wide suppression (~40–100 LOC) is not safe enough: it can detect simple assignments but mishandles destructuring, nested functions, local imports, and lexical scope; it also suppresses valid outer calls when a nested function shadows the name.\\n\\nA fully call-site-accurate Python scope resolver—AST calls plus import/binding spans—is the most correct option, but likely 400–800+ LOC and unnecessary for this PR.\\n\\nOne important boundary: keep the guard Python-specific. The current raw-import fallback is also used in the generic path for JS/TS; applying Python binding logic globally would alter unrelated import behavior.\"}},{\"agent_name\":\"/root/pr184_python_binding_options\",\"agent_status\":{\"completed\":\"No small reusable Python local-binding signal exists.\\n\\nCheapest safe option: disable the raw FFI fallback for Python callable symbols; retain it only for top-level file calls. Estimated 3–6 production LOC, 35–55 test LOC.\\n\\nLoss: valid bare FFI calls inside Python functions/methods/nested/async callables, including function-local imports. Top-level bare calls and dotted calls such as `frobnicate.compute()` remain.\\n\\nA selective, semantics-preserving solution needs a per-file Python AST scope scan: roughly 180–260 production LOC and 180–300 test LOC.\"}},{\"agent_name\":\"/root/pr184_remote_race_semantic\",\"agent_status\":{\"completed\":\"Sent the minimal AST-scope port design and targeted regression matrix to the parent.\"}},{\"agent_name\":\"/root/pr184_ubuntu_ci\",\"agent_status\":\"pending_init\"},{\"agent_name\":\"/root/pr188_ci_monitor\",\"agent_status\":{\"completed\":\"Cleanup completed successfully.\\n\\n- Removed only the registered review worktree.\\n- Removed its now-empty parent directory.\\n- Primary worktree is clean.\\n- Fetched `origin` and fast-forwarded `main`.\\n- `HEAD`, local `main`, and `origin/main` all point to:\\n  `9cdc0592352da8987ee727d8e1cfd0bf871ae4a5`\\n- PR 188 remains merged.\\n- Trail 146 remains merged.\"}},{\"agent_name\":\"/root/pr188_final_luna\",\"agent_status\":\"running\"},{\"agent_name\":\"/root/pr188_final_terra\",\"agent_status\":{\"completed\":\"Findings:\\n\\n1. **P1 — the new comprehension-capture regression test already fails.**  \\n   [call_resolution_precision_test.go:712](/Users/thomi/Projects/entire-graph/.entire/worktrees/pr184-review/internal/sem/call_resolution_precision_test.go:712) fails with `got []string{\\\"frobnicate\\\"}`.\\n\\n   The new boundary clone preserves the enclosing comprehension’s bindings, so it does not itself discard the loop variable. But [walkComprehension](/Users/thomi/Projects/entire-graph/.entire/worktrees/pr184-review/internal/sem/python_scopes.go:429) records the `for compute` target at its textual offset, after the lambda body’s textual `compute()` offset. `contextAt` consequently treats the lambda’s captured loop binding as not yet bound, though Python binds it before evaluating the comprehension result.\\n\\n   Focused test result:\\n   `TestPythonDefinitionNameBindsOnlyAfterItsOwnSignature/a_lambda_nested_in_a_class-body_comprehension_keeps_its_local_binding` fails.\\n\\n2. **P1 — nested generator expressions under a class-body comprehension still use the pre-class scope.**  \\n   [python_scopes.go:407](/Users/thomi/Projects/entire-graph/.entire/worktrees/pr184-review/internal/sem/python_scopes.go:407) switches to `deferred` only when a generator’s immediate parent is a class. For:\\n\\n   ```python\\n   from frobnicate import compute\\n\\n   class compute:\\n       values = [(compute() for _ in ()) for _ in range(1)]\\n   ```\\n\\n   the generator is created by the eager list comprehension but its body runs after class creation, so its `compute()` must resolve to the completed class, not the import. Its immediate parent is the list-comprehension scope, so the current code leaves the pre-definition chain intact and emits the import edge.\\n\\nCPython confirms both the loop-variable capture and deferred nested-generator semantics.\"}},{\"agent_name\":\"/root/pr190_ast_guard_review\",\"agent_status\":{\"completed\":\"#190 is a narrow, conservative fallback—not a Julia resolver rewrite.\\n\\n- Actual base is `origin/main` at `d292e1a3`; local `main` is stale. Graph diff: 4 files, +477/−17.\\n- It emits exact CALLS only for one unique, same-file, same-immediate-module Julia method called by a non-local module method.\\n- It rejects parameters, local binders, overload ambiguity, nested callables, sibling modules, cross-file methods, qualified calls, module bodies, macros, inner constructors, and nested callers.\\n- It preserves main’s generic function CALLS, external/import fallbacks when merely declining the additive path, and same-name struct `CONSTRUCTS`.\\n\\nAt `3bc54590`, two deliberate/known false-negative areas remain:\\n\\n- Callable-wide binding suppression loses valid calls outside a nested shadowing scope.\\n- `global helper = helper` is incorrectly treated as local and loses the module-method edge.\\n\\n#190 behavior absent from #161 includes:\\n\\n1. Parameter and lexical-binding shadow protection.\\n2. Fail-closed overload ambiguity instead of nearest-definition guessing.\\n3. Nested-callable outer-attribution protection.\\n4. Same-name constructor preservation.\\n5. AST parse-failure/timeout safety for the additive fallback.\\n6. Parser byte-span isolation rather than #161’s line-based textual masking.\\n\\n#161 has broader recall that #190 intentionally omits: module-body calls, macro bodies, nested functions, inner constructors, and broader module/cross-file handling.\\n\\nExact-head focused tests passed, and `git diff --check origin/main...3bc54590` was clean. No state was changed.\"}},{\"agent_name\":\"/root/recover_original_pr_order\",\"agent_status\":{\"completed\":\"The exact current session is `01a0616d-0c18-7443-82f2-b4c5bc498542`; recent checkpoints include:\\n\\n- `9647ef5f2a08` / `5cb1e49f…` — branch `codex/pr161-review`, 2026-09-03 03:45Z.\\n- `2fa86cd65bcd` / `dafa31a5…` — same #161 review chain, 07:39Z.\\n- `3ee465863b3f` — the session’s current “Pull main / open PR” review checkpoint.\\n- `5077bd01eee6` / `0b1aad48…` — branch `codex/julia-module-bare-calls-minimal`, 10:32Z.\\n- `3333797990ff` — later continuation on `fix144`, 12:41Z.\\n\\nSearch confirms the current handled chain centers on **#161 → #190 → #188** (with #189/#187 in the surrounding merge wave), but the historical index did not return the full “Sort your table by suggested merge timing” table text itself. I therefore cannot safely reconstruct every row/order beyond that exact chain from evidence returned.\\n\\nConfirmed merge commits in the current wave:\\n\\n- **#190** — `3129db620420…`, merged 2026-09-03 11:47Z.\\n- **#187** — `19644107b6d…`, merged 2026-09-03 13:17Z.\\n- **#188** — `9cdc0592352d…`, merged 2026-09-03 15:40Z.\\n- **#189** — `d292e1a3b5d…`, merged 2026-09-03 06:27Z.\\n\\nThere is no `Merge pull request #161` result; #161 appears to have been reviewed/worked in the chain, but not merged.\"}}]}","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a0690a-a093-7352-9a6c-ed6ea1d6d3c0","create_time":1788484346.120751}}}
