{"timestamp":"2026-09-04T01:19:19.746Z","ordinal":25428,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"agent_message","id":"amsg_01a069ff-b0c2-7912-aae9-c4960be004b2","author":"/root/pr188_final_terra","recipient":"/root","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Message Type: FINAL_ANSWER\nTask name: /root\nSender: /root/pr188_final_terra\nPayload:\n**P1 — eager list/set/dict comprehensions in a method header now skip the required pre-class view.**\n\n[python_scopes.go:227](/Users/thomi/Projects/entire-graph/.entire/worktrees/pr184-review/internal/sem/python_scopes.go:227) routes every nested header scope through `lexicalContainer()`, which now follows `classScope.deferred` to the completed outer scope. That is correct for a lambda body and a generator body, but wrong for an eager list/set/dict comprehension evaluated as a method default.\n\nReproduction:\n\n```python\nseen = []\ndef compute():\n    seen.append(\"import\")\n    return object()\n\nclass compute:\n    def method(self, value=[compute() for _ in range(1)]):\n        return value\n\nassert seen == [\"import\"]\n```\n\nThe comprehension runs while the class statement is still being evaluated, before `compute` is rebound to the class. The walker instead gives its `compute()` the completed module scope, where the class event hides the import. Add a method-symbol scope regression expecting `frobnicate` for this fixture; retain the completed-scope behavior only for deferred lambda/generator bodies.\n\nThe focused existing test passes, but does not cover this case."}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a0690a-a093-7352-9a6c-ed6ea1d6d3c0","create_time":1788484759.74672}}}
