{"timestamp":"2026-09-04T01:41:24.541Z","ordinal":25857,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"agent_message","id":"amsg_01a06a13-e7bd-7f53-bfb7-a61e3eded15a","author":"/root/pr188_final_terra","recipient":"/root","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Message Type: FINAL_ANSWER\nTask name: /root\nSender: /root/pr188_final_terra\nPayload:\nP1 — `pythonFromImportRE` rejects valid punctuation immediately after `import` at [provider.go:20452](/Users/thomi/Projects/entire-graph/.entire/worktrees/pr184-review/internal/sem/provider.go:20452).\n\nReproduced with CPython:\n\n```python\nfrom math import\\\n sin\nfrom math import*\n```\n\nBoth are legal. The new regex permits only horizontal whitespace or `(` after `import`, so it drops both statements. Consequences:\n\n- `from math import\\\\\\n sin` loses the `sin → math` binding in the shared scope/AST path.\n- `from mod import*` is never recognized, so `scanPythonImports` loses module `mod`, despite correctly exposing no bare binding.\n\nCurrent “star import binds no name” test passes even when parsing fails, so it does not constrain this behavior. The tab/parenthesized FFI tests do exercise shared paths, but no test covers backslash continuation or proves star-module capture.\n\nRecommendation: port remote `9b60f3d1`’s identifier-boundary parser (rather than further narrowing the regex), preserving invalid `from pkg importcompute` rejection. Add assertions that backslash-continuation resolves `sin`, and that star import adds `mod` to scanned imports while creating no name binding."}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a0690a-a093-7352-9a6c-ed6ea1d6d3c0","create_time":1788486084.541149}}}
