{"timestamp":"2026-09-04T01:45:08.911Z","ordinal":25921,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"agent_message","id":"amsg_01a06a17-542f-7512-b0f2-7d885a7f5589","author":"/root/pr188_final_terra","recipient":"/root","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Message Type: FINAL_ANSWER\nTask name: /root\nSender: /root/pr188_final_terra\nPayload:\nP1 — legal no-whitespace relative imports are rejected by the greedy module capture at [provider.go:20452](/Users/thomi/Projects/entire-graph/.entire/worktrees/pr184-review/internal/sem/provider.go:20452).\n\nCPython accepts all of these:\n\n```python\nfrom .import x\nfrom ..import x\nfrom .import*\n```\n\nBut the regex captures `.import` / `..import` as the module, leaving only the item text; `pythonFromImportParts` then fails its required `import` prefix check. The shared AST/scope and line scanners therefore omit these relative imports entirely.\n\nThe remote `9b60f3d1` boundary search avoids this by locating the `import` keyword rather than greedily consuming a dotted module prefix. The current checks correctly reject `from pkg.import x` and `importcompute`; Unicode item names, comments, and backslash continuations appear fine.\n\nAdd regressions asserting:\n\n- `from .import x` and `from ..import x` produce the expected relative module/name bindings.\n- `from .import*` yields the relative module in `scanPythonImports` while binding no name.\n\nThe focused existing tests pass, but they do not cover these relative forms."}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a0690a-a093-7352-9a6c-ed6ea1d6d3c0","create_time":1788486308.911105}}}
