{"timestamp":"2026-09-04T02:15:54.202Z","ordinal":26313,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"agent_message","id":"amsg_01a06a33-7c5a-7071-b801-4f59ed6882b1","author":"/root/pr188_final_terra","recipient":"/root","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Message Type: FINAL_ANSWER\nTask name: /root\nSender: /root/pr188_final_terra\nPayload:\nP1 — port the async/data-flow widening, but do **not** cherry-pick `d80db146`’s resolver refactor wholesale.\n\nLocal HEAD already has the necessary Python call-site scope data:\n\n- `genericImportModuleSets` / `genericImportModules` for unaliased imports visible at a callable.\n- `genericCallAllowed` to prevent a module import or `from … import member as alias` from falling through to an unrelated same-named symbol.\n- `importContexts` plus `resolvePythonAliasImportTargets` for aliases.\n\nIt simply does not use that data in the `ASYNC_CALLS` and `DATA_FLOWS` loops; those still call `resolveCallTargets(... importsByName ...)`, so unique Python→C imported calls lose their sibling relations.\n\n`d80db146` correctly fixes ordinary unaliased calls, ambiguity suppression, and function-local-import isolation by using scoped bindings in the sibling loops. Its tests cover those direct spellings. But its alias replacement is incomplete and regresses current safety:\n\n```python\nfrom frobnicate import compute as c\nasync def run():\n    return await c(1)\n```\n\nThe remote binder tries `c` first. If an unrelated workspace symbol named `c` exists, normal fallback can resolve that coincidental symbol; because `targets` is then nonempty, its later `pythonAliasImportCallTargets(compute, …)` is skipped. Current HEAD avoids this through `genericCallAllowed == false`, then resolves only via the original member. The same omission means remote async/data-flow scans do not resolve aliases when no conflicting `c` exists.\n\nSmall safe port:\n\n1. Add a narrow shared binding helper for the three call scan loops, preserving current semantics:\n   - non-Python: raw candidates + normal import map;\n   - complete Python scope: `genericImportModuleSets`, `genericImportModules`, `importsWithName`, and `genericCallAllowed`;\n   - incomplete Python scope: disable raw widening, preserving existing fail-closed behavior.\n2. Use it in `ASYNC_CALLS` and `DATA_FLOWS` with `resolveCallTargetsWithRawImport`.\n3. When Python generic resolution is disallowed, skip normal/name-only resolution; then reuse the existing alias resolver and union its targets, exactly as the CALLS loop does.\n4. Leave the current alias/external fallback machinery intact; do not take remote’s `pythonImportBindings` replacement or its unrelated `langcompat.go` comment-only churn.\n\nRequired tests:\n\n- Existing remote direct C-import async and data-flow positive, C/Ruby ambiguity negative, and same-language Python precedence.\n- Import local to one function does not resolve an unrelated function’s async/flow call.\n- `from frobnicate import compute as c`: async and flow resolve C; a conflicting workspace `c` must not win.\n- Local rebinding, late same-function assignment, and local function/class definition of `compute` suppress async/flow FFI widening.\n- Existing class/definition-timing cases should remain scoped identically to CALLS.\n\nExecuted `entire graph commit d80db146 --json` first as requested; it confirms this commit changes the two sibling scans and tests, while `langcompat.go` is documentation-only. Existing local FFI positive/local-import-isolation tests pass."}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a0690a-a093-7352-9a6c-ed6ea1d6d3c0","create_time":1788488154.202469}}}
