{"timestamp":"2026-09-04T02:30:30.645Z","ordinal":26532,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"agent_message","id":"amsg_01a06a40-dbf5-7eb3-bcc7-28344793e834","author":"/root/pr188_final_terra","recipient":"/root","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Message Type: FINAL_ANSWER\nTask name: /root\nSender: /root/pr188_final_terra\nPayload:\nFunctionally superseded — no production port needed.\n\nLocal `35ee4247` already resolves aliased imports for both `ASYNC_CALLS` and `DATA_FLOWS`, including the original-member lookup. It is safer than `ceef245b` for alias conflicts: `genericCallAllowed` suppresses the ordinary coincidental-name fallback before `resolvePythonAliasImportTargets` resolves the member. The remote refactor can still let an unrelated workspace `c` win before its alias fallback runs.\n\nLocal behavior covers:\n\n- Aliased async and data-flow C targets.\n- Alias conflict safety (async test with a competing `run`).\n- Call-site scope isolation and raw-import ambiguity through the existing scoped resolver.\n- Both relation types reuse the same alias resolver, so target/resolution/confidence provenance remain aligned with CALLS.\n\nRemote test coverage not yet duplicated locally:\n\n- C/Ruby ambiguity specifically behind an alias, for both relation types.\n- Module alias (`import frobnicate as m`) emits no async/flow edge.\n- Function-local alias does not leak to a sibling.\n- Full alias-vs-unaliased metadata parity, including both data-flow directions.\n\nThose are test-only additions; I found no missing behavior or required code delta. Focused local scoped-import and FFI tests pass."}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a0690a-a093-7352-9a6c-ed6ea1d6d3c0","create_time":1788489030.645736}}}
