{"timestamp":"2026-09-04T03:06:40.794Z","ordinal":26975,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"agent_message","id":"amsg_01a06a61-f91a-7f43-aa99-793e05ad9d23","author":"/root/pr184_relative_import_fix","recipient":"/root","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Message Type: FINAL_ANSWER\nTask name: /root\nSender: /root/pr184_relative_import_fix\nPayload:\nRead-only snapshot complete.\n\nTrail #142:\n\n- URL: https://entire.io/gh/entireio/entire-graph/trails/142\n- Branch: `fix/cross-language-name-only-edges`\n- Base: `main`\n- Status: `open`\n- Phase: `building`\n- Head/code version: not exposed by `trail show`\n- Assignee/author: `suhaanthayyil`\n- Findings: 3 open, 48 resolved, 5 dismissed; 54 stale, 2 current\n- Approvals: none\n\nExact Trail body:\n\n> Problem  \n> The workspace-wide short-name index is keyed only by symbol name. Global fallback resolution could attach type, call, inheritance, constructor, field-access, or interface-method edges to same-named declarations from incompatible languages. Foreign declarations could also create false ambiguity that suppressed a legitimate compatible edge.  \n>\n> Solution  \n> Apply a directional declaration-compatibility policy before global name-only resolution. Preserve same-language resolution and supported sharing across shells, JVM languages, Clojure dialects, JavaScript/TypeScript, CLR languages, Swift/Objective-C, and directional C-family interoperability. Refine C++ consumption of Objective-C-labelled files to declarations that are actually plain-C compatible, and Objective-C consumption of Swift to declarations exposed through Objective-C interoperability.  \n>\n> Filter Java constructed receivers, Go qualified receivers, and every constructor/method-return chain hop using the originating caller language. Keep same-file and direct package evidence unchanged. TESTS edges remain naming conventions rather than type interoperability.  \n>\n> Permit explicit import-path evidence to cross an FFI boundary only when it identifies exactly one callable target. This applies to receiver calls and bare imported calls; ambiguous module matches remain external. For Python bare imports, a tree-sitter scope pass tracks call positions, ordered imports and reimports, parameters, assignments and destructuring, loops, with/except targets, walrus bindings, local imports, nested functions/classes/lambdas, global/nonlocal declarations, class lexical boundaries, and ordered comprehension scopes. The pass is computed once per file and fails closed for raw cross-language fallback on parse errors, timeouts, or depth truncation while preserving ordinary import resolution.  \n>\n> Correctness invariants  \n> No workspace-global name-only candidate creates a type-style semantic edge unless the source language can name the target declaration.  \n> Filtering occurs before ambiguity decisions, so incompatible collisions cannot hide a compatible target.  \n> Compatibility is reflexive but not globally symmetric or transitive.  \n> ClojureScript accepts portable .cljc declarations but not JVM-only .clj declarations; portable .cljc consumers may reference either dialect.  \n> Unique explicit import evidence may establish an FFI call; zero or multiple import-path matches never fan out into concrete foreign targets.  \n> Python lexical shadowing and import order cannot manufacture a raw FFI edge, and incomplete scope analysis disables only the raw exception.  \n> TESTS relations continue to work across language boundaries.  \n>\n> Verification  \n> Focused tests cover incompatible and compatible type/call targets, ambiguity restoration, directional C-family declaration visibility, Clojure portability, cross-language TESTS, Java/Go/return-chain receiver resolution, unique and ambiguous receiver/bare FFI imports, Python lexical and temporal scope behavior, malformed/deep fail-closed behavior, and ordinary import preservation. The focused semantic matrices and diff checks pass locally; GitHub CI and Entire Gates must pass on the final head.  \n>\n> Non-goals  \n> This change does not alter the graph schema, make compatibility globally symmetric, resolve ambiguous FFI imports, introduce general Python call-site attribution beyond guarding the raw import exception, or change unrelated same-file, package, dotted-call, and test-subject behavior.\n\nFindings (`ID | severity | status | freshness | path | summary`):\n\n```text\n01M1N4BNVX22B5ZT2C3EHMKJ4E | medium | open | stale | internal/sem/provider.go | A semicolon-compound import line invents an external symbol from the alias spelling.\n01M1N4B7ETE63SZEYK5Q4CNMCM | medium | open | current | internal/sem/langcompat.go | Treating @objc(RenamedThing) as ordinary exposure preserves candidates under their Swift name, but Objective-C can only name the declaration as RenamedThing.\n01M1N4B7EFFW45XVZ0KEV45DKH | medium | open | current | internal/sem/langcompat.go | Objective-C++ can import a module’s generated <Module>-Swift.h and name Objective-C-visible Swift declarations, but this compatibility row omits Swift.\n01M1N3J7VMGRWNCA9A43TY27G9 | medium | resolved | stale | internal/sem/provider.go | Comment stripping only occurs when a multiline parenthesized import is successfully joined.\n01M1N2R4SJXF1RT2894NJ1KMBP | medium | resolved | stale | internal/sem/provider.go | The alias-import fallback is still missing from the async and data-flow scans.\n01M1N1JFRE22NG66C1RZ308DYG | medium | resolved | stale | internal/sem/provider.go | The raw-import exception added for CALLS is not applied here, so DATA_FLOWS through explicitly imported cross-language functions disappear.\n01M1N1JFRAYTRNVRKKJTTF5Y29 | medium | resolved | stale | internal/sem/provider.go | This filters candidates before import resolution, so an explicit cross-language import no longer produces ASYNC_CALLS.\n01M1N0Z1XYRTKK663DD0GMG5B9 | medium | resolved | stale | internal/sem/python_scopes.go | A walrus target is recorded at its own offset, so compute := compute() is wrongly shadowed.\n01M1MZJZ66PJEYJVZ2F12M0PGN | medium | resolved | stale | internal/sem/python_scopes.go | The function-wide binding pass does not recognize structural-pattern capture bindings.\n01M1MZJZ5S0AYASS60YXBV7FHS | medium | resolved | stale | internal/sem/python_scopes.go | The target is recorded before the assignment's RHS is walked.\n01M1MY3BG7J9MNECYT5JSSJVBW | medium | resolved | stale | internal/sem/python_scopes.go | This parser requires literal spaces around import, although valid Python permits other whitespace.\n01M1MY3BFV4HAB9RWB2V9TYKES | medium | resolved | stale | internal/sem/python_scopes.go | Lambda default expressions are evaluated in the enclosing scope, but this branch walks only the lambda body.\n01M1MXK8DQ2843W4X79QE33XTR | medium | dismissed | stale | internal/sem/langcompat.go | Objective-C++ can import and name Swift declarations through the same generated <Module>-Swift.h mechanism as Objective-C.\n01M1MXK8DC1PJMCJZP9087CAXJ | medium | resolved | stale | internal/sem/langcompat.go | This exception only admits C consumers, so Objective-C .m files lose edges to declarations in dual-use headers classified as C++.\n01M1MVSC9V7XMSHTJAG8J4AZBR | medium | resolved | stale | internal/sem/python_scopes.go | This binds the new function name before walking its defaults/annotations, although Python does not replace the enclosing binding until the def finishes.\n01M1MTF2KX02MFKNZCMCQE58W4 | medium | dismissed | stale | internal/sem/python_scopes.go | All non-for_in_clause children, including intermediate if clauses, are deferred until every generator target has been bound.\n01M1MTF2KMA1TBWY5EAVAEN498 | medium | resolved | stale | internal/sem/provider.go | The new alias metadata is still derived line-by-line, so a multiline from mod import never records that c names compute.\n01M1MTF2K36THH2HM02FG6X6CJ | medium | resolved | stale | internal/sem/python_scopes.go | Returning after walking only the handler body skips calls in the exception expression.\n01M1MRQH8GZ9H234S1GJ57WR4W | medium | resolved | stale | internal/sem/parser.go | A dual-use C header guarded with extern \"C\" is labelled C++ and becomes unreachable from C.\n01M1MR2JHKJ3ZCJM0YRT12D9HH | medium | resolved | stale | internal/sem/langcompat.go | C sources can include plain-C declarations from a .h file classified as Objective-C.\n01M1MQ4YSXR5NAFQF9BBPB13SW | medium | resolved | stale | internal/sem/provider.go | Alias metadata is aggregated file-wide even though import visibility is computed per callable.\n01M1MPH09JDS5B66H57MCCJD6P | medium | resolved | stale | internal/sem/python_scopes.go | Function decorators and default-argument expressions are executed in the enclosing scope, but this branch walks only the function body.\n01M1MPH0917Z10TM68JQNQXGZA | medium | resolved | stale | internal/sem/python_scopes.go | Parenthesized imports such as from mod import (compute,) are parsed as a binding named (compute.\n01M1MNET96G3HXTAYD9HF2HYW9 | medium | resolved | stale | internal/sem/python_bindings.go | This pattern captures only the first target of chained assignments.\n01M1MNA5RYR5E6R149RPVD851G | low | resolved | stale | internal/sem/provider_test.go | The merged test file retains callRelationsFrom and importedReceiverCallsFrom with identical implementations.\n01M1MKP1DQXZ228CHCAC1N1DZF | medium | resolved | stale | internal/sem/provider.go | When Python scope analysis is incomplete, the integration replaces the established import map with an empty scope-derived map.\n01M1MKFVKMTW0DWT97R8J4B74S | medium | resolved | stale | internal/sem/python_bindings.go | An aliased local import is treated as a shadowing local binding.\n01M1MK64XS8BC7NDY9RPG6RDE7 | medium | resolved | stale | internal/sem/provider.go | The Python scope pass assigns comprehension walrus targets to the comprehension child and does not predeclare walrus locals in lambdas.\n01M1MK5B2W3APZRG0DS9WDBH8Q | medium | resolved | stale | internal/sem/provider.go | Python method scopes currently inherit the class-body scope, but method functions do not lexically close over class locals.\n01M1MK5AX7ZHNSP4JYTBTFD7RY | medium | resolved | stale | internal/sem/provider.go | Imports declared global or nonlocal inside a nested Python function are written into the parent or module scope.\n01M1MJAXK5A0DGHDCR895KGDBC | medium | resolved | stale | internal/sem/provider.go | The Python scope guard treats trees with parse errors or walk-depth truncation as authoritative.\n01M1MJAXFBB8WJANMH8TEMEKHW | medium | dismissed | stale | internal/sem/provider.go | The new Python scope state is time-insensitive: it stores only the final binding for each name and no call position.\n01M1MJ96DEJBT30FASZTT2EPZB | medium | resolved | stale | internal/sem/langcompat.go | Treating all Objective-C-labelled candidates as visible to C++ lets ordinary .cpp code resolve against Objective-C classes and methods.\n01M1MJ96D5VMGXBB385SR3XKZM | medium | resolved | stale | internal/sem/langcompat.go | Allowing every Objective-C reference to bind every Swift declaration creates impossible edges.\n01M1MHGQ9P6B4GT05YESAWC43D | medium | dismissed | stale | internal/sem/provider.go | The new Python scope collector misclassifies several AST constructs.\n01M1MHGQ5YH3Y9ZR0BMD3YJMNM | medium | resolved | stale | internal/sem/provider.go | Bare FFI resolution still uses a file-wide import-name map.\n01M1MH2RC4RKP60Q0928MQWJQS | medium | resolved | stale | internal/sem/python_bindings.go | Lambda parameters are added to the enclosing function's binding set.\n01M1MFX6S3Q5NXAF0VM8MHDSPN | medium | resolved | stale | internal/sem/provider.go | The raw FFI import fallback still ignores non-parameter local rebinding.\n01M1MFNVNA3VH404VX5W3CP5GM | medium | resolved | stale | internal/sem/provider.go | The unique raw import fallback ignores lexical shadowing.\n01M1MFJXQFGMJZ5PA8CZ5D64TV | medium | dismissed | stale | internal/sem/provider.go | The raw-import exception is wired only into per-symbol call collection.\n01M1MF7C3B4J9J678Z1RYSY1TY | medium | resolved | stale | internal/sem/provider.go | Filtering candidates before resolveCallTargets discards valid explicit cross-language imports.\n01M1MF309YCNNS9S6ZDK6B7J13 | low | resolved | stale | — | The languagesShareTypes comment says different languages are compatible only when they share a typeSharingLanguageGroups entry.\n01M1MEB7DQBWBTXNH4FYC7B98N | medium | resolved | stale | internal/sem/java.go | Java constructed-receiver resolution still consults the unfiltered global short-name index.\n01M1MEAX89RF52PDRKXDTYYRCC | medium | resolved | stale | internal/sem/provider.go | Chained constructor and method-return resolution uses raw global short-name candidates.\n01M1MEAWXZMS41YAB4YZA3QZ74 | medium | resolved | stale | internal/sem/provider.go | Go qualified receiver resolution still selects from all-language symbolsByShortName entries.\n01M1ME2AF9W0X4WFA6MD4JEJYC | medium | resolved | stale | internal/sem/provider.go | Filtering candidates before resolveImportedCallTargets drops valid bare imported FFI calls.\n01M1JFZTB8XK2QCP67FRQG8WDX | medium | resolved | stale | internal/sem/provider.go | Using the type-sharing predicate here drops valid import-resolved calls across FFI boundaries.\n01M1J83902SVJ2NQT4ZYC5PQD3 | medium | resolved | stale | internal/sem/langcompat.go | Objective-C can directly name @objc Swift classes and methods through the generated Module-Swift.h header.\n01M1J6FZ0J0CXPVZ662ZY35YEC | medium | resolved | stale | internal/sem/langcompat.go | Allow C++ to consume Objective-C-labelled declarations.\n01M1HZK6P1Z9EWBJK1ZN9A156H | medium | resolved | stale | internal/sem/langcompat.go | Swift supports direct C++ interoperability and can import C++ types and functions.\n01M1HZK6NNTTMZS68P6YBVGFBW | medium | resolved | stale | internal/sem/langcompat.go | This rejects every .cljc reference to a declaration in .cljs.\n01M19M7CBDBXKT4G1FYVBH0WK9 | medium | resolved | stale | internal/sem/langcompat.go | This group is made symmetric, but the interoperability is directional.\n01M19M6D39B1W1N681TF104WQP | medium | resolved | stale | internal/sem/langcompat.go | This filter drops legitimate calls from Zsh into sourced .sh libraries.\n01M17JEVQRXM7Q12WFQZ19MG86 | medium | resolved | stale | internal/sem/langcompat.go | This group cannot distinguish shared .cljc declarations from JVM-only .clj declarations.\n01M17JEVQDVMWY09ZTWH3FFSXH | medium | resolved | stale | internal/sem/provider.go | Filtering TESTS candidates by type-sharing language drops legitimate cross-language tests.\n01M17H63JDYXN24MBGXCV8C7ND | medium | resolved | stale | internal/sem/langcompat.go | This symmetric group marks C as able to name C++, Objective-C, and Swift declarations.\n```\n\nPR #184:\n\n- Head: `e181f5f78f84ced29f74300dc2bba1cbad175b05`\n- State: `OPEN`\n- Mergeable: `MERGEABLE`\n- Merge state: `BLOCKED`\n- Approvals: none\n- Entire Gates: `IN_PROGRESS`\n- Passing: lint, all builds, Ubuntu test, Windows compile/plan/vet, BM25 adapter, both statusline checks.\n- In progress: macOS test, Windows non-heavy package test, Windows shards 0–7.\n- PR: https://github.com/entireio/entire-graph/pull/184\n\nEntire status: enabled; active Codex session uses `gpt-5.6-sol`; checkpoint sync is configured to `origin`. No Trail or PR mutations were performed."}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a0690a-a093-7352-9a6c-ed6ea1d6d3c0","create_time":1788491200.794676}}}
