{"timestamp":"2026-09-04T03:45:39.892Z","ordinal":27458,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"agent_message","id":"amsg_01a06a85-aa33-7181-bf14-006f89094585","author":"/root/pr188_final_terra","recipient":"/root","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Message Type: FINAL_ANSWER\nTask name: /root\nSender: /root/pr188_final_terra\nPayload:\nFindings:\n\n- P1: `swiftAttributeArgumentsEnd` still mishandles escaped normal strings. It closes at `\\\"`, so a `)` later in that string can end a preceding attribute early and hide a following renamed `@objc`. `swiftc -parse` accepts:\n  `@MyAttr(message: \"a \\\" ) still string\") @objc(RenamedThing) class Renamed: NSObject {}`\n- P2: comments between attributes (for example `@MyAttr /* comment */ @objc(...)`) currently yield a conservative false negative. Acceptable only if documented as intentional.\n\nCost recommendation: prefer a 60–80 LOC fail-closed leading-prefix parser. Preserve plain `@objc`/`@objcMembers`, same-name custom selectors/classes, no-arg qualified actor attributes, and ordinary `@available(...)`; return an explicit `uncertain` result for malformed/unknown parameterized/raw/comment-heavy prefixes and make visibility false rather than falling through to class inheritance. This prevents false cross-language edges and is materially cheaper than the current ~179-line generic lexer.\n\nDirect Python import terminator implementation is clean. Focused Swift/direct-import tests and `^TestPython` pass; diff and formatting checks pass."}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a0690a-a093-7352-9a6c-ed6ea1d6d3c0","create_time":1788493539.89191}}}
{"timestamp":"2026-09-04T03:45:42.024Z","ordinal":27459,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a0616d-0c18-7443-82f2-b4c5bc498542","turn_id":"01a0690a-a093-7352-9a6c-ed6ea1d6d3c0","item":{"type":"Reasoning","id":"rs_064395e280df5569016a9a3ee5838887d094fe00fa8d1c302c","summary_text":[],"raw_content":[]},"started_at_ms":1788493541485,"completed_at_ms":1788493542024}}
