{"timestamp":"2026-09-04T04:00:27.663Z","ordinal":27606,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"agent_message","id":"amsg_01a06a93-360f-7593-ac78-1911768c6d86","author":"/root/pr188_final_terra","recipient":"/root","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Message Type: FINAL_ANSWER\nTask name: /root\nSender: /root/pr188_final_terra\nPayload:\nP1: non-ASCII leading attribute names bypass fail-closed handling.\n\nAt [langcompat.go](/Users/thomi/Projects/entire-graph/.entire/worktrees/pr184-review/internal/sem/langcompat.go:406), ASCII scanning can leave an empty attribute name and unchanged offset. The next pass returns `NoExposure`, so an inheriting class falls through to the class-inheritance branch and is exposed despite a later renamed `@objc`.\n\n`swiftc -parse` accepts both:\n\n```swift\n@属性 @objc(RenamedThing) class Renamed: NSObject {}\n@Módulo.Actor @objc(RenamedThing) class Renamed: NSObject {}\n```\n\nSmall fix: if no attribute-name byte was consumed, return `swiftObjCUncertain`; add the first case expecting false.\n\nOtherwise the simplified design is proportionate and direct-import handling is clean. Focused Swift/ObjC/FFI matrix, `^TestPython`, diff, and gofmt checks pass."}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a0690a-a093-7352-9a6c-ed6ea1d6d3c0","create_time":1788494427.663141}}}
