{"timestamp":"2026-09-04T04:10:30.844Z","ordinal":27743,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"agent_message","id":"amsg_01a06a9c-6a3c-7913-ba45-ef8dc2a8e9d0","author":"/root/pr188_final_terra","recipient":"/root","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Message Type: FINAL_ANSWER\nTask name: /root\nSender: /root/pr188_final_terra\nPayload:\nThe local async/data-flow matrix is present and was never removed: `TestPythonScopedImportBindingExtendsAsyncAndDataFlow` at `call_resolution_precision_test.go:1111`, introduced by `35ee4247`; HEAD and the worktree retain it.\n\n| Regression case | Local | `bf88d09f` |\n|---|---|---|\n| Aliased `ASYNC_CALLS` resolves C target | Yes — with a competing Python `run` symbol | Yes |\n| Aliased `DATA_FLOWS` resolves C target | Yes — caller→callee direction | Yes — both directions |\n| Unaliased async/flow positive | Yes | Oracle only |\n| Same-language import beats C | Yes | No |\n| Late local rebinding suppresses async/flow | Yes | No |\n| Class shadow suppresses async/flow | Yes | No |\n| Function-local import scope isolation | Yes, unaliased | Yes, alias-specific |\n| C/Ruby ambiguity suppresses async/flow | Yes, unaliased | Yes, alias-specific |\n| Bare module alias emits no async/flow | No | Yes |\n| Alias async/flow metadata equals unaliased oracle | No | Yes |\n| Alias flow reverse direction exists | No | Yes |\n\nAssertion strength differs:\n\n- Local checks exact type, caller suffix, target suffix, and cardinality; it does not assert resolution/scope/confidence/reason and only observes forward `DATA_FLOWS`.\n- Remote’s `relationsTouching` adds no assertion by itself, but lets the remote test compare both directions and metadata (`Resolution`, `RelationScope`, `Confidence`, `Reason`) against an unaliased oracle.\n\nSo the remote async test is not wholly needed, but it has four real test gaps: alias-specific ambiguity, module-alias suppression, alias-specific no-leak, and metadata/reverse-flow parity. The cheapest port is to extend the existing local table for the first three and add one compact parity assertion for both flow directions—not the full remote test.\n\nThe remote multiline/CALLS cases remain mostly redundant."}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a0690a-a093-7352-9a6c-ed6ea1d6d3c0","create_time":1788495030.844317}}}
